    15	He was nineteen, and he had no Path. Everybody else whom he knew had been given one at fourteen, at a Kindling, and had been ranked by it ever since; his own Arbiter had woken on its brass plate that day, and taken eleven seconds over him, and gone dark. What answered him instead were fragments. There were ten of them now, pieces of other people's Paths, each one taken in the middle of a fight that somebody had meant, and each one written down in an old man's book with a name beside it and a price.
    16	
    17	The prices were what he knew best, since he had carried all ten down the roads of this country for a hundred and thirty days and used them as seldom as he could. He could have said to a grain what each fragment charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
