   249	They wove the hurdle that evening on the shed's floor, by the lantern, which hung from its nail by the chain. Cael had driven nine hazel rods into the beaten earth of the horse-walk in a row, a hand apart, to be its bones. Lira sat on her heels at one end and he at the other, and they passed the reeds in and out between the rods, turn about, each of them beating a finished course down with the edge of a hand before the next was begun.
