   261	The ferryman grunted, and bent to his sheave. Behind him the dusk came down on the landing, and with it the quietest of the ten lifted in Cael to the height of his knees, as it did at every dusk, and was held there while he went on weaving.
   262	
   263	It was two-handed work, and she had known that when she sat down to it. A reed had to be bent round the end rod and held hard while the next was brought through, or the whole course sprang. She held with the left. He saw her set that hand on the first reed as she would have set it on something hot; the fingers closed, slowly, each a little after the one before, and the arm above them, which was still no thicker than a rail, began to shake.
