     1	# Chapter 10 — There Is No Key
     2	
     3	Seln had the door at noon, and he had taken it without being asked, as he took any door that mattered.
     4	
     5	He stood just within the post, on the hinge side, where a person coming in out of the light would be three steps into the room before he saw that anybody was there. The long board lay beyond him, by the hearth. Brom had six bowls on it and the ladle in the pot, and the stool stood at its end with nobody on it. Karis sat with her notebook shut under her hand. Oryn was on her feet beside the stool, and Lira on hers at the hearthstone, with her back to the fire and her arms folded, the right over the left.
     6	
     7	Cael's eyes had given him back the far end of the room by then. He could see the keeper's casks, and the cloth on its nail, with the black still wet on it. The board before him was coming back more slowly, and the faces along it had as yet no more edge than faces seen through steam.
     8	
     9	The smith's hammer stopped, up the bank, as it stopped every noon. A stick sounded on the flags of the yard.
    10	
    11	Vastin did not come in. He stood on the sill with the day behind him, as he had stood on a landing in Ostrand, and his head went round that room in its order: the hearth and the casks, the one small window, the board; and then six people, one by one, with the same count given to each. If it stayed on the man beside the door-post no longer than on the rest, it stayed no shorter either.
    12	
    13	In all the dealings that those two had ever had, neither had said a word to the other. One had opened a door in a borrowed room and shut it again. One had read forty copied orders at a table, knowing from the doorway what sort of man had copied them, and had ridden home taking care not to wonder at his name.
    14	
    15	"You came through the board," Seln said.
    16	
    17	"I came through the board."
    18	
    19	"Then you already know it doesn't care who you were."
    20	
    21	"I'm learning," Vastin said.
    22	
    23	Seln moved his shoulder from the post by the width of a hand. From him that was a door held wide.
    24	
    25	Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been set down as nothing yet. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
    26	
    27	Vastin came in. He made his bow, the short stiff one, to the room and to nobody in it; and then he stood at the board's foot with his stick, and Cael understood that a man who had been shown to a chair in every room of his life did not know which one was his.
    28	
    29	"Is that place taken?"
    30	
    31	He was looking at the stool. It had a bowl before it, and steam going up from the bowl, and nobody near it but a woman who was plainly not sitting down.
    32	
    33	"It's the mule's," said Brom.
    34	
    35	He said it with perfect gravity, and nobody at the board laughed. Vastin considered the stool, and the bowl, and the healer; he gave the matter the attention that he would have given a point of procedure which he had not met before.
    36	
    37	"Then I shall not presume upon it."
    38	
