./editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:4915:books/book-08-before-the-paths/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:55:- [x] **Book 11 plants (the Architect's will is active and hunting Cael — earliest plant Book 8, CANON_RULES minimum THREE, and Book 8 is where they ORIGINATE — three present, each deniable, none disclosing that the will is active):** *(1)* **"Did not die in the conventional sense" (Ch15):** a damaged *secondary* source in the Deepstacks says the Architect did not die in the ordinary way. The language is obscure — antique, hedged, possibly metaphorical. Karis marks the passage and does not theorize it; the book offers no gloss. (Plants the persistence of the will. In-world it reads as the kind of reverent ambiguity old chronicles use for founders.) *(2)* **The six-year process (Ch3, Ch5):** buried in the suppressed Quieting records is a sub-layer log entry showing that *something initiated in the Arbiter deep layer roughly six years ago* and has been running as a slow, uninterpretable sequence ever since — a counter no Compact technician can read or halt — correlated in the file with a classification event the record redacts and no official will name. (The reader can date it: six years ago Cael Kindled [SHATTERED] and the Arbiter system threw the flag "nobody in the Compact knows how to interpret" — Book 1. In-world the Compact reads it as a decades-known deep-layer malfunction. It is the will's slow-activation, seen from outside, uninterpreted.) *(3)* **The anomalies clustering on Cael (Ch1, Ch5, Ch14, Ch22):** small instrument and record errors that gather around Cael specifically and *increase* across the book — the recovered survey kit's instruments return clean readings for everyone and errors or nulls in his hands (Ch1, Ch5); a Treswick Arbiter station's residue does something faint and wrong as he passes it that no one else triggers (Ch14); tallies and bearings taken near him at the sites drift in ways Karis re-checks and cannot source (Ch22). Every instance is attributed in-world to his unreadable architecture — "the still place" — and is individually deniable. Cumulatively, and only in Book 11's hindsight, it is the will beginning to find him. NOTHING here says so.
