editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/BOOK_MAP.md:112:seam ("two months" after the road home) — is **one academic year**: [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: OWNER #19's restructure keeps its SHAPE (one academic year; served on the eighth day; the cache and Vastin before the midyear recess; the ruling in the year's last month; the Line crossed after the year's close) and carries NO season or month names (OWNER #42, coordinator default); B5 is season-blind (#35, #36.4, #37 O1), and the bible's and Book 7's season words are cited here by reference only. Owner may restore seasons when the series calendar is ruled; audit A1]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/BOOK_MAP.md:138:[B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: OWNER-pending #42 (extends #35; audit B8). The birthday is a fixed date — "the eleventh of Sowing" by default (the owner may move it, e.g. to "the third of Reaping") — with no cross-year day count and no month-of-year ordinal stated. The edition's own counts put the B5→B6 interval near 205 days, so the series calendar must later decide whether the academic year, the recess or the birthday moves; this plan's earlier "about the year's third month" and Book 7's day-102 arithmetic are self-consistent only against the source's calendar, which the edition has not adopted (#35, #36.4). Default: season-blind until the owner rules]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/BOOK_MAP.md:688:   continuing until the year's close. [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: season words struck: #19's SHAPE stands, with no season or month names (OWNER #42 vs #35/#37 O1; default season-blind until the owner rules the series calendar); the birthday is the fixed date of §2, OWNER-pending #42; audit A1, B8]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:21:- **When/where:** the first day of Halcenvane's qualifying year, after a recess of twelve weeks and four days [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: no season word anywhere in this book (OWNER #35; B4 names no season for the recess or the new month); "the recess", weather words only. Halcenvane's weekdays are the office's First-day … Seventh-day, never English names]. The bluff above Ostrand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:24:- **Seln:** at his desk through the whole recess (his name against every working day of the wing's recess counter sheet, B4 ch62). He has functionally switched sides and files null reports; his quarterly closed "Continuation of embedded coverage recommended." One unsigned slip of wing paper has passed between him and Cael, once, inside a returned coursework folder (B4 ch53), learned and burned on the common-room coals the same night with Karis watching and asking nothing; nothing has been said of it by anyone [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: B4 establishes ONE slip, not a running "stove protocol"; whether a second ever comes is this book's to decide (M8); if it does, the stove is the same unspoken procedure]. The locked case stays unopened. Karis moved her notebooks out of the archive sweep after he warned her in the corridor (the twenty-seventh of Reaping), and the circle took it in silence: *Gratitude is the leak.* "Thank you for the form" every week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:46:- **The watchers** return by the twelfth day of the road. Book 6 opens with older, senior men.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:235:- **The road and the tournament.** The road is seventeen days, with the melt under the wheels, and arrival on the eighteenth. The opening is on the first day of a new month (unnamed), about a week after arrival (Cael "had waited a week" for first sight).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:27:At Greyvane, on the last day of a week, after the fifth bell, they had come to her on a sheet laid square on the corner of a long table, in answer to the plainest question her form allowed: who had entered this mark, and on whose authority? She had read the answer twice. Then she had written a referral, pinned the return to it, marked it an irregularity of administration, and sent it up on the first courier of the next week, to the office above the office that had answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:113:[[TIER-B: the technician's sheet — the Compact date that stands after "Sequence initiated" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:61:*To the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy, chartered. The Guilds Compact gives notice that a delegation of the Compact will conduct a formal charter inspection of this institution, commencing on the twelfth day of Reaping, for such time as the delegation requires. Scope: charter compliance; enrollment-basis review; observation of provision-status evaluations.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:She had been Auremont's since twelve, a year before the garden. The house had seen something in a set of entrance papers and sent for her, and kept her near, and waited for the rest. Afterwards nothing in her life had been left to happen by itself. Floors were relaid to her measure. An evaluator who wanted a look at her had his station opened before the day began, and came, and looked. Whole classes were assembled round her and broken up again when she grew past them. Other people spoke of climbing; she had never once had to look for the next rung. It was always there, put down where her foot would go, by people who were very good at their work. At Auremont the day of the garden court was a half-holiday now, with a demonstration in the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:247:Cael could guess how a sitting had ended for him, on every day of forty years: with a leaf to be signed, or a seal to be set, and a clerk at the door to gather what was to go down a stair; and then with the next thing, drawn toward him across a desk. There had always been a next thing. Cael had been the next thing himself, once, on a bluff, for three days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:23:Along the row to her left, Havel was writing something small in the margin of his attendance sheet. Beyond him the other seats were emptying for the midday interval. At the end of the row the twelfth chair stood empty behind the grey rail-cloth, as it had stood every day of the sitting, with its pale square and its two empty screw holes. Ilsev did not look at it. She had a referral of her own still pending somewhere above her desk, the second she had ever filed on a single matter, and it had taught her a great deal about the difference between what a record said and what a record was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:It was the last day of the academic year. Cael had known it when he woke, as one knows a date that has been set against one's name for a long time. Somewhere behind him, six switchbacks up from a river, a bell would ring that evening and the house would close its books; and at some hour between, on a north road short of a toll-bar, a stout fair man would unlock a windowless building. He did not know the hour, for Withrow's sheet had given only the day, and he kept looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:79:His supervised hour on the third day of the second week was the one he would remember, though nothing much happened in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:111:The Stone Path page was the broad student's. Cael had watched him three times now, on Brom's floor, across a chalk ring at first bell, and once more on the fifth day of the second week, drilling alone in a corner of the defensive hall while Wray worked with someone else. Alone, with no one to be furniture for, the boy moved differently — slower than Cael had expected, and lower, his feet barely seeming to leave the floor, though every shift of his weight went down through his heels and came back up a fraction stronger, as if the ground were lending him something each time he touched it. Watching, Cael realized that the boy had been solving the same problem Brom solved, how to take a strike and survive it, from the opposite direction entirely. Brom gave and borrowed. This boy refused to be moved at all, and let the ground do the arguing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:135:Lira's standings bout fell on the sixth day of the second week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:23:"The ruler is brass. It is the two rings under it that I answer for, and I set one where a carter bent his road and the other where a broker's paper said a day and a little aside." She laid a finger beside the third. "This one I drew round a place where he had lifted his hand. He told me, on the evening that he gave me the skin, that he had seen that corner once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and had never had a wheel within a day of it. So I have three things on this lid, each of them a little wrong, and I cannot say by how much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:The instrument woman said so. A woman with an oil can, who came on the posted day of every month and initialled a yellowed card by Gault's door that nobody had ever told her to keep. And this morning she had stood outside her own shut store, not on her posted day, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:3:The standings were read on the main floor at dusk, as they were read at the end of every day of the sitting, and on the ninth day there was a plaque to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:Quenna found him at the pump on the Thursday of week twenty-one, at the end of the afternoon, when the yard was emptying and the light had gone long and yellow across the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:Naveth's last notice went up on the board in the main hall on the Monday of week twenty-two, below the posting for the end-of-term sittings and above a sheet about the kitchen rota.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:He went back to the table on the sill. Its first renewal's row gave one day, the day of the trestle, which was the day the house's own enrollment book carried in Bracken's small upright hand, because that was the day the house had seen it happen. The registry's file, wherever it sat, would carry the other day, the day its own copy was sealed. The house had never kept a copy of that one. It had gone back up the road the same afternoon. The only place on the continent where both days stood side by side was a book he had filled himself and shut in a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:And on the way down the stair he remembered the counter, and the four sentences Seln had spent on him in a queue on the third day of the year. *Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check. Keep the documentation current.* The renewals table was the provision's standing papers, set out in a grid. Whatever went down the hill this week would sit in the registry's file beside the registry's own dates when the half-year check came, and a table that disagreed with them by nine days would have been the first thing the check found. Karis had not heard Seln say it. She had kept the documentation current anyway, because that was what she did with documents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:341:"*Air cleared,*" said Karis. "He asked me what we'd done, and I told him, and he's written it down as though a person cleared air every day of the week." She had counted the four in. "Fifty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:319:"Sleep. Tomorrow's the seventh. You get one more day of being sure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:119:The timekeepers' tower stood at the east end of the Concourse, across the outer court from the west tower where the adjudication office lived. Cael had passed under it every day of the sitting and never been inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:41:On the last full day of term Cael took the week's coursework down to the assessment wing for the last time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:114:*Rent: from waking, spent or not. Drifts come in the gaps, never in the work. A whole chair-back of Lira's thumbnail marks on one bad night; two on the day of the sitting; fewer every week since. The falling count is the one line on this page that cheers me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:185:"Fight him," said Lira, on the Tuesday of week eighteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:So: paper. Not product, nothing that would ever go down the river, but his own, in his own keeping, dated, so that on the worst day of his working life he could put his hand on a page and say *this is what I saw, and this is the night I saw it.* If that day never came, he was out a few sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:23:He headed the page with the Third-day of the pin, ten days late, and set the afternoon down whole. He had never needed notes for what he had seen; the service valued that in him above his Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:17:She said it the way she had said *Early* on the waystation clay, the one word she had found for a thing after a whole day of failing to say it in a hundred. Then she stepped back from the cap and folded her arms, and her left arm went in close to her side the way it always did now, from habit, and she did not look at him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:127:*You are required to attend a senior evaluation, convened under the District Handbook, Part Six, at the post, Ardenmere, on the Thursday of this week, at the second hour after noon.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:"And here, a long way south-east, among his water. Strokes all round, and a round of bare skin in the midst, with no road within a day of it." She had ringed both. "Three blank circles. One I can source, since a one-armed man has named it. The other two are a man lifting his hand from a skin. I have written *guess* beside each, and I shall not call them anything else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:103:Then, because it was the seventh day and the shoulder had had its day of rest, they did the redirect.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:217:"The seat reserves," said the hood. "It will give its ruling upon the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the hour after noon. From the First-day of that week the parties will keep within the bell, at the seat's call and the evaluation seat's. So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:73:"Dated the day of your arrival."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:203:Hesk's answer came up on the evening coach of the Third-day, the fourth day of the silence, and it was four lines long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:261:"Since the second day of drafting. You went quiet at supper that night, when Karis came in with her hair down." She stood and brushed off her coat. "Come and eat your own bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:471:Nobody moved at once. Lira stood in front of the Iron leaf with her hands in her pockets and read her own name at the top of it, and the figure beside it, and the word under it that the clerk had chalked in capitals: *Title.* She read it the way she had read the milestone on the ninth day of the road, all at once and without stopping. Then she did not look away from it. She looked at it for a good while, and Cael, standing behind her with his arm in a scarf, understood that this was the difference, and did not say anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:He had done this for two years so that a house on a bluff might keep its charter, and he had hated every Fifth-day of it. He was doing it now so that a mule, and a man with twenty-two years on a road, and four people he would not have traded for a tier, should come to a fence. It was the same act, and it felt entirely different.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:He had gone out at noon on the first full day of their stay, with the witnessed slip in his hat and nothing in the cart, sitting on the tailboard as he had not once sat while the grain was on it. An empty cart behind a rested mule, it seemed, could do in one day what a loaded one did in two. He had brought salt up with him, and a keg of nails, and a bolt of blue cloth that three women came out of the house to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:265:Hall three had frightened him to the bone once before, on the day of the pin. The next afternoon it did it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:157:The review sat on the Friday of the week after, in the small room on the upper floor of the guild hall by the canal, with the shutters half closed against the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:"One." Karis looked at him. "It opens on one day, the day of the ruling, and stays open until the revocation takes effect, and then it shuts. And it only opens at all if we lose. If the seat rules for the house, there's no door, because there's nothing to resign from." She sat back. "Until that day there's only the corridor: every sitting attended, every form filed on time, every bout in wing three at the band, and every first-year at that counter handing in a certificate, and you standing behind her in the queue with your face doing nothing." She was quiet a moment. "Perfectly, until the ruling. If we put one foot wrong before it, the door we'd use afterward turns into the word on four files."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:185:Cael sat with his back against the wall, with Hesk's pen in his breast pocket and Karis's chart folded inside his coat, and watched the good lamp turn the long scarred table into a place where people lived. Somebody who was not Hesk, in a house nowhere near Fen Street, had kept his day for him, and until tonight he had not known that such a thing could happen. He had turned sixteen at some hour before dawn, and gone through a whole day of lectures and floor sheets without its once seeming to matter. Then three people had decided that it mattered, and gone to some trouble about it. There was no column in any book he kept for what that was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:There were eleven names on the manifest and he had pages on nine of them. The library had given him three of the four escort riders out of a dozen years of attendance lists, the counsel out of her published findings, and the three clerks out of a registry yearbook that nobody had borrowed in a decade. Havel had a page with a question of Lira's at the foot of it. Ilsev had the thickest page and the least use, because six findings told him what she would do with a document and nothing at all about what she did with a morning. The fourth rider was too new to be anywhere. And the eleventh name had one line on its page, and under the line, in his own hand, a single word: *Lira*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:Vastin had heard her through walls for four nights of inns, and he no longer heard the words. He heard the shape. It went on low and level, about the water in the jug and the state of a collar and something a magistrate had once said to her on a stair. Then it stopped in the middle, and there was a silence about as long as a page, and then it went on again from the exact syllable where it had broken off. She had been handed something to read. He had noticed the habit on the second day of the road and had not written it down, because he did not take notes on colleagues. He only noticed that he had noticed, and let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:They ran a session every day of the tenth week, and on three of the days they ran two. The slate on the nail said *K. — declaration practice* so often that a Current Path first-year, who wanted the room for a declaration of his own, began to take it personally, and Karis gave him the evening hour without a word and moved them to the morning. She did not think it a sacrifice. She thought it a variable, by the rule she had set herself on the first night: no two sessions the same. A failure repeated under the same conditions only told you that you had repeated it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:191:They ran the thirteenth and fourteenth on the last day of the tenth week, two short ones, a quarter-hour each, one at dawn and one before supper, at five paces, because Karis was still cold at the fingertips from the long afternoon. Both were null.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:187:*Sit out four sessions running and you slip one anyway. Year's end: the best four in each column are seeded to the semifinals, which run in the second term's closing weeks. The final comes on the year's last day of competition. Faculty sit as the panel. The registrar keeps the record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:He had been sitting there a quarter of an hour, after the grey half, with two loaves from underneath and a twist of the fruit woman's apricots and nothing at all in his head, which was a thing he had been learning to do. The market was loud and slow around him. It was the first warm Saturday of the year, and people had come down to it who did not need anything, only to walk about in the sun and look at things and be seen looking. He had been watching them without meaning to. He had been watching the way a man in a brown coat came down the second row between the stalls, unhurried, reading the bread woman's high cracked lantern as if it were a notice, and he had thought, idly, *that man reads things the way they're written*. Then the man had turned out of the row toward the pie stall, and Cael had seen the set of his shoulders, and the brown coat had stopped mattering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:291:On the second day of it he wrote it down, sitting on the tailboard with the observation notebook on his knee while the wagon climbed a long grey hill at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:Last of the manifest came Assessor Ilsev, carrying her own case down the steps herself. Porters had offered to carry it every day of the visit and been refused every time, so politely that two of them had started offering just to hear how she would do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:73:"It isn't a day off the map. It's the map. Your column says whether the shelf takes a cart, and why, and those are part of why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:259:On the Monday of the seventeenth week, at first light, the frost was so thick on the training yard that their boots left prints in it like prints in snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:29:He watched the boards the way he watched a sitting, at an even pace, giving each part its share, and Ilsev did not try to read him. She had stopped trying on the second day of the road. A colleague's face was not her question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:139:Norhold on its day off was another place altogether. He had known the tournament's Norhold for two weeks, the Norhold of boards and criers and broadsides and the long roar out of the Concourse, and he had thought that was Norhold. It was not. It was a costume the city put on every three years. Underneath it, on the rest day, the real place went on with its own business at its own pace.
