editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:*The wing's counter: open every working day of the recess, with the same clerk's name against every day on the sheet. Nobody on this bluff has said a word about that sheet. Everybody I live with has read it to the bottom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:63:"I'm coming to them. You said four wheres. One's walked. One's this, with a paper nailed across it." She nodded at the sheets on Seln's knee. "The third's a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and nobody's put a price on it at all. So we go and pace that one, on our own time, with a healer at the edge. I'm not saying leave them alone. I'm saying I'll not walk into the one that somebody's paying us to walk into."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:The yard at first bell on Friday was colder than it had been on Thursday, and emptier, and the frost lay on the benches unbroken because nobody had sat on them. Five people stood at the rail on the open side. Cael was one, and Lira beside him with her bruised shoulder strapped under her coat, and Brom, and Karis a little along with her hands in her pockets and no notebook again. Hobb stood at the far corner, alone, as he had stood at every bout Cael had seen him watch. That was all. Somebody's re-certification review at the first bell on the last day of the week was not a thing anybody at Greyvane had a reason to come and see, and so nobody had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:245:"The last day of the eleventh week," she said. "The first bell. The same panel." She looked at Gerda over the desk, and something passed across her face that Cael, standing in the passage outside the door, could not read. "Filed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:27:*Evaluation: the Fourth-day following. The last day of the academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:155:The red book lay on Rooke's table, where it always lay. There was a word in it under her name; he had written it on the day of the Blade, and then a second, and she had never seen either. She stood over the book in the dark with her hand on its cover, and did not open it. It was his, and whatever he had written, he had written of the program's entrant. She would tell him tomorrow, after the bout, in one sentence, to his face, for she owed him that much and he would want no more. If he chose to show her the page then, he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:173:"They didn't keep me," said Ansel. "Not for that, they'd say. For other things, that winter. But it was that." He put the spoon down very carefully beside the bowl, square to the edge of the board. "I've not said his name aloud on this hill in two years. Nobody here knows I was ever up the river. The dock man knows because he was in the hall that day and saw it, and he's never once said so, and I've been grateful to him every day of my life." He looked at Brom. "And now you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:27:The first was the district's own impression book. It lived in the records room at the district seat, on a slanted desk of its own under the window, a book as tall as a child and as thick as a door-step. Every die issued for use in the district went into it on the day of issue, pressed into a red wafer and pasted on the page in the order it came, with the office's name and the date beside it in the keeper's hand. Havel turned its pages for two hours on the Third-day afternoon with the clerk in the wet hat hovering at his elbow, offering to help and being thanked. He found the seat's own seal on its page, forty years old, the river and the scale. He found the fee office's, and the bridge-ward's, and a ferry company's, and a hundred and eighty others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:The old man was not there. Cael had not thought he would be. The old man stood in his gate at the top of the lane of yards on every day of the year, and whatever happened in the district went past him or did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:151:High above the east rail the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk before her, and Havel beside her. At the far end, behind the grey rail-cloth, seat twelve stood empty as it had stood every day of the sitting, with its pale square and its two empty screw holes, and a steward at the head of its stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:105:"Then the wing receives it." He put out a hand, and took the card, and read both sides, and laid it in a tray. "The provision's hours were complete on the Fourth-day of the week before last. The card agrees. It is pleasant when they agree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:165:"I'll take your letter down tomorrow. I'm going anyway, and the post goes out on the third day of the week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:187:Brom's first full day of coursework was the next day, and he came back from it talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:145:The coast Blade stood on his chalk afterward and looked down at his own feet, like a man looking at a pair of friends who have let him down. On the coast house's bench their coach had risen. She was the lean woman who had ridden alongside the wagon on the third day of the road, and she was looking at Ephram now as she had looked at Cael then, a long, unhurried look, item by item.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:313:It came down the row at the last bell of every sitting, from Ilsev's hand to his, and from his along to the end. It was his office's sheet. He had ruled it himself on the coach coming north, a page for each day of the sitting, twelve lines to a page, and he knew every line of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:315:Except one. On the morning of the opening a steward had brought him the whole sheaf back from the west tower, and line twelve on every page had been filled in before he saw it. It was the same word in a hand he did not know, on every day of the three weeks, down to the last: *held*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:129:The founding stock ran along the back of the archive in nine narrow cases, against the old grey wall. Prynn had given it to her on the first day of the three weeks with a single sentence, *It's yours by day and mine by night, and the roof's in charge of both of us*. Karis had spent fourteen days reading as much of it as one person could, and she had got perhaps a third of the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:She had borrowed Brom's docket out of his left inside pocket to copy its date, and Brom had stood in the annex doorway until she gave it back, which she did inside a quarter of an hour. She had the two dates on a slip, one above the other. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The seat's ruling, before the year's close, and there was only one Fifth-day in the year's last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:The sittings went up on the covered walk's middle board that afternoon in Bracken's hand, under the house's one-line answer from the eighth day: *Sittings at the registry seat, Ostrand, on the seat's Fifth-days. First sitting: the sixth Fifth-day of the year.* Underneath it, smaller, Bracken had added a line of his own, which was a thing he never did on a public board. *The house will attend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:239:"*Review for advancement of tier. The panel convened under this office's docket of the fourth day is confirmed: three assessors and a sigil frame, at the district seat. The practitioner's record is received and entered as it stands, the title included. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year.*" He turned the sheet to the light. "And at the foot. *Outcome anticipated: advancement, per title.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:191:He did not turn round, but went on looking at the rain. Cael watched the back of his head and could not have said what the man knew. Rooke had eaten in the open seat at a refectory table on the day of the riders, and had struck three bouts off a card without asking how an ankle had come to be turned on the way to a sitting. He knew what a ruling could do to an enrollment, and he knew whose friend his entrant was. He had built her a hall. He had never once asked her for a promise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:At the end of the row was the twelfth chair. It had been held, unplated, for thirteen days before anybody sat in it, and had been empty again since the day of the duelist. There was a man in it now. He wore the plain grey of an inspecting officer and nothing on his collar. Seln said he had come in by the east stair an hour before the brackets began, alone, with no case and no clerk, and had sat down, and had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:169:On the Fourth-day of the tenth sitting's week he fought in wing three for the last time that year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:5:It had been run at the first bell on the Tuesday of the twenty-fourth week, in the training hall, closed. Nobody sat on the benches along the wall but the clerk and one first-year who had been sent in for a slate and forgotten to go out again. Quenna had the middle chair, and Wray the instructor's seat on her left. The rota had given the right-hand chair to the white-haired spare man from the movement floor, who had sat the first sitting and the fourth and had asked nothing at either. Hobb stood at the north mark with his feet a shoulder-width apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:The eighth day of the year was a First-day, hot and still, and at the second bell Cael was on the covered walk with a book under his arm and nowhere in particular to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:*Public adjudication. In the matter of the enrollment of the candidate on the unclassified observer track, on challenge filed by the Compact's field service. A magistrate of the Compact presiding. Assembly room, lecture wing. The first day of the twenty-second week, first bell. The record will be public.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:95:Not less than usual. Nothing. He had pushed against air every day of his life without knowing he was doing it, as a man does not know he is breathing. For one step it was not there to push against. The air had stood aside, the way Karis had said it did for her, the way a crowd stands back for a steward. He gave the step a stride's worth of effort and it carried him two. He went along the foot of the barrier, out of her road, through the place where the air had been, and came out at the far end of his own lane level with her lane's west flank, a lane's width away from it. Nothing he owned could have put him there from that corner. Nothing on any sheet of any Path on the continent said a fighter with no tier could arrive there at that speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:234:Cael looked up once more, past the officials, to the high row over the east rail. Every face in it was turned down toward the floor. Every face but one. The man in plain grey at the end of the row was standing, and going along behind the plated chairs toward the east stair, at the same unhurried pace he had used on the day of the duelist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:133:Cael had been told, more than once, that he would stand in a tunnel for a long time on the first day of the new month, and he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:85:He read it. It was exact and dry and held no theory, only measurements: how big his letters had been the day of the table, the day of the first session, and every day since the request. Each of the last three was a little smaller than the one before. At the foot she had written *Cause unknown. Probable.* He laughed out loud, which surprised him, and slid the notebook back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:218:"I made them," said Seln. "They are true." And then, because Karis had asked him to on the stair, "On the card for the Fourth-day of the seventh week the original carries a correction in the desk clerk's hand. I copied the corrected figure and noted both in the margin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:She unrolled the paper on the canvas and laid the brass along its top edge to hold it. It was a straightedge, an old one, which she had had from Ternhall. Cael knew the sheet before the firelight had found the whole of it, for it had hung on her reading-room wall on the first day of the year, and she had taken it down in the first week to make room for a code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:*Seventh-day of the recess's first week. The road column: none, none, the post, none. Four coats, senior, every bell. No third move. Seln's reading of the silence, unchanged: why spend a team when the seat will deliver him at the year's close.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:153:On the Tuesday of the twentieth week, the seventh day, Brom came to the archive door at the end of the fifth bell and stood there without coming in, because Prynn did not like people who came in only to fetch other people out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:On the tenth of Reaping, the morning after the file was closed, the woman who kept the wing's instruments came into the wing's outer office with her oil can and her brass rag at the third bell, as she did on the posted day of every month, and checked the instruments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:The final stayed on the nineteenth, and the evaluation stayed on the twentieth. Rooke's cohort worked its ordinary hours at its ordinary marks in the east hall, and the supervision sheets went across the quadrangle to the registrar the same afternoon they were signed, as they had gone every afternoon since the night of the notice. The fire-watch boy stood on his bucket at the covered walk's north arch each morning and chalked the night's hours on his slate in his flat-footed figures. Withrow walked once round the first quadrangle at the fifth bell with her spectacles in her hair, as she had walked it every day of the season, and stopped at the same place under the clock to speak to the same porter about the same leaking gutter. In the registrar's outer office the four grey boxes sat on their shelf with their lettering turned to the room, and nobody touched them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:167:*One.* The courtyard leaned. The two clerks from the first carriage were crossing toward the third before its door was fully open. The ranking rider of the escort moved four paces to his left without anyone telling him to. The counsel, at the records hall's door, stopped in the middle of a word about the gutter and stayed stopped. The whole courtyard leaned toward the third carriage. Cael had seen people do that round Withrow every day of the season, and he could have drawn it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:201:It was the fourth day of the silence. Nobody broke it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:125:"I do not ask what the room is being used for," he said. "I ask when a thing is wanted, and never why, and I have found that it saves me a great deal. I have come about the district's evening bag, which reached my counter a quarter of an hour ago with one item in it. It is addressed to the practitioner, care of the registrar, and so I have read it." He held out the packet. "I walked his docket up to a refectory table on the fourth day of the year. I thought I had better finish as I began."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:135:"Found," said Bracken. "The eighth I have seen, and the eighth found for the tier. I should like to say that it is the only one of the eight I was certain of before the bag came." He cleared his throat. "There is a pen on my counter. There has been one there since the fourth day of the year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:173:"*Or to her order*," he said, and his voice was not quite steady. "I wrote that in a deposit book on the first day of the month. I have been thirteen years at that counter and I knew what I was writing." He put his lantern down on the top stair, carefully, and took the last pair of glasses off his forehead, and then there was nothing between his eyes and the lamp. They were wet, and he let them be wet. He stood on the landing with three pairs of spectacles in his hands and wept, briefly and without any noise, as a man does who has kept true records all his life and has just watched one do what this one had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:195:"And Rooke," she said. "I haven't a line for that. He found my name at the foot of the Iron column and built a ladder up from it with his own hands, and he ate in an open seat on the day of the riders. I'm going to have to tell him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:15:Then the rest, which was ordinary, as Karis had said. *The seat at Ostrand gives notice that it has convened formal proceedings upon the petition served on the eighth day of the year. The seat will sit on its Fifth-days, upon the schedule of the academic year, and will rule before the year's close. Counsel of record will confer with the seat's clerk upon the schedule on the twenty-fifth day. The first sitting will be held on the sixth Fifth-day of the year. Senior evaluators of the district will be called to the evaluation seat on a day to be notified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:Naveth had re-papered the enrollment before the lamps were lit on the day of the ruling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:Lira let Karis off her promise on the Monday of the twenty-third week, at the scarred table after supper, and she did it as she did most things that mattered, without any warning at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:203:They went back to the quiet room on the Thursday of the twenty-third week, at the sixth bell, with the post in its bucket of sand and Lira's water and Brom's scoop. The Ember work had stopped for the three weeks of the archive and the hearing, and Lira said a thing you stopped practising was a thing you were letting go of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:79:"I've taken the day off," she said. "From all of it. Lira says I'm allowed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:85:Cael knew what it cost her. Everybody at the table knew. A whole afternoon of things happening and nothing written down was not a rest to Karis. It was a day of her own life left unwritten, on purpose, and she was spending it on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:37:*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier, mandatory upon a continental title. Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane; Path, Iron Skin; tier, Copper formal. A standard panel is convened by this docket. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the district seat. The panel will read the practitioner's record as it stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:"Don't thank me. It's a docket." Bracken picked up nothing, because there was nothing to pick up, and turned to go. Then he turned back, as if he had forgotten something. "The last Fifth-day of the year," he said to the table at large. "Somebody write it down. I'd hate for the house to be told I let it slip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:105:She turned it with a paper knife, flat, and smoothed it with the side of her hand, and went on reading. The hand in the volume was small and steady, and every seven in it wore a little crossed bar and then a second bar across that. He had heard her describe that hand at supper on the sixth day of the three weeks and had not thought about it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:"The respondent filed, in the week before the recess, a supplemental table of the renewals of this enrollment, with the day of each sitting and the day of each seal set side by side. I have read it. I read it twice, on a boat." He turned his head a little toward the house's table, toward Karis, and inclined it. "It is correct. The petitioner accepts it as filed, in every line, and adopts it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:83:It did not speak often, and when it did the long room went still to hear it, even now, even on a day of paper. It had the same unhurried voice it had used at the preliminary, a voice with no grade in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:3:On the Monday of the twenty-first week the outline still held, and Cael spent the first hour of the morning trying to break it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:63:"The weeklies," said Brom. He took the pencil from behind Cael's ear and turned the last sheet over to its clean side, and worked it out the other way round from how anybody else would have, from the answer down to the reasons, the way he checked a load he had already lifted. "That's what it comes to. The bridge-end stall sells three quires a day of each now, by what the girl says, and a quire's twenty-four sheets. The back of the pamphlet says both weeklies sell in three other towns as well as Ostrand, and all three are bigger, so call it four quires each to be safe. Four towns. Two weeklies. A copper a sheet." He wrote the figures in his large slow hand, each line lashed to the one under it before he trusted it with the next, and drew a line, and the total under the line was what he had said before he began. "There."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:123:She did not read anything aloud this time. She found the two provisions, the old one and the new, and laid the volume open at the old one with her finger beside its last two words, *shall receive*. Then she got the digest down from the bottom shelf, the one her assistant had found for herself on the day of *find it*, and opened it at the table of uses at its back, and turned it round so that they could see the line for themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:233:There was one slip for each. Lira's first meet off the hill, three weeks away now, on a road, against Silvers from other houses. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The next sitting at the wool exchange. And a fourth slip with nothing written on it, for the re-evaluation, whose date the seat would fix when it ruled. He set them in a row, as Karis had set out her six on the long table, and sat looking at them with Hesk's book open on his knee, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:25:*Three. Nothing served on this house since the eighth day of the year speaks of moving him anywhere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:The day after Kindling was the strangest day of all, because nothing happened in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:23:*Seln: at the copying table every working day of the recess, and at the middle of the bench every evening. Nobody has said a word about the bench. Nobody needs to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:37:"Then tell me what it looks like." He meant the working of it. Names for things had never interested him as much as how the things ran. "A day of it. If I said yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:91:"The merits are concluded. The seat sets the matter down for closing." The unhurried voice went on, with no more weight on these words than on any others it had spoken that year. "Two closing sittings, in the last month of the academic year, a week apart. The ruling will be given upon the last Fifth-day of that month. The parties will be notified of the hours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:203:*Deposited for safe keeping, the first day of the year's last month. Three boxes, roped. Depositor: K. Dellenmoor. Contents: the depositor's own papers. To be released to the depositor, or to her order.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:181:On the Tuesday of the eighteenth week Karis asked if she might try two things, and asked it at breakfast, formally, before the porridge, so that everybody at the scarred table could refuse it in front of witnesses if they wanted to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:207:"On the board in the main hall. Petitions of standing are always posted, within a day of filing, all of them, in full." Gerda set down her spoon. "It's the rule that protects whoever files one. If a petition's posted, everyone can see it was asked, and nobody can quietly lose it in a drawer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:103:Cael sat in the plain chair and understood what he had been given, and what he had not. It was not warmth. In the first months after Denvash he had wanted warmth badly, had wanted some grown person to be moved by a boy's bad luck, and it had gone the same way every time. On the day of his Kindling the certification office had been gentle with him. The clerk had lowered her voice. She had apologized for the classification while her other hand stamped it. Then the appointment ended, and the gentleness ended with it, on the step outside, at the exact moment the door shut. Naveth was offering something else. He had opened a book of numbers and shown Cael why he was worth having, and that steadied Cael more than kindness would have. Anybody could call kindness back without warning. An incentive came with its terms written down, and you could read the terms, and you could see from a long way off when they were going to run out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Lira crossed the east hall again on the First-day of the recess's second week, and this time she did not know she was doing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:It did not ask, for the quiet thing from the stair never asked. It only offered, as it had offered every day of his life for two years, and it offered now with a kind of patience, as if it had been waiting since supper for him to see the sense of it. A man was sitting somewhere ahead in the dark, counting, and Cael was trying not to be counted; and here in him, sealed and paid for every morning since he woke, was the one thing he owned that could take him down that track past any man alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:"Dated the first day of the year," said Bracken. "The morning of convocation. Somebody a week's road from here pressed that wax while the Chancellor stood on her platform telling this house to be what its papers say it is. I don't suppose they knew. I don't suppose it matters." He touched the second mark. "Served this morning. Seven days on a road the post does in three, at a walk, with three horses and a man in the middle who'd been told the pace. It wasn't sent to arrive. It was sent to be watched arriving. I watched it for a full minute from my window, and so did the gate, and so did the coats at the foot of the hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:167:"I watch the sittings from my window," the provost said. It was the mild dry voice of the room with the ledger, the second day of term. "I make a point of it. One can't see a great deal from up there. I saw a girl put together something very fine. I saw a boy go down on one knee in the middle of it. And then I saw the girl laugh." He regarded Cael over his spectacles. "That is what I saw, and that is all I saw. I find it generally wise, in my position, to have seen exactly as much as can be seen from a window."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:165:He took the provision's boxes back down to the archive in the middle of the afternoon, with the enforcement digests he had kept at his elbow for three weeks. He did it because he would not need them again and it seemed wrong to keep them from her on that day of all days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:"I used to think," said Lira, a mile on, "that the best day of my life would be the day a letter came from Fenmark. Saying they'd been wrong. Saying the examiner had been wrong, and the hall had been wrong, and they'd like me to come back, and they were sorry." She walked a few paces. "I used to lie awake writing it for them. I had it word for word. I'd changed it a hundred times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-41.md:211:It was the Thursday officers' table, the weekly one, in the long cold room on the second floor where the river-side and the Ranked-side officers sat for an hour together and read each other the week. Coss had sat at it every Thursday for eleven years and said, by his own count, about four things worth saying at it in all that time. He was there to be seen to be there. Most of the men round it were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:409:Lira was sitting on the low wall at the side of the yard with her knees up and her coat round them, in the last of the light. She had waited, and had not come to the table, but had sat on the wall, as she had sat on the river wall on the day of the six weeks, with the look of somebody who had been happening to pass a particular place for about an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:37:Then he opened the log. Behind a year of ordinary entries were the two pages he had left empty on purpose, the day of the grey slip and the day he had read Havel's return, because a thing written down could be found. He turned forward to today's page and wrote on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-60.md:71:He had not meant to read it. Nobody read the district returns, which came in every week in a canvas sack and stood a month in a rack by the door before they went down to the cellars, counted and never opened. But the Ardenmere return had been put on his desk on Monday of last week with a line under it in red, and since then he had gone to the rack himself for the next one before the clerk could tie it up.

