editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:258:*Day 88. Five in Karis's room by three stairs across an hour. The case opened. What was in it does not go into a book. It is in a room with two keys, and the five of us have it, and that is where it will stay.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:They had all been afraid of him. He had first seen it on the morning of his Kindling, and had not had the word for it then. It had looked like anger, mostly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:223:It was the kind reading, and half the continent had reached it already. A small house in the hills had staked a year on it, and he did not think less of them. It said the file was a mistake. Some clerk at some station had made one wrong mark at a boy's Kindling, and everything since, two academies and a hearing and a provision and a stamp, had been the long labour of picking out one bad stitch. It was comfortable because it asked nobody to change their mind about anything: the machine was sound, it had caught its sleeve once on one boy, and decent people had spent a year working the sleeve free.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:Nobody had decided anything about the boy when the designation went onto his sheet. The Kindling had entered it, in the first minutes of the file's life. It had stood ever since. Three houses, a public adjudication, and a registrar's index that left the other side almost nothing to say: it had come through all of them. And through all of it nobody, at any height, had put forward another classification, or amended the entry by a word, or written so much as a sheet suggesting it be looked at again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:151:Coss had not minded that. Over three weeks he had come rather to like it. It had a narrow bed with a patched counterpane somebody had sewn with care, and a washstand with a cracked jug, and a single chair, and a table under the window just wide enough for a document case and a lamp. On the wall by the door a nail held a little framed sampler worked in faded thread, the alphabet and a row of crooked birds. Some mother, some winter, had sat by this window waiting for a child to come down the hill from a Kindling, and had passed the time with a needle, and left it behind. He had looked at the birds a good many evenings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:195:"The first sheet is a copy of a station's Kindling record," said Karis. She read it in her plain voice, the one she used for a citation, giving each word its weight and no more. "*River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth. A girl of the town. Reading at the frame: Current, second rank. Keeper's note at the margin: reads into Mire. Strong.*" She laid it face up in front of her. "That's the keeper's hand copied. You can see where he pressed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:201:"*Order of reclassification at evaluation. River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth.*" He stopped, and found his place. "*Per corrective classification at Kindling evaluation: enter Current, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks following entry.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-18.md:299:*Copper. Rank 2. Wind Path.* Her two declarations underneath, in the order they had come, the first one from Kindling and the second from the spring, which had come with its own odd small rush of pleasure that she still remembered. There was no new line and no change. It had looked exactly like this since the spring, and she was used to it, and only very occasionally did it annoy her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-18.md:305:She had never asked him. You did not ask; declarations were private, and an Arbiter was the most private thing a person had, and she had not asked anybody what their Arbiter said since the week of her own Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:101:"Section one also says what a primary classification is. *The classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.*" He did not hurry. "Mine was returned at Kindling. It took eleven seconds. It's entered in the Supplementary Register, statute nineteen, with an incidence count. I have a primary classification. It isn't one the standard framework can use, which is why section four's first half fits me. But section four lets you enter a practitioner into the category, and section one says who the category is for. It's for practitioners without one. Not being assessable by ordinary means isn't the same as not being classified."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:So they laid their own fire in it. Seln's flint was at the bottom of his coat. Karis put out one finger and lit the kindling with a point no bigger than a pin's head, as she had lit a page for Cael under a cart two nights before. The red wood caught fast and burned with a high clear flame; and its smoke stood up out of the valley, straight, in the still air, to twice the height of the trees, where anything with eyes on any of seven ridges might have looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:197:It was the same fact, and that was what he saw, sitting across a fire from her, and it went through him coldly. Nothing out here measured anybody; and to him that was a door standing open, and to her it was a floor taken away. He had been measured wrongly ever since his Kindling and had wanted only to be let alone; she had been measured rightly, a rung at a time, and it had been the nearest thing to love that any institution ever gave her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:35:The taxonomy chart still hung on the end wall. Somebody had thought of taking it down and decided it was too much trouble, or had not thought of it at all. It hung where it had hung on his first morning at Greyvane, a tall sheet of stiff paper with every Path the schedule recognized laid out in families, Movement and Hardening and Edge, each name in a neat box, and lines joining the boxes. He had sat on the right-hand bench under it for twenty weeks. Oona had sat beside him under it until her Kindling, and had asked the taxonomy master, in the first week, why shattered wasn't on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:117:At the very bottom of the page, in the oldest ink, was the first line, and it was not a rank at all. It was a Kindling a year ahead of the law, checked three times and entered with a note, of a kind the registry's tables counted by the century and had never needed more than one hand to count. And the classification, which had come back inside the hour: Storm. A Path that worked the weight of the air and the fire in the air through one channel. The registry kept all its living Storm practitioners on a single sheet, and hers had been the newest name on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:129:It was much smaller than she had been told. Everybody who spoke of Kindlings spoke of them as great things, and it was not great. It was a mark of light no larger than her palm, finer at its edges than any engraver could have cut, turning very slowly in the air in front of her. It was not in any hurry. It had come a long way, she thought, and it could wait. It was older than the pear tree and the school, older than the city outside the school wall, and older than the law the city kept, which said quite plainly that nothing of this kind was to happen to anybody before the age of fourteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:261:It had lain there, under three other things, since the third evening of the junior's visit. A station's Kindling entry, four years old, for a girl in the northern district, her Path and her first rank in a station clerk's ordinary writing. The junior's notes beside it, in a hand still deciding what shape it meant to be: the frame, the clock, the bouts, the tables. The two recommendations they had settled between them. *Second frame reading: within tolerance.* And under the station's line, *Continue observation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:The first sheet was a copy of the registration. *Caelen Hesk-ward. Registration 41-7843-V.* The certifying office was on a street called Weaver's Row, in Denvash, and the officer was a Warden-Adjunct named Pellin, whose signature was small and quick and gave nothing away. The date of the Kindling was in the autumn, fifty-odd days ago. On the line for the classification there was one word in square brackets, stamped, and no tier, no rank and no Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:The third sheet was a gate entry. *Denvash, Outer gate, eastbound, on foot.* It was dated the third day after the Kindling. It was passive, which meant that a guard had read the card in the ordinary way and the record had marked it and nobody had been asked to do anything about it. Eastbound from Denvash, on foot, was the merchant road, and the merchant road came here in two days, and anybody who knew the road would have guessed this city. That part was not strange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A non-standard result at Kindling set a procedure going. He had never handled a [SHATTERED] file himself; he did not know anybody who had. But he had handled three files for results the standard framework had trouble with, and they had all grown the same way, in the same order, as faithfully as a tree grows rings. The certifying district wrote a preliminary risk assessment within the first week, because the certifying district was frightened of being blamed for anything that came after. Then a residential compliance note followed, setting out what the household's standing now was and what it was likely to become. Then there was a cross-reference to the standing-holder's own file, because the residential notice threatened a guild review in so many words. When the Compact notified a guild under that statute, the guild opened its review by its own procedure, and the review had a number, and the number went into the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:*Kindling assessment, observer track. Oona. Registered Arbiter Station, town. Second bell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:It was Karis who lit it. She had come over the stone with a morning's pencil in her arms and her cheek bleeding, and she put the sheets into Lira's lap and held out her right hand; and a point stood on the kindling that Seln had laid ready before first light. It was the first thing that any of them had declared in a day, and Cael saw her look at her own palm afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:He tried to see it, Hesk sitting on the step of the workshop on Fen Street, in the street, where people could see him, with a letter in his hands and his big square face doing something Cael had never once seen it do, and he could not. He had seen Hesk prepared, and still, and careful, and sometimes angry, and once, across the workshop bench on the second day after Kindling, ashamed, but he had never seen him afraid. It had happened anyway, a week ago, on a step, where nobody but strangers could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:"Under the policy there's something older: the reading, whatever it is that wakes behind your breastbone at Kindling and reads you. The function sits on it. It's older than the function by however long, and the case doesn't say how long, and I don't know." She laid her hand on the cloth. "But the case does show me one thing about what was there before the function came, because the first memoranda had to clear it out of the way. The first three issues, about eleven years apart, in the oldest paper. And they're not about the forty. They're about the old custom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:161:"I went through everything for the anomaly," she said to Cael, "for session nine. You asked me to, at Greyvane, and I've never forgotten that you asked." She did not look at the slip. "Anything in the forty that read across a family with nobody of that family near. Anything in the reports about a practitioner who took something from no one. Any memorandum that mentions Tide by name, or a reading at a distance, or one that came without an engagement. Anything in the leaf." She shook her head. "Nothing. Not one line in it touches session nine. The function works at Kindling, and session nine wasn't a Kindling. Whatever it is, the case didn't do it and doesn't know about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:It was not only the hold. A reading taken with that thing could be checked by nobody, and Cael had lived since his Kindling under figures that could not be checked by the person they concerned. What Karis did with a knotted cord, any carter could do after her, at the same place, and get the same number or call her a liar. That was the map the man was buying. The other would only have been a boy on a hillside saying that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:161:"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:111:She was quiet. He knew what she was seeing, because he was seeing it too. Standard protocol meant a station: a frame in a stone room, a registry officer at its side, a practitioner standing in it while the frame read him. It was how every Path on the continent had been declared since before the registry kept books. The frame read the Path and named it, and set a tier against it, and a rank inside the tier, and the officer wrote down what the frame had said, and that was a person's classification for life. It was how Lira had been declared, and Brom, and Karis herself. It was how the frame at Denvash had been asked, on the morning of his Kindling, what he was, and had answered with one word in brackets and nothing else at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:"Four, in the Compact's own digests, in all the years they go back, who carried this word. I've read their files, one by one, at the long table downstairs. The first was ordered out of the place they lived nine days after the Kindling that flagged them. The second in six. The third in eleven. The fourth in four." He kept his eyes on Yorlan's. "Not one of them had a hearing. Not one of them was asked anything that their file sets down. In every one, an order went out under an authority nobody looked at, because the word sat on the form in the place where a classification goes, and everybody who handled it trusted that somebody before them had checked. Three of those files don't end. They stop. I'm the fifth. I'm the first who's ever been let stand in a room like this and put the question out loud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:289:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:79:By sixteen she was Iron Rank Three, early for anyone and unheard of at Ternhall for a student two years past Kindling. Her name sat at the head of the standings in a hand the clerk had stopped bothering to rub out between terms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:203:He was the exhibition steward's cousin, and he was everything his large signature on the challenge form had promised. He was quick, and he knew it; he was handsome, and he knew that too. Nobody in his valley had beaten him since his Kindling, and somewhere along the way he had begun to treat that as a law of nature rather than of valleys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:71:*Second frame reading: within tolerance.* The note was in a young man's hand, a hand still deciding what shape it meant to be. It was pinned to the copy of a station's Kindling entry, four years old, for a girl in the northern district: her Path, her first rank, in a station clerk's ordinary writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:145:It was the leaf about his Kindling. He had not known Karis had written one. It set out, in her plainest prose, the morning at Weaver's Row in Denvash: the circle of pale wood, the registrar who conducted it, the brass plate that lit late, the one bracketed word on the card, the classification line stamped where it had stood empty for fourteen years. Every fact in it was true and every fact was his, and he had never heard any of it in another person's sentences before. He read it in his own voice to a registrar in the middle of the night with the rain on the roof, and Bracken's finger moved down the other copy a line behind him, and twice Bracken said "Slower," and once he said "Again, that line," and made a small mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:373:And for the first time since his Kindling, nothing anywhere was deciding what he was, except him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:111:*Four of them, now. I've been watched by four kinds of people since my Kindling. I think I can name the kinds.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:179:"He'd done nothing," said Withrow. "Twenty years before, a station clerk made a slip writing down his Kindling, and the one officer who could have sworn to the true reading was dead, so the slip could never be put right. The registry stood him in a frame again. Twice. Twice the frame had nothing to say. That was all." She touched the printed list beside her old note. "These are the nine headings. Employment, guild, credentials, property, debts, enrollments, travel, office, and *other relationships*. The district was short of clerks that year and I was good at it. I walked his fences myself. Seven weeks. I was proud of how careful it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:223:Cael took it to the hearth. There were not a dozen people in that long room so early. Brom was laying out the bowls at their end of it, as he had at every halt, and Lira sat on the hearthstone with her right hand full of kindling. Karis had come out of the room behind the chimney with the hide under her arm, and Seln stood at the far end of the fire, by the small window that looked down on the landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:269:He read them one at a time, with his finger under the line. *Assessor.* No. *Senior evaluation.* No. *Primary classification: the classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.* He stopped. He read it again, and his finger did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:191:And Cael understood why on the third, from the inside, which was the worst place to understand anything. To move inside a crowd's movement, he had to know what the crowd's movement was going to do: which gap would open, which shoulder turn, which two Mire boys were about to halt in the middle of the walk to argue about a girl. So he went to the gaze for it, the whole of it, every layer at once, as he had gone to it in every crowd since long before his Kindling. And the moment he did, he was the most awake person in the covered walk, and the other half of the thing went out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:43:"*Memorandum to keepers of stations and to clerks of entry. Corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.*" She put her finger under the line on the newest leaf. "And the body. It's three sentences, and it's been three sentences since this one." She touched the yellowest. "A word changes here and there, and *shall* became *is to*. A clause about the post came in when the post did. Otherwise you could lay the oldest on top of the newest and read straight through them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:131:"The registry corrects them. At Kindling, when the station reads them. Downward, by a form, for as long as it has had paper to write the form on. That is what I have carried."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:99:He had done this once before. He had been new to a district, and not long past his Kindling, and he had stood in front of a board at a crossroads in Ardenmere for the better part of a morning before he let himself go near the yard it spoke of. A girl had told him afterward that almost nobody did that. Most people either never looked, or walked straight in and were carried out the same day, and he had done a third thing, which was to stand in the road and try to understand the board first. It had served him then, and he did it again now, slowly, a slip at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:He looked for the anger, because he thought he might be owed some, and found instead something older and more tired that had his own face. He had kept a great many things in a great many neat columns for a great many years: Tuesdays, the provisions, the preliminary assessment, and a file he had once seen opened in a room above his own clearance. He had kept them from the boy until the boy found them out for himself and came to him angry, across the workshop bench, on the second day after Kindling. *A few years,* he had said in the side room at Weaver's Row, and he remembered the boy's face. If there was a figure missing from this letter, it was missing for the boy's reason. He would tell it when it was worth telling, or he would not, and either way it was not Hesk's to dig out of a letter with a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:He had not spoken to her since the morning of the boy's Kindling, when they had passed her two blocks from home on the way back from Weaver's Row. She had been on the other side of the street with a basket, and she had lifted one open hand to the boy without crossing, and the boy had lifted his. He had heard from Cael's letters and from Ressa that she had gone to the healers', and had not thought about what that meant. It meant this: a fourteen-year-old girl carrying basins in an apron too big for her, with her sleeves pushed up past the elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:141:"This is your enrollee of record. Look at the jaw. You could split kindling on that jaw. I've watched him on more floors than anybody in this room, and I give you my word as your captain that he has never once stood with his feet like that. If he did I'd have him on his back before the glass turned." He folded it in quarters into the file. "Confiscated. You'll have it back when one of you can tell me what's wrong with the stance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:151:Beneath the category, in smaller type, a printed note explained what the category was. It was reserved for any prospective practitioner who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. Such students were usually children whose families had asked for early schooling before their certification appointments. A Path, a tier, a rank, any record from an Arbiter: the category conferred none of those. What it did confer was a seat in lectures, training under supervision, and academic standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:He read the category line a third time, and the gloss under it. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:15:He had seen the name before, of course. It was on the registry's own extract in the enrollment file, and on the Greyvane transcript, and on the summons that had brought him up the bluff road in the first place. But it was not a name anybody had ever called him. It was the name a clerk at Denvash had built out of a boy and a workshop on the morning of his Kindling. The given name had been made formal, the household he came from had been turned into a word with a hyphen in it, and a number stood after it that meant nothing to anyone but the registry. Seeing it here, in a list of five, under Lira and Brom and Karis and Ephram, was like hearing his own voice played back from the far side of a valley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:He had copied it a month ago from the foot of the registry summary page, under the four-line entry he already knew by heart. It was a footnote, and it told anyone whose Kindling came out non-standard to present themselves at a Compact district office inside two days for something it called standard compliance processing. He had gone and found out what that processing involved, written it underneath, and struck both lines through. He never struck anything through. A wrong figure stayed on the page with the right one beside it, because Hesk said an erased mistake was a mistake you got to make again. This one he had crossed out anyway, and he still could not have said whether he had done it to be rid of it or so that he would always know where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:69:The file's standing entries ran down beneath, in order: his Kindling at Denvash, the Greyvane hearing and its finding, the provision with the stamp and its renewals, the tournament's entry with all eleven of its words. And between the hearing and the provision, given its full title as if it were the most ordinary line on the page, sat the one he had known was on his file since the inspection year and had never once seen written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:He was not frightened. Not yet. He was something nearer cold. It was the feeling he had had on the morning of his Kindling at Denvash, standing in a frame that had nothing to say about him and was perfectly calm about it. The fright would come later, he knew, at the window, in the dark, where there was time for it; not here, where there was work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:21:"They've stopped asking that entirely. They gave it away in the sixth paragraph." The counsel closed the volume on her rule to keep the place. "Now they ask whether the registry can read you. Everybody at this table knows the answer. It has been in your file since your Kindling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:195:Cael was trying very hard not to laugh, because he suspected she would not forgive him for it. "Ask me again after your Kindling," he said, "if you still want to. You'll be busy with your own things then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:231:"Nothing. It went dark at my Kindling, eleven seconds in, and it's been dark since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:155:"Because I've no defence to offer that I believe," he said. "Counsel has tried for two days and found none either. I would sign that form again this afternoon. We made the category fit him because the alternative was a boy with nowhere to go, and I'm not sorry. But the gloss keeps the category for a candidate who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. He has, and the Warden can prove the day. A magistrate doesn't rule on whether I'm sorry. This is the best-drafted paper anybody has ever sent up my stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:177:What had he practised longest? Reading people. Watching a body until it told him what it meant to do, and writing it down. That was six years, at least, since the Outer District and long before any Kindling. Under that, older still, was a boy hiding at the back of rooms where adults were deciding things about him, and learning to keep his face still while they did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:135:"I'm scared about my Kindling. Not very. A little. My mother says it doesn't hurt. My uncle says it's like being hit by a door you didn't know was there." She frowned. "I don't know which one is lying."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:125:A young man from the secretariat answered, after some looking at papers. The office that had tried to follow the designation home had followed it into the registry, he said, to a sub-layer under the registry's own floor, and there lost it; there was no instrument of record behind it, and the attempt had been closed. The room made the small pause that rooms of that height make, and a line went into the minute, and the sitting went on. The designation was useful, and at that height useful things are not asked where they came from. It had sat in the file since the first minutes of his Kindling, and it sat there still: a flag no living hand had authorized.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:*A few years*, in the side room. *Some years*, under the lamps on Chandler Street, about the folding chairs. They were the same span of time. For as long as Hesk had known what Cael's coupling looked like, he had been going to sit in the dark behind a curtain every Tuesday and learn how a body moves when it carries a Path, so that he would know what he was seeing if it ever happened to Cael's. Then, on the night before Kindling, with nothing left to learn and no time left to explain it, he had taken Cael along. He had sat him down and said *watch his hips*, and *the third exchange is where they tell you the truth*, and *there*, out loud, over and over, so that one day, with Hesk a week's post away and no chair beside him in the dark, Cael would be able to say *there* for himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:181:Since his Kindling he had not had one day without somebody writing him down. Assessors, a panel, an Arbiter with a classification, a warden with a challenge in his pocket, and a semester evaluation waiting at the end of this one; grey coats at every gate and ferry on two roads; and now a whole hill that had bought his transcript at six silver marks a copy and read it before he walked through the gate. He had grown so used to it that he only noticed it now in the way a man notices his own coat when somebody else asks him to take it off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:In his head he had made the place enormous. He had never once meant to, and he had never noticed doing it, but somewhere since his own Kindling every registry station in the world had become, in the back of his mind, a hall with a high roof and a long bench and a cold floor. And here was one of them, being a building, between a rope-maker and a cat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:153:Assessor Ilsev. She had read his Kindling across a desk in a district office with a wall so thin he had heard the next clerk sneeze. She had read a Greyvane hearing's exhibits to the last page and followed them where they went with a warden glaring at her across the table. Six of her findings were in his head, and he had still not settled whether she was the worst danger on that manifest or the best hope on it. Seeing her walk did not decide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:There were eleven names on the manifest and he had pages on nine of them. The library had given him three of the four escort riders out of a dozen years of attendance lists, the counsel out of her published findings, and the three clerks out of a registry yearbook that nobody had borrowed in a decade. Havel had a page with a question of Lira's at the foot of it. Ilsev had the thickest page and the least use, because six findings told him what she would do with a document and nothing at all about what she did with a morning. The fourth rider was too new to be anywhere. And the eleventh name had one line on its page, and under the line, in his own hand, a single word: *Lira*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:245:The first page set out the provision. It did so accurately. It quoted the half page Cael had read more times than he had counted in the archive, and then explained, in plain sentences, what the provision was for: the academic exposure and supervised training of candidates still waiting on their formal Kindling assessment, which was to say, in nearly every case, children. It noted that such candidates were assessed monthly by demonstration, and that the standard was consistent, observable practice. It observed, correctly, that a child who had not yet Kindled would ordinarily have very little to practise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:*Four. I want it. Since my Kindling there hasn't been one day when somebody wasn't looking, and I would like, just once, to stand in a room and go unseen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:283:*The quarter-burst. Gain: the clerk keeps his feet; the record keeps its shape. Bill: every bit of one hip, three days, likely four. I shut a burst halfway through paying for it, which I have never once managed on purpose since my Kindling, and I can't say how I did it, so I can't say I can do it again. One point isn't a line. Karis taught me that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:49:*Nobody builds that at fifteen unless somebody has been coming for him since his Kindling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:It was a chart. A line of time ran along the bottom, with his Kindling at its left-hand end and this month at its right. Six marks stood on the line, one for each fragment. Beside each was its date, and the gap to the next one, and how long it had taken to settle. Over every mark she had written its price in her small upright hand, sorted under four heads of her own invention, which a little key at the foot of the sheet explained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:91:"I have." Coss brought out a second paper, a thick packet tied crosswise. "The enrollment is invalid as categorized. The category's own gloss reserves it for a candidate who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. You were assessed, at the station in Denvash. The registry holds the date, and I hold the entry. The provision's drafting records show what it was written for, and it was not written for you." He set the packet on Naveth's blotter and squared it to the edge. "That is the case against the category. The case that makes it urgent is this one, Provost. Keep an enrollment on your rolls that you know to be invalid, and a chartered academy exposes its own charter to review. I'm not threatening you. I'm telling you why this can't be left alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:"My Kindling is in nine days." She said it as if it were part of the same sentence. "Nine days after tomorrow. I counted it from yours, not from mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:196:"Just one. The first one. The one you got at Kindling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:208:Cael knew it too. Declarations were the language an Arbiter used, and every practitioner received them from Kindling on: a small glowing sigil only you could see, and then words in a form only you could read, telling you what your Path had just given you at your new rank. They were the private record of a person's growth, written in a hand nobody else ever saw. People did share them, brothers sometimes, or married couples, or a teacher and a student who had trusted each other for years. But you did not ask, any more than you would ask to read someone's letters, and you did not show unless showing meant something. Lira knew all of this perfectly well. She asked because she liked watching Brom refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:91:He did it so quietly that half the gallery did not notice the case had changed direction. He took the certified copy of the enrollment form from the first case, where it had lain since the morning, and set it on the green cloth's edge in front of the bench. Beside it he laid a single registry sheet, which Lira could not read from where she sat but could guess at. Then he read the gloss under the category line once more, slowly. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.* Then he read from the registry sheet the name of a station in Denvash, and a date, and the word *assessed*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:181:He gave each its district and its year and its name. The first was Sarnholt. A chartered academy there had enrolled under the provision a youth who was past the age of Kindling and had refused to be tested, on the reasoning that a person who would not be assessed had not yet been assessed. The enrollment had been voided inside a single sitting. The second was Wexley, where a registry clerk had made an honest error that nobody caught for three years, so that a girl who had Kindled and been classified was entered on an academy's rolls as untested. When it came to light the academy had defended the enrollment on the ground of the girl's good standing, and lost, and had spent two years under review of its own charter for having tried. The third had no name read out, only its district and its year, because it had never come to a hearing at all. A disputed assessment, an enrollment under the provision, a challenge filed; and the academy had withdrawn the enrollment before the magistrate arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:He read it in full, from the certified copy with Prynn's pencil at its foot, every sentence of it from *a candidate without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter* to *the panel's finding shall be entered in the candidate's record*. He read it slowly, and when he reached the end Yorlan said, "Again, for the roll." He read it again at exactly the same pace, with the same small pauses in the same places, so that the second reading sounded like the first laid over itself. Then he took the enrollment form, the certified copy of the one Cael had signed on his first afternoon. He laid it beside the provision and read the category line from it. *Unclassified observer.* Then he read the gloss beneath it. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.* He read that twice as well, without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:293:*I. The oldest in the digests. Flagged at a Kindling. Order of removal, unopposed. Nine days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:295:*II. Generations later. Flagged at a Kindling. Order of removal, unopposed. Six days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:297:*III. Later again. Flagged at a Kindling. Order of removal, unopposed. Eleven days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:299:*IV. The most recent, and still long before I was born. Flagged at a Kindling. Order of removal, unopposed. Four days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:31:Oona was in the middle of it, in a clean grey dress he had never seen her wear and her hair pinned so tight it pulled at her temples. Her slate was on her knees, face down, and both hands were pressed flat on top of it. Her mother sat on her left, very straight, in the dark travelling cloak, with her hands folded in her lap. There was nobody else on the bench. It was the only Kindling the station had that morning, the clerk said, and he said it kindly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:The clerk was writing in his ledger at the far door. Cael watched him write, and knew without having to see the page what the line would be, because he had copied out the gloss of the category on his own form three times over cold tea in the first week of the term. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.* Somewhere in the clerk's ledger, and by the end of the week in a regional book at the bottom of a long road, and on Monday morning at the intake desk at the top of the hill, Oona was being moved out of a category she no longer fitted, by the plainest procedure there was. The observer track at Greyvane had held two names since the autumn. On Monday it would hold one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:155:"You said I could study you," she said. "After. In the second month. You said after my Kindling, if I still wanted to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:229:*Kindling assessment entered. Candidate formerly enrolled under demonstration provision. Classification: Anchor Path, Copper. Category of enrollment vacated; standard enrollment to follow.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:237:The plain-face argument had been sound from the first night, at the waystation table, with its two true lines. A category whose gloss kept it, word for word, for a candidate who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. A respondent who had undergone it, at the station in Denvash, on a date the registry held. That much he could have filed from his district office. But a tribunal did not like to be told that a provision meant what its plainest sentence said, if another sentence of the same provision said something wider; it liked to be shown. So he had gone looking for what the provision had been written to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:243:Then he had found the precedents, which had been easier, because the enforcement digests were indexed by people who expected to be asked. Three times in other districts, across the long life of the provision, somebody had tried to stretch it over a person it had not been written for. Once it had been a youth past the age of Kindling who had refused to be tested. Once it had been a disputed assessment the family would not accept. Once it had been a clerk's honest mistake, never caught until it was challenged. Three academies, three enrollments, three challenges. Each enrollment had been voided. None had survived the hearing, and one had not survived to a hearing at all. He had read each digest twice and copied the citations onto a clean sheet in his small plain hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:*The respondent is enrolled at Greyvane Academy as an unclassified observer, a category its own gloss limits to candidates yet to undergo formal Kindling assessment.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:159:*The respondent underwent formal Kindling assessment at the certification station in Denvash, on the date entered in the registry, and was classified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:231:"The way this school takes its students breeds paper quarrels, lad. Families of late Kindlers who argue with the station. Old schools that sit on a re-certification candidate's file. And the big schools, which take it badly when a student chooses poorer floors here for the sake of a better idea." He laid down his pen. "Since before your Kindling I've been dawdling, lawfully, in front of the Compact. It's the skill this place has practised hardest. Yours is merely the first case big enough to call on all of it together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:69:The code had never held it. Only the Arbiter's instrument did: it wrote the word out on the morning of a Kindling, a flag of its own making. Generation after generation of enforcement had simply treated that flag as if it were a classification, because it looked like one and sat where one sat on the form, and because nobody in all that time, so far as he could tell, had ever thought to go and look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:103:Cael sat in the plain chair and understood what he had been given, and what he had not. It was not warmth. In the first months after Denvash he had wanted warmth badly, had wanted some grown person to be moved by a boy's bad luck, and it had gone the same way every time. On the day of his Kindling the certification office had been gentle with him. The clerk had lowered her voice. She had apologized for the classification while her other hand stamped it. Then the appointment ended, and the gentleness ended with it, on the step outside, at the exact moment the door shut. Naveth was offering something else. He had opened a book of numbers and shown Cael why he was worth having, and that steadied Cael more than kindness would have. Anybody could call kindness back without warning. An incentive came with its terms written down, and you could read the terms, and you could see from a long way off when they were going to run out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:29:"The kitchen woodpile," he said. "There's a stack behind the bakehouse door the cook keeps for the ovens. Split oak, mostly, and a few old fence posts somebody brought up from the town." He considered his spoon. "She calls it kindling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:*Kindling assessment, observer track. Oona. Registered Arbiter Station, town. Second bell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:43:"And who keeps the list?" the instructor asked the room, and answered himself before anyone could guess. "The registry. Every Kindling in every district in Valdris is entered within the week, and in all its history the registry has never once taken an entry out. It is a ledger that has never closed an account." He let that sit. "The list on this canvas has been changed many times, too, by amendment. When you're older, read the amendments. Every one of them is a confession. It tells you what somebody was afraid of, the year they wrote it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:51:"Your first declaration comes at Kindling, when your Arbiter gives you your Path and your first ability together. After that, new declarations come as you climb. Not every rank brings one; some do. You'll learn the rhythm of your own Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:85:"Because it isn't a Path. It's a registry matter, a classification the registry has used, very rarely, in its records. It isn't one of the Paths on the schedule, so it isn't on the chart. The chart only lists Paths." He picked up his chalk again. "It's not something you'll need for your Kindling, Oona. I wouldn't worry about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:127:He had known since orientation that she was on the track; he had not, until that moment, thought about what that meant. The category had been built for someone, and it had been built for her, a guild family's daughter who wanted to sit in lectures before her Kindling so that when her Arbiter came she would already know the shape of the chart. That was the real use of the hollow circle. He was the one borrowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:"Form efficiency. How much more you get out of a declaration, rank by rank, by refining the way you use it. Not by getting stronger. By getting better." She tapped the steep part. "Early on, every hour of form work pays. You learn where your weight goes, you stop wasting motion, and your first declaration does twice what it did at Kindling without your gaining an ounce of raw output." She moved the chalk along the bend. "Then it slows. Somewhere around the middle of a tier, for most people, form work stops paying the way it did. You've learned the big lessons, and the small ones are smaller. And here—" She tapped the flat part. "—is what this school, and every school I know of, calls the ceiling: the point past which refining your form gives you so little that you're better off spending the hours on raw output instead. More force. More endurance. Bigger declarations, earned the slow way through rank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-04.md:113:He kept his face where it was. He had a fair idea what had got them counting. It was on the officials page in a column of dates with nothing written beside it. There was no evidence for the thing he wanted to write, only the shape of it, and the shape was a boy who had come in through the west gate a year ago with a word on him. The registry's word for him was *Shattered*. It had been written against four people before him. Three of them had been dead within weeks of their Kindling, and the fourth was a line that said nobody knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:Nobody described Kindling the same way twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:11:*Like remembering something you never knew,* announced the girl from three streets over. She was two years past her own Kindling and explained it with the confidence of someone to whom a thing has happened exactly once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:This was the morning before his Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:Everyone in the northern quadrant knew Joren's result, so Cael did too. Joren had spent a year predicting his Kindling, starting with *something fast* and narrowing it week by week until, by midsummer, he was telling people at the market *Blade Path, Silver-track, probably Rank 3 by sixteen*. He had told Garrik, and Ressa, and even the guard on the Merchant Row gate, who had said *a door opening* and then *good luck, son* in a voice that made Cael wince. The whole quadrant had followed his certainty with the close attention people give to certainty they suspect is wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:Cael was not near enough to see its shape. He did not need to be. He had seen that shape pressed into dark wax on the second morning after Kindling, on a paper that was still folded small inside the back cover of his old notebook, with its creases gone soft from being opened. It was a hand, holding up a balance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:He lay on his right side on the cot with Yeni's lamp down to a bead and the wheezing man whistling by the door, and he did the thing he had done on the night before Kindling and the night before Renn and every night before a bout since: he went through it. Not the fight, which he did not know, but everything round it, the things he could count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:267:He woke before the light, as he had woken on the morning of Kindling, and lay still and took stock, as he did on days that mattered: ceiling, the beam across it with its old nail holes, the wheezing man asleep, Yeni's cot empty already, the grey of an Ardenmere morning in the window, which he had stopped seeing months ago and saw again now on purpose. He got up and dressed and went down, and Torvin's wife put a bowl of oats in front of him without a word, and stood over him with her spoon until he had eaten all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:27:*Not taking. It isn't all there, and it isn't mine the way Lira's Path is hers. She was declared at Kindling and it's been hers every day since, whole, to use when she likes. What I've got is more like the outline of how she moves, without the inside. It comes properly when something's coming at me and I haven't got time to choose. When I ask for it on purpose, slowly, by the cot, it comes too, but badly. It's like copying out a word in somebody else's handwriting. You can see whose hand it was meant to be. Nobody would mistake it for theirs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Kindling assessment, the usual way. Your Arbiter wakes here, and the result goes straight to the registry ledger." She laid two fingers on a flat brass plate let into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment runs from three minutes to fifteen. Do you have questions first?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:A year of being ready, and he was not ready, and it turned out you could be both. He knew the procedural summary by heart: *The practitioner stands within the marked circle. No external device is required. Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth, and Kindling is the moment it wakes.* What the waking felt like, nobody had managed to tell him; only that it came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:289:He did not know how much she knew. Kindling results travelled through a neighbourhood like water through a ditch, so she probably knew that his had not gone well, and probably not the word. What she had given him was what he had given her on the step: notice, and nothing asked in return. That evening, under her starred line on page seven, he wrote in small letters: *She was right that it's done. It doesn't feel done.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:253:It came from the place where the sigil had gathered on the morning of his Kindling, small and risen, before it spoke one word and went out. That place had been dark ever since; he had gone looking for it in Torvin's yard on the twenty-eighth night, for an hour, and found only the place where a door should have been. He could feel that it was still dark. Nothing lit. No sigil came up out of it to the edge of his sight. But in the dark, the way words come up on the inside of your eyelids when your eyelids are open, something was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:The day after Kindling was the strangest day of all, because nothing happened in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:The third was the current one, with eleven descriptions of Kindling and two stars and a Stone Path declaration in Joren's round hand near the back. Folded into its back pages was a copy of a registry entry about four people he had never met and was never going to stop thinking about. Into the bag they went in order, the oldest underneath, as you stack a thing you mean to keep adding to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-44.md:39:She talked for a quarter of an hour without stopping, and it was the most anybody had said to him at one time since Hesk on the night before Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:It was not a seam. It was a wall, and it had been built on the second morning after Kindling, and he had not even known it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-31.md:125:He turned to the back of the volume. There were two appendices. The second began on a page that had come loose from the binding and been tucked back in, slightly crooked, by somebody's careful hand. At its head was a subheading he knew. He had copied it in a green book's handwriting at a table under a north window in Denvash, on the second morning after Kindling, and had not seen it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:He told her about the quiet before a thought. He told her about the dark place behind his breastbone where the sigil had gathered at Kindling, and had been dark ever since, and how it had still been dark, and how something had been written in it anyway. He opened the Log to the back, to the page where he had copied it, and turned it round on his knee so that she could read it, and she leaned forward on the bed and read it with her lips moving very slightly, as she read the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:There was a bed, and a press, and a table, and a sill, and he had lived among them for two years and the better part of a third. Seln had given the rule in an orchard: forty pounds for a day, and thirty for thirty days. Cael's came to less than thirty. He folded two shirts and the spare coat, and rolled the strapping the healer had left him, and put Hesk's clock into a stocking; it was a quarter of an hour behind the bell, and losing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:247:The rotation's book was in his coat. It was a thin grey thing, cheaper than any other book he owned, and it held a year and a half of Fifth-days: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure each was meant to buy. Its last line said *All of it.* It was a true record. It was also the only paper on the hill that could turn a house found compliant into a house that had signed figures an enrollee arranged, and Gault's name stood under every one of those figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:Brom leaned forward in the good chair with his forearms on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"I stand in it. Within five weeks, at the district station, with Ilsev seeing to the conduct of it, and she'll see to it properly. I put my hand where I'm shown. It was asked once before, and it took eleven seconds." He did not look at Seln, and he went no nearer to it than that. "We know what it says. Karis told a seat so for fifty-one minutes and nobody contradicted her: *no valid classification.* Then the chapter opens, in its order. One: every enrollment or standing that rests on my being a practitioner is void, and that's this hill. Two: I'm suspended as a practitioner pending a review, and the review is to be begun by the registry at its discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:123:Bracken stood on the landing with a lantern in one hand and a long packet in the other. He was in his coat, and he had all three pairs of glasses about him, two on his forehead and one on his nose, which Cael had only ever seen when he had been reading something more than once. He looked at the bare table, at the five of them, at the lamp in the window, and he did not look at the floor between Seln's feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:189:She took the carter's book out of her coat. He had seen it perhaps three times in the term: stitched, in oilcloth, with a pencil in the spine. She opened it near the back and laid it on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"I've wanted it all year. I thought the thing I wanted lived on that floor, and I've looked for it there every dawn since the first week." She put the book away inside her coat. "Turns out it doesn't live here. It came up the road with me. I landed on a capstan bar in a wool yard two days ago on whichever foot I liked, with nobody holding a card, and it was all there. It's in my feet. They can't keep it in a hall, and they can't revoke it." She looked at the last slip. "But the ladder lives here, and I'm leaving the ladder. I want that written at full price, and I don't want anybody telling me it's cheap because I can still jump."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:"Iron Rank One stays mine," said Lira. "That's a certificate, and it's in my coat, and it was true before any of this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:231:"Eight heads," she said. "I wanted a room with a lock on it my whole life, and I had one for a year, and it was the first room I ever wanted to stay in. I said so to you on its second day." She put her hand on the table's edge, the pale fingers pressed to the wood. "I'm not going to say it doesn't cost. It costs the whole of what I asked for. But I found out this term that I can find a leaf in the dark on my knees in under thirty seconds, with cold hands, and I didn't build that habit for nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:259:In that time Seln lifted the satchel from between his feet onto his knees, under the table's edge, and folded his hands on top of it. Karis put her bibliography back inside the notebook's cover, and left the last slip where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:319:"One question, then, in two parts," he said, and kept his voice low. "That we go, by the clean door, inside the year. And that before we go I give him what he's asked for, in the Crown yard, with the lid off, in front of the school and the town and whichever coats come up to write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:333:Seln sat with both hands folded on the satchel on his knees. He looked at the four hands, and then at the lamp in the window, turned up as high as it would go. He had been in a great many rooms, and had sat at the edge of every one of them for fifteen years, and he had counted five beans onto a gardener's table and called it arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:43:That was what she had been braced for, all the way up the stair. It was a room with a board in it and a row of backs, and it had a door, and she had expected the old cold to come up through her boots, the cold that had lived in her knees since she was a girl. She had stood in a doorway there with a paper in her hand, filled in correctly, in somebody else's writing, and been put on the outside of that door by people who were being perfectly polite.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:55:Rooke came in at the first bell with his board under his arm, as he did every morning of his life, and stopped inside the door. He had looked at her on the bench, and at the key on her knee, and she had seen him understand it; he had very likely understood it at midnight, chalking a board in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:77:It was the same thing. She could see that plainly, and she was not going to tell herself a kinder story. It was a school, and a room with a board, and a girl going out of it for good with her certificate in her coat, and anybody watching from the court would have seen exactly what a porter at Fenmark had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:89:"It had better be." She tucked the book inside her coat. "I'm going down for the horse. Don't let Brom start without a cart to put them in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:He went through it a thing at a time. There were four shirts, and he kept two. He kept the strapping, and the awl, the wax and the cord, and the whetstone in its sock, though it had lain all year on a stack of dockets. He weighed a good coat in his hand, the one he had worn before the panel, and folded it with care and laid it on the chair, and he put the tin of rivets beside it, and a pair of frame gloves, and three books.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:153:Brom buckled the pack's flap. He did it slowly, and when he had done it he sat down on the bed's edge with his forearms on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:207:"Two. I can count." He wiped his palms on his coat. "One more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:211:He came from the chancellor's range with Bracken's senior clerk beside him, and he was just as Cael had seen him from a trough on the north road: stout and fair and in no hurry, with his coat buttoned wrong at the collar. He had the long envelope in his hand still, opened and shut again. He stopped under the arch, by his horse, and looked out at the cart, and at the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:229:Ephram walked beside him, on his left, at the same pace. He had his right arm bound across his chest in the healer's linen and his coat hung from the shoulder, and his left hand was empty; he was carrying nothing whatever. He did not look at the box. He looked straight ahead, as a man walks in a file, and he kept his step to Brom's. He could not have taken an end of it with one arm, and he had not offered. He had only come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:315:At the road's foot the coats had their two lanterns out, though it was not yet dark. There were two men by the milestone, senior, in travelling grey, with a tin cup on the wall between them. They had been waiting since before the year opened for a word to change in a column, and it had changed at a quarter past the first bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:317:The cart came down onto the level and went by them at a walk, and neither man moved. The elder had a walking stick he did not need, and he looked at the horse, and the canvas, and the three shapes under it, and at four people on foot, and at Cael last and longest. There was no ill will in it; he had the face of a man reading the last line of a long ledger. Then he took a book from his coat and a pencil from the book, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:333:He wrote the day's line there, sitting on the press's platen with the book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:3:The rule was older than the wool exchange, and the house's counsel explained it on the First-day morning in the coach, with her bag on her knees and her steel rule lying across the bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:17:From the window of the room he was to share with Brom, Cael could see the green cupola over the roofs, and hear the bell under it. He could see, too, the upper window of the inn across the lane, where a man in a travelling coat sat with a cup and did not pretend to be doing anything but sitting there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:39:Then Withrow reached across the table and shut the code herself, with one hand, quite gently. She drew the single sheet toward her and folded it once and put it inside her coat. She looked up as she did it, and saw him through the pane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:55:He had his coat off and his feet set and his forearms crossed above his head, the right over the left, and the platen rested on them. Lira was at the capstan with both hands on a bar and her heels dug in, walking it round a step at a time. With every step the black timber came down a finger's breadth, and Brom took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:75:His knees gave an inch, and held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:89:He sat down on the pump trough with his forearms on his knees. There were two red bars across them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:113:And there were two boxes roped under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:147:The coat came with him. It was the one from the upper window, the older of the pair, a grey patient man with a walking stick he did not need. He followed at forty yards, in plain sight, down the middle of the street, as Seln had taught four people in an orchard to walk when they wished to be written down as what they were. Cael did not mind it. They had been walking the same roads for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:157:She was about fourteen. She had on a clean brown dress that had been let down at the hem, and good boots, and she sat with her hands in her lap and her knees together and her back very straight, as she had plainly been told to sit. And somebody had done her hair. It was plaited in two braids and the braids were pinned up round her head in a crown, tight and even, with not a strand loose, and a narrow green ribbon was worked through the plait from one side to the other. It was not a style for an ordinary day. It had taken somebody the best part of an hour, early, by a window, with a mouth full of pins.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:159:The mother sat beside her with a basket on her knees. Every little while her hand came up, without her seeming to know it, and touched the braid above the girl's ear, to be sure of a pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:He waited. He did not count, for once. The mother sat with the basket and looked at the door. The coat, forty yards back, had stopped by a garden wall and was looking at a row of beans. He had seen a thousand stations, and this one had nothing in it for his column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:201:He walked back. The coat fell in forty yards behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:233:She stood up from the middle one. She was a spare woman of sixty or so with her grey hair cut short, in a plain coat, and she had her file open on her left forearm at the angle at which he had seen it for a year. She did not come forward. She stood where she had sat, and looked at the seat's clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:Karis ate nothing on the morning of the second closing, and Brom carried her breakfast down the hill in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:In the coach she sat with the wallet on her knees and did not open it. She had the twenty-five slips by heart; he had heard her say them through twice more since the east hall, once to the reading room's window and once, at midnight, to the landing, when she thought the stair was empty. She looked out at the switchbacks going by, and her fingers lay on the leather, quite still, and paler than the rest of her hand, as they would be for the rest of her life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:29:He had counted it once, and the sum had told him what he needed to know. He sat down behind Withrow's chair with the lot number under his boot and looked instead at the things that were different. The whole of Ephram's cohort was on the ninth and tenth benches, in their greys, sitting like people who had been threatened. The lake house's instructor had brought her Blade, who had his lath in its canvas sleeve between his knees. And Rooke was there, who had never come down for a sitting; he stood against the back wall by the doors with his arms folded and his board under one of them, as he stood at the east rail on a meet day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:31:On the fourth bench Lira had her hands under her, as Karis had sat on hers in the Crown yard, and Brom sat beside her with his coat buttoned over four apples. Gault had his case very straight on his knees. The assistant was holding something flat against her chest, and Cael saw the edge of it and knew it for a clean sheet of the house's good paper, which she had brought to write on and would not put a word to. At the end of the bench, by the pillar, Seln sat as he had sat last week, with his hands in his lap, looking at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:111:"And the fourth," said Karis, and her little finger went down. "I asked. I asked the petitioner what evidence would defeat his petition, and a reading in the frame is evidence, and he told me there was none. He did not say, *let us see what the frame returns.* He is too honest a man to say so, and in thirteen sittings he never has. Nobody has. No officer, no clerk, no witness has stood in this room and told the seat that the frame will return a classification. It was asked once before, at a district station, when he was fourteen, and it took eleven seconds to answer, and its answer is the word on his file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:"So this is the remedy," said Karis. "Stand a man on that scale, write down that it returned nothing, and then apply to him the chapter of the code that opens by supposing an evaluation under the standard protocol which *returns no valid classification.*" She gave the four words their full length. "I have read the fourteenth chapter a great many times this year. It is a careful piece of drafting. It was written for the case in which the registry tried, in good faith, to find out what a person was, and could not, and it gives the registry something to do in that case, so that the person is not left in no column at all. It is harsh, and I think it was meant as a kindness."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:153:"The petitioner called it the best-documented enrollment he had reviewed in thirty years, and I believe him. If the remedy is granted, the registry will strike that record. In its place it will enter three words: *no valid classification.* It will exchange the fullest true entry in its books for an empty one, and it will do that under sections whose only purpose is that entries be true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:207:The evaluator nearest the records table looked at Ilsev. Ilsev did not look at him. She was looking at the clerk, with her file shut on her knee, and her face told Cael nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. The refusal was the Archmarshal's; the form's old column had only Gault's title to put it under. Gault sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:237:She did not cry, though he had wondered whether she would. She slept with her mouth a little open and the wallet sliding off her knees, and Brom put out one hand and held it there all the way up the switchbacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:249:"It wasn't for the seat," he said. "I thought it was, all year. It's in the minute now. Every word she said is there under her own name, in the registry's own presses, for as long as there's a registry, and somebody will read it. Some girl in a reading room with ink on her collar, looking for something else." He watched Karis's pale fingers, open on her knee. "And it was for her. She wanted to do the thing properly, once, with everybody looking. She's done it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:255:Bracken, in the corner, had the seal on his knees and his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:21:"Then you should have the whole of it," said Withrow, "since you have brought me yours." She took a folded sheet from inside her coat and opened it on the blotter and turned it toward him. It was not the house's paper. There were four lines on it in the small capitals of the house's counsel, and each had a day written against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:119:Brom's lamp was lit. He was sitting on the edge of his bed in his shirt with his forearms on his knees, and on the table under the window lay two papers. On the left was the district's finding, folded along its folds, with the printed slip tucked into it; on the right, a hand's breadth off, lay the envelope with the red seal, as he had squared it four weeks ago, and its seal had not so much as a crack across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:He had not waited for the porter, or for morning. Somebody had come to his door at midnight and told him, and he had put on his coat and gone down to the yard in the dark and chalked it himself, before any officer of the house could wake up and wonder whether it was wise. She watched until he climbed down and held the lantern up to read it over, and then rubbed out one word with his cuff and wrote it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:207:Cael watched it from the north wall, because there was nothing else he could usefully do. The ferry came across low in the water and went back empty and came again. The bluff road had people on every switchback: wool-factors in their good coats, and carters, and a file of district clerks who had found that they had business on the hill. The man who had bought a second press was there, and so was the woman who kept the guesting-house, with a basket. The lake house's blue-grey wagon had not gone home after the ruling, and it came up at a walk with a lath in a canvas sleeve sticking out of the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:211:Two men in travelling coats came up among the rest, and waited their turn at the trestle like anybody, and took the pen, and wrote. Cael was too far off to see what they put. They did not look up at the wall, and one of them had a walking stick he did not need.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:213:He had no line to read at his chalk today, and it was strange. For a year and more he had read one before every bout: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure it was meant to buy. He had the rotation's book in his coat from habit, and at the north wall he took it out and opened it at the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:229:He found his own people without looking for them. They were on the east tier, low down: Brom, with his arms folded, and Karis beside him with her hands under her, and Lira between the Current girl and the Stone. The assistant sat behind them, and behind her were two benches of Ephram's cohort, who had been told nothing about cheering today. At the end of the stand, by the porter's boards, where the year's names stood in their columns, a man in a dark coat stood with his hands in his pockets, and nobody would have looked at him twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:231:High on the west tier, at the very top, sat a grey man with a walking stick across his knees and a pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:263:He did not choose to. That was the shame of it, and he felt it even as it happened. A year of Fifth-days in wing three had put the waiting into his body as a drill puts a landing into a foot. Twelve hundred people were looking down, and a grey man with a pencil, and the coat was on him before he had so much as thought of it. He stood in his own bout and waited, so that it would look as if he had found the answer when a good Iron would have found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:293:Cael stood on the south chalk with the sun on his back, and he could feel every one of them above him. He had felt eleven hundred empty seats on his neck at dawn in his first week, and it was nothing to this. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair, and Gault had signed two years of his figures, and a coat held a pencil at the top of the west tier. And there were four hundred people he had eaten beside since he came up the road, who knew him as a quiet enrollee with a provision and a decent read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:299:He did not take the coat off. He let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:65:Havel stood a moment with the key in his hand and looked at his own pins, and the street went by: a carter, and two women with a basket between them, and on the far side a man in a travelling coat with a walking stick, who glanced at the board as he glanced at everything and wrote nothing down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:71:The senior evaluator was still in the long room, which he had not expected. She sat in the middle chair below the covered platform with her file shut on her knee, quite alone, looking at nothing, like any woman at the end of a day's work. As he came down the side of the benches her right hand went to the breast of her coat and rested there, over the cloth, and came away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:75:But it was not the same now, and he made himself see why. She had not taken out the sheet in her coat, which was hers and would stay hers. She had taken out the returns, which were the registry's, and stood up in the registry's own room, and read them into the registry's own minute at a pace a clerk could follow. She had found the one door her clearance left open and had gone through it in the plainest way there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:121:The bridge-ward had lit his lantern. Havel sat at the window with his aching hand open on his knee and watched it, and thought, a little, about the young man at the far table, who had sat up straight for a year. He did not know what a ruling would do to him. He had never known what any of it would do; that had not been his to know. He hoped, sitting there, that the registers would be kind to him. He knew of no register that entered kindness, and he hoped it all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:131:Cael watched them go, because he could not go with them. He was a respondent on a call day, and though the district hall was well within the bell, a respondent did not walk into another proceeding's room on the morning of his own ruling; the house's counsel had said so, and then had said that she was sorry. So he stood with the registrar and saw a broad back in a good coat and a narrow one beside it go down a street. Brom walked as he walked to a frame, and Lira had her hands in her pockets and was, for once, not talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:139:It was the longest hour of the morning. He spent it at the trough by the exchange's corner, counting the people on the steps as they came, and stopped at ninety. Bracken went in to see the seat's clerk about the house's copy and came out again, and the older coat stood across the street under an awning with his stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:141:Then Lira came round the corner at something that was nearly a run, and stopped herself, and walked, and Brom came after her at his own pace. He looked just as he had looked going in, with his coat buttoned, except that there were two red bars across his forearms where the cuffs had ridden up, darker than the wool press had left them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:"*The seat, having heard the petition and the responses in full, and having found the enrollment lawful in its execution and the respondent institution compliant with its charter throughout — which findings stand undisturbed — nonetheless finds the evaluation-integrity question sustained on the merits. The demonstration provision describes the practitioner; it does not evaluate him within the meaning of the integrity provisions. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol is granted. The re-evaluation shall be conducted at the district Arbiter station within five weeks. Should the re-evaluation return no valid classification, the procedure of the code's chapter fourteen shall apply: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. The demonstration-provision enrollment is revoked effective at the close of the present academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:207:He came to Withrow first. He stood in front of her, a man of middle height in a coat with mended cuffs, and bowed, not to the registry's depth but a little past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:Then he looked at Cael, and Cael looked back and did not send the read. He had sat across from this man at a conference table in the year's fourth week and found nobody in the coat, and he knew why now. There had been nobody to find. Jent had been sent with an honest question, and had asked it honestly, and had been given the answer the code gave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:233:Bracken changed the slate before he took his coat off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:267:Nobody had asked him to. He had come in behind them and stood about for a little with his coat on, a man who had held a frame that morning and had nothing to do with his arms. Then he had gone to the table's end, where the finished leaves were being laid in threes, and had taken up each three as it came, and knocked its edges true on the wood, long side and short side, and laid it on the pile with its corners exactly over the corners beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:Lira was on the stair. It was the narrow stair behind the counter, the one that went up to Bracken's rooms, and she sat on its fourth step with her knees drawn up and her chin on them, in the shadow above the lamps. She had not said a word since the coach, and she was watching Brom's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:5:The desk clerk opened the assessment wing on the First-day, as he had opened it every working morning for nine years. He hung up his coat. He took the cover off his ledger and sharpened a pencil, and he had the day book open and the date written before he looked round to say good morning to the copying table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:115:He took his coat from its peg. He put the strap of the post satchel over his head, so that the weight lay against his hip, and he went out by the wing's side door and drew it shut until the latch dropped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:For two years he had fetched the house's pouch across that bridge, at the end of a day, with the same satchel on the same shoulder. He nodded to the ward's lantern and was nodded to, and went over the middle arch above the brown water, and nobody would think of it again. The two coats were at the bluff road's foot and the two at the landing, where they had been set to stand, and they had not been set to watch a clerk go for the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:141:*A thing kept is a wall.* An old woman with a stick had taught him that, over a chandler's, with a cat on her knee. *A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.* He had said those lines on nineteen first mornings. He had kept the case shut for twelve years and it had been a very good wall: it had stood between him and every person he was ever sent to watch, and he had sat behind it and written true things, and been safe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:191:It took him the rest of the day, and that surprised him. He had supposed that a man gathered sticks for an hour and had done. He had not built a fire out of doors since he was a boy on a quay, and in nineteen postings every hearth he sat at had been lit by somebody whose work it was. He went along the brake and found that most of what lay under it was rotten, and that what was sound was green. He went up the north lane as far as a fallen ash and broke off what he could with his hands and his boot. He carried it back in his arms, four journeys, and each time he came round the thorn he looked at the heap and thought it small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:199:He broke the ash across his knee and laid it small to large, as a quay-man had shown him forty years ago. He was absurdly careful about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:203:On a Seventh-day he wrote a return. He had not written this week's; he had been at a stove. But the pattern was fifteen years deep and did not know that it had been retired, and while his hands laid sticks it composed the thing by itself, in the voice he kept for the audit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:205:He took a pencil and a leaf from his coat. He wrote it down, to be rid of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:He read it over. Every word was true. It was the last of its kind, and it was as good as any of them; a reader could have checked each line against a book and found it stood, and he would have learned nothing about a boy who had let a coat fall in front of twelve hundred people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:215:He did not fold it in the old way. There was no outside fold to put a reference on, and no bag, and no drawer at the end of any road he meant to walk. He twisted the leaf into a spill instead, and put it under the smallest sticks, where the heap wanted something dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:225:It caught. He watched his last true sentence go brown and curl, and the small sticks take from it. Then he stood up from the fire and walked out round the end of the thorn into the middle of the road, in what light there was, with his hands where they could be seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:7:Cael did not go to meet him. He stood on the north chalk, with his hip aching from the crest to the knee and seven bursts gone out of a day that gave six, and he went down the rule as he had gone down it on his knees in a chalk road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Ephram went down on one knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:115:*One.* He tried to get down beside him and his knees bent a beat after he asked. *Two.* "Healer," Rooke was saying, in the same voice in which he had called the touch, and a spare grey woman was already off her bench and coming along the stand with her bag. *Three.* Somebody very large had come over the rim wall without troubling about the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:117:"Don't move it," said Brom. He was on his knees on the oak with one hand between Ephram's shoulder blades. "I've had one out. It goes back. It goes back worse if you help."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:He said it through his teeth, with his eyes shut, and Cael, slow, kneeling at last, thought that he had not heard right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:143:He had felt it there all through the fourth, in the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, patient as a clerk. He looked at it now, on his knees on the oak, with the yard coming apart into noise above him and the healer winding linen round a captain's arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:194:At the top of the west tier the seat was empty. The grey man had gone, with his stick, to catch a bag. And at the end of the stand, by the porter's boards, there was nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:234:He came down the steps slowly, with his right arm bound across his chest in a sling of the healer's linen and his coat hung over the shoulder. Rooke's clerk was waiting by the boards with the chalk and the bout sheet, and Cael got up off his step and went along the stand to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:254:"You always are." Ephram took his hand back and hitched the coat on his shoulder with it. "Don't make me sound clever in it. I walked into a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:260:He wrote it at the rim, sitting on the coping with his feet over the oak. He wrote in pencil, as Karis had learned to, with the book on his knee and the bound arm steadying the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:270:*Left forearm, wrist to elbow: the touch, and four more on the guard, the last to the bone or near it. Hip, crest to knee: ten on the oak, four past the six. Shoulder: six pushes at load, one past Norhold. Two lanes, ten and ten. Behind the eyes. One spark. Not the quiet thing; in a yard with twelve hundred people looking down it had nothing to offer and didn't. Not the one from the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:15:Cael found him there afterward, crouched beside the brown cob, with its near fore foot between his knees and his awl in his teeth. The grey rider was nowhere to be seen, having taken his supper to the loft, and the cob stood for it patiently. A shoe had been clicking on that foot since the second ford. Cael had heard it behind them all afternoon, a small loose sound, and had thought nothing of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:23:"The horse isn't." Brom wiped his hands on his coat. "The horse is only walking where he's pointed. I'll not listen to that for two more days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:141:He looked at each of them in turn, as the elder coat had done at the road's foot, and at Seln a little longer than the rest, and his face did not change. It was a plain weathered face. He had stood by a milestone with a tin cup through a recess and eleven sittings, and he had ridden four days at their backs without once pretending to be anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:361:It was the first night of his life outside any jurisdiction, and he lay under a cart and waited to see what that was like. It was wet, and his arm ached. Lira, on watch, was telling the gelding that he had been perfectly right about the hill. Karis had her back against a box and her knees drawn up. Nobody on the continent was required to write a line about any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:407:They put the cart across. It was not the brown river over again, for the water was nowhere above the knee; but the bed was all loose stone, and the bank on the far side was broken, and it took them the better part of an hour. The near wheel went down between two rocks and would not come out, and Brom got under the hub with his back and lifted, without any grey in his arms at all, while Seln and Karis dragged a flat stone under the rim. Lira said a number of things to the gelding. The man on the far bank finished what was in his cloth, and watched, and offered no opinion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:417:Cael had the letter inside his coat, dry, and he took it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:439:He sat down on the bank with Hesk's book on his knee. It was his log, where he set down what he carried and what he owed. One promise had gone west that minute in a mule's bag, and it was answered, not paid. The other was older, and nobody had been written to about it at all. He put the two side by side on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:453:Then he stopped, and it was not the stopping of a clock. His head was down between his knees and his sides went in and out like a smith's bellows, and there was a rattle in his breath that had not been there on the upland. Lira had her hand on his neck, and she did not say one word to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:33:She got up by herself, on the right hand and both knees, as she climbed a ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:He shut the book, and put it back inside his coat, against the strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:89:He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:95:He took the book out of his coat, and held it toward her, shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:105:It was an odd thing to feel a weight leave his fingers that he had not been asked for. She did not move for a breath. Then she shut her hand on it, and held it against her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:113:He could not dispute that, and he did not try. She had the book open before he had turned to go on; she walked the next mile with her nose a hand from a dead man's pencil, and went into a rut to the knee, and did not appear to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:117:His own hand went to his coat that evening, on watch, and found a strap, and the corner of a lock, and nothing beside them. He let it lie there. He had thought that he might want the thing back by nightfall, as a man wants a tooth that has been drawn. He left it where he had put it, that night and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:The summary came to the Archmarshal's desk in the ordinary bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:"Enter it as returned," said the Archmarshal. "That is what has occurred. If the seat wishes to know what the office observes, it will see that the office has not said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:107:The clerk looked at him a moment longer. Then he put the leaf with the rest and went down the too-steep stair, and the Archmarshal listened to him go, every step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:115:Cael had not slept much. The parlour had a settle and a floor, and he had taken the floor, and the bills had come in during the night, as the healer had said they would. His hip had stiffened from the crest to the knee, and his shoulder would not lift past the level of his ear. The forearm had gone the colour she had promised, a dark plum from the wrist to the elbow with five darker bars across it, and it beat in time with his heart whenever he let it hang. Behind his eyes there was a heaviness that had nothing to do with any lane; it was only what was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:181:A man had come round the bend of the road behind them, on a brown cob, perhaps three hundred yards back. He wore a grey travelling coat, and he was not hiding. He rode at a walk in the middle of the road, with his reins slack, and when the cart stopped he stopped as well and let the horse put its head down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:183:"One," said Brom. He had counted before he spoke, and now he counted again. "The younger of the two from the road foot. Not the one with the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:217:He stood quite still, in what was left of the light, with his hands a little out from his sides and open. He had his coat on, and a satchel across him. He had stopped just where a person on the road would see the whole of him at once, and he was plainly waiting until they had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:251:Brom did not sit at once. He stood at the fire's edge and looked round the circle, slowly, and Cael watched him do it and could see the large head mark each of them as it turned. Lira had her boots off already. Karis sat very upright. A man in a dark coat had his hands round a cup he had not yet turned. Then Brom looked at Cael, and last, briefly, down at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. There has not been a First-day like it in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:291:Cael ate at noon. It was not much, a heel of the guesting-house bread and a piece of cheese, but it stayed where he put it, and afterward he wanted more. Brom, who had been watching him with no attempt to hide it, took a second piece of cheese out of his own coat and held it out without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:315:Brom was already on his knees at the wheel. He measured the axle against his own thigh, and then he waded in, boots and all, to the middle, and stood where the ripple was, with the water at his hip. He waded back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:327:She went. She held the chain in both hands with her coat kilted up, and she did not look back until she stood dripping on the far bank. Seln crossed behind her without touching the chain at all, and stood a little way up the slope, where he could see the road in both directions.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:343:The wheel climbed, and the bed came clear. Brom let it down as he let down a bar into its catches, without a sound, and followed it up the far bank with the river running out of his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:41:He did the other thing, which he had done in a room under a floor four days before without being asked. He stepped up beside her, and turned his left side to the wall, and bent his back, with his one good hand on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:It opened as a trap-door opens. The plate under Brom's boots went up at its inner edge, toward the hole, with a roar of water; and Cael saw Brom do the only thing that was in him to do. He did not jump. He straightened his back. He drove up from his heels with the whole of his strength, and Karis went off his shoulders like a sack off a hoist, and landed on her chest across the top of the wall with her legs kicking. Seln had her by the coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:He had been thrown toward the gap, and he had gone on as he was pointed, wading, with the water flying from his knees. The wall beside the gap, on its right hand, was the lowest in the room; it was broken down to the height of his waist. He threw himself across it, chest first, and dragged his legs up after him, and lay there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:111:Karis had turned herself about on the wall-top. She was on her knees, facing outward, with her boots toward the hole; and then she lay down along the stone on her stomach, and put her head and both arms over the inner edge, and hung there. She had a sheet spread on the cut face below her, upside down, with her left forearm across it. Her right hand began to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:It was the only thing that was said about it. Cael heard the pencil going, steadily, on thin paper against stone. He heard Karis say *sheet*, in a voice that had the blood in it, and Lira let go of the belt, and tore one from the roll with her teeth, and passed it down, and took hold again. A finished sheet came up over the edge and was pinned under a pebble. The two of them moved a yard along the wall on their knees, and Karis went over once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:129:It lay a little lower than Brom's knee, and to reach it he must let himself down the end of a wet wall with one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:153:Through the gap Cael could see a boot, and a length of grey coat, lying in a foot of water. The boot moved. It did not move far. Brom had his face out of it, and that was all that anybody on a wall could tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:She did not walk and she did not run. She skipped. She went from plate to plate with her arms out and her coat flapping, a short quick hop to each, and never the same plate twice, and never two counts in one place; a square woman in a dead man's coat, coming across thirty yards of broken floor like a child on stepping-stones. It would have been comical on any other morning of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:Cael did the sum, since it was the only thing left to him that was quick. Brom lay two strides from the sill. She had fifteen yards still to cross, hopping. The thing had been at a stone in eleven counts from a third of the dish away, and it had only the width of a room to go. And when she reached him she would stop, since a healer who has come to a fallen man gets down on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:181:There was Anchor, which he had set for the first time yesterday, on his knees, on a kerb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:205:He had his knees a little bent and his weight on his heels, as a fighter on a circuit had once stood in front of him and let him strike. The edge of the slab came up against his shins, and behind it the head, with the whole weight and haste of the thing below. It struck where the weight was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:229:He was already moving, since he had not yet begun to pay. He threw himself back across the two strides of deep water, and got his chest onto the ledge, and his knees; and behind him the whole of the hole stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:305:They came off the wall on the side of the gap, one at a time, and did not stand. Seln had Cael's arm across his shoulders. Lira carried the roll, and Karis had her finished sheets held against her chest in both arms, with her face scraped raw along one cheek where the stone had had it. They went along the ledge, and over the sill, where the healer got up from her knees with Brom's weight on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:319:She took it. Brom had not waited. He was three strides out along the grey path already, with the healer's shoulder under his left arm and his coat black from the shoulder to the belt. He had turned his head to look at the roll of paper, and at what Karis held against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:41:He did not ask anybody. He went round the foot of the block, where the pine had been dropping dead limbs for longer than there had been a road, and gathered them up, and broke them across his knee and laid them small to large. He was quicker at it than he had been four days ago, and he had the fire's place chosen, in the lee of the stone, before the gelding was out of the shafts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:They all looked at Seln. He straightened up from the fire with a stick in each hand, and considered the flat, and the pine, and the track going up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:"You made us say it back to you. All of it. You made me walk a farm track in the dark with my hand on Brom's coat and count people by touch, and you said if a thing's worth teaching it's worth trusting." She pointed a stick at him. "So either you taught us right, and you can sleep, or you didn't, and you'd better say so now, because I've been doing it your way for five nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:271:*I can work within the system. The system was never designed to include me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:329:He had not yet lain down. He was sitting by the fire with his coat round him and his cup in his hands, and Lira and Karis were awake as well, since nobody had found the way to sleep on the first night of a place. Brom had come back from the lip to warm his hands. So all five of them were there, and the pine was burning with its high clear flame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:339:Cael had the book shut on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:349:"Let them escalate," said Lira. She had her feet to the fire and her chin on her knees. "They'll need better maps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:355:Cael looked at the two of them, and at a researcher with her pale fingers round a tin cup, and at a mild man in a dark coat who had sat at the edge of other people's tables for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:27:"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:175:He moved to the next on his knees, and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:197:Seln had not got up from his knees. He was looking along the bars, away into the dark, as he looked through a paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:The woman had not waited to be made known to anybody. She was sixty, or near it, and as lean as a rake. Her grey hair was knotted up under a cap, and the satchel on her hip had been mended more often than Teague's coat. She had gone straight past the fire to the blanket where Brom lay, and she stood over him with her head on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:There was no print over theirs but his own crew's. He had gone back along it himself at each dusk, a mile, to be sure. He had lain on his face on a knoll for an hour on the second night, with the cold coming up through his coat, and watched the way that they had come. Nothing moved on it. Nobody was behind them, and nobody had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:147:It was dusk before he went himself. The boy was sitting alone at the near fire, against a scaled stake, with a book shut on his knee. Teague had stood over a great many men in his time, and it had got him the answers that men give to somebody who is standing over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:149:He bent his knees, and sat down on the turf beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:155:Nobody had done that to him for a long while. A big boy had done it once, on a stone floor, with a circuit's crowd shouting over them. He had won a bout. He had crossed the floor to the loser without any hurry, and had lowered himself, and sat with his forearms on his knees, so that his head should be no higher. Teague sat exactly so. He was ten lines up a sailcloth sheet, and he had put himself down on the cold turf beside a boy, so near that their shoulders were a foot apart, and he was looking at the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:163:Teague's right arm lay along his knee, with the band of black leather laced on the wrist. It was a foot from Cael's left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:167:He did not put out his hand. It lay on his own knee, where it was, and he looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:193:Teague sat on with his forearms on his knees. The fire ticked. Down by the blankets an old woman was telling a very big man what he might do with his left hand, and it was not much. He put it no second time, and no other way. Cael had waited all through that silence for the man's eyes to go to his hands, as a magister's had gone, and a warden's; and they did not go there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:Cael opened Hesk's book against his knee, and wrote while it was still in him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:11:So there would be one leaf in each box, and the first of them in her coat, with a leaf of trials that had a struck numeral on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:39:Seln sat down on his heels at her elbow. He had the limp little book out of his coat already, open at the first page, and he held it to her fire at the height of her eyes, with his thumb beside the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:55:He went back to the mules, with the book in his coat. She did not thank him. He would have disliked it, and she had the three figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:189:She fetched the rafter herself, and broke it across her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:7:"Down. On the left side. Somebody get that coat off him, and somebody light me a fire, and I don't care which of you does which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:The coat came away from Brom's shoulder with a sound that he did not care for, and under it the binding hung in a loop, and the linen beneath was black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:Lira held nothing; she sat on the grey stone with the bow across her knee, and looked from the healer's hands to the reeds, and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:33:Brom said it to the sky. He was lying on his left side with a coat over him, grey to the lips, and he said it as he might have repeated the weight of a load that he had been told he must not lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:She had done with Karis's cheek, which wanted only washing, and she had Brom's coat on over her own. She was grey, and she stood and looked down at him for some time, as she looked along a bench, and he waited to be told which of his hurts was his own doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:81:He lay down on the turf where he was. She came the stride and a half and got down on her knees, and he felt two fingers on the inside of his wrist and two under his jaw, as cold as the iron behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:133:It was the middle of the afternoon when he saw the other thing, and he saw it only because Brom was making a face at him. Brom lay on his back by the near fire, under two coats, with his right arm strapped to him in every strip of linen that the pannier held. He had his left forefinger laid across his lips, and with his eyes he was pointing at what sat beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:213:When he raised his head, she was sitting on the far side of the dead fire with her arms round her knees, watching him. She had her own coat on again, and he could not tell how long she had been awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:63:Seln went a quarter of a mile in front, where he could be seen against the sky. Karis walked behind him with the hide under her arm, and at every halt she unrolled it on her knee, and laid the brass along it, and looked from the skin to the country and back again. Lira came next, on the whole of her foot, and a road gone soft was no better for her than a road gone hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:109:Seln had his hand flat on the breast of his coat, where a surveyor's book lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:151:Seln had gone down on one knee. He looked at the scale, and at the way the turf had closed about the foot; then he got up, and walked ten yards along the reeds on either side of the ruts with his eyes on the ground, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:"Bent. Over, about the height of a knee, with its head lower than the bend. Like a nail that's been struck from underneath."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:193:"We learned what the Fallow did to each of us before we walked a furlong into it," she said. "I should like to know what this one does before I kneel down in it with my back turned."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:229:Karis sat down on the grey with her knees drawn up, and set Hesk's clock beside her on a folded stocking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:235:He had carried four stones in from the track's end, in his coat, on her orders. He took out one as big as a loaf, and lobbed it left-handed, underarm, ten yards out. It went in with a clap and lay on a plate under a foot of water, grey on grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:243:The plate tipped. Cael saw only two things: a darkness as broad as a door rising at the plate's edge, and the two halves of it parting like the shells of a mussel. Then the stone was not there. The plate fell back with a slap that wetted the six of them to the knee, and the water rocked, and was clear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:303:The water was as cold as the trough at Thornwater. He reached down through a foot of it and laid his palm on the nearest plate, which was smooth, and furred a little with silt. He knew what he was about to make. He had been inside two of them, and had felt one against the front of his coat in a chalk cut: a thing set against a surface, and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:335:He was on his hands and knees, shaking, and Brom's left fist was in his collar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:371:Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:3:Karis asked for the room before she had taken off her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:She took the purse out of her coat. Cael had seen that woman pay for a cart, and a boot, and a kid; each time she had opened her notebook first, and done the sum, and done it again beneath. She did not open it now. She put her fingers into the neck of the bag and laid six marks on the board, one upon another, with her eyes on the door behind the chimney.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:105:She did her sum as she climbed, since she did it every morning, and it came out as she had given it to five people at a heap of stones. There was a bench at the mill that she had left a day short, and two gates beyond it that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:155:"You said it to a woman lying on a hurdle. I never agreed to it." The old woman rapped the knee with her knuckles. "I rested it ten days, as I was bid. Then I came over to my daughter's, as I do, and I came on my own two legs, to find out whether the thing was yours or mine. It's a day and a half. I did it in two. I'd one of the boys with me to carry the bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:"On a stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:201:The small one had been in a loft when Oryn said that an old woman's knee did not care what was under a floor. She was a person who liked to know how a thing came out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:225:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:243:He had the strip from Denvash in his coat. It was cold from the back room, and it took the firelight all along one edge. He set it down on the skin beyond hers, with its end on the ring by the marsh, and slid it up until the two lay in one length; and he bent, and looked for daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:253:She had not written it. She said it once, to the board, in the small exact voice; and nobody at that fire answered her. Cael counted four seconds by the tick of the wood. Lira put a stick on. Brom was looking at the sixth bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:104:**Who knows the cache/falsification history:** the five travelers plus Vastin. Vastin does not know the integration mechanism. Ephram, Withrow, Bracken, Ilsev, Havel, Rooke, Gault, Jent and the public know neither. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit N6]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:118:| Vastin | Inside; not an ally; writing nothing | One window only (M6) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:170:| Archmarshal Vastin | Inside, writing nothing | One three-sentence window (M6) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:220:- **Roles (all unnamed):** the courier (about fifty, a split lip long healed, a hat cut away on the right, a hatchet in a saddle loop); the three carts' people, who are one group throughout: the old carter with two mules, who takes the shade of the ash; the boy of about fifteen; the hide-woman, who is also the wash's hirer; the ferryman and his boy; the standing-money boy; the smith; the landing broker and his clerk; the old man of the north-water steading, his white dog, his goat; the Thornwater carter (short, thick, twenty-two years on his road); the steading's woman (broad, a grey braid); the bowman; the north-road woman captain (a red cloth round her hair; two men and a girl); the eleven-bale captain (a new coat).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:227:- **The carter's map (flag):** a scraped calfskin in oak-gall ink and burnt stick: roads as double lines, fords as three wavy strokes, holds as a square with a dot, Lowmarch as a ring with a bar, days as ticks, and bare hide wherever a wheel has no business. Given to Karis for the wheel Brom rebuilt. Five roads run off its edges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:228:- **Teague, as seen (flag):** forty or so, middle height, heavy in the forearm, cropped hair grey over the ears; a black leather band laced on the right wrist; a hand-axe in his belt; a coat mended by somebody else. His week on the cold road is salt on mules to the top holds before frost shuts the pass, along two miles of ledge; he has done it nine times. He thinks only "the spear", "the bow" (a bad leg), "the boy". One other crew on the sheet could clear the ford and is up the north road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:240:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One, the certificate in her coat. Uninjured (palms skinned at the wash on day 33, healed by day 41). One burst went off by itself on the gravel bar in the first week of drills; none since. She can stop dead from a full run. She wears a long knife bought at Lowmarch's smith. She has been noticed, once, by Thornwater's bowman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:301:- **Injuries, as fixed on the page:** Brom, left forearm, two furrows from wrist to mid-arm, to the bone, fourteen stitches; opened again at the wrist on day 59 (a hand put out to steady the cart in a wet rut), seen at the fire that night; stitches out on day 64; closed clean and re-wrapped on day 66. Lira, right ankle, the ligaments, bound flat to a barrel-stave; a stick from the bowman on day 56, left against the inn wall on day 64. Karis, two cuts across each palm; bound fists, then strips; a pencil on day 68. Cael, left calf, in at the outside and out at the inside; eleven strides, then twenty (day 62), then "a leg on level ground" and nine uphill (day 69).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:313:**Calendar.** The evening of the **sixty-ninth day past the Line**, under the lower inn's eave at Lowmarch. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the thaw" in three places; "frost" once). Days on the page: 51, 52, 53, 54, 55–57 (the stick on the 56th; the carter back on the 57th), 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, 66, 68, 69. Told, not shown: the dusk of the 49th at the ford; the 50th. Weather: dry roads; mist on the river at first light; cold coming up out of the wash after dark. The next movement opens on the morning of day 70.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:319:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. Right ankle: ligaments, never seen by a healer, bound eleven days; no stick since day 64; runs on the gravel bar from day 65; "it bends less, and later". No burst at the ford or since. She killed one hound with the long knife. Her question stands unanswered in Cael's log. She has been told at Thornwater's gate that a healer would have had the ankle straight in an hour, and that there is none; she asked for nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:327:**Objects and money.** Three boxes over the ferry drum. The reserve unopened. Board purse: **sixty-two marks earned** (18 + ford 40 + well 4); **fifty-two in hand** (one mark on flour and bacon on day 51; one to the hold for linen and thread; coppers unitemised). Hesk's satchel holds the log (with the finding and the seal entry), the old volume, the observation notebook, Pike's two lines on bark, and Hesk's letter. Cael's coat has lost its left sleeve. The ash stick is propping an old carter's shaft under the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:344:- Equipment details: three boxes are back over the ferry drum; the reserve remains sealed; Cael's coat lacks its left sleeve; Brom still owns the hedging bill and the older ash wedge; the borrowed ash walking stick has been left and repurposed under a carter's shaft (`manuscript/chapter-11.md:231`; `manuscript/chapter-14.md:345-359`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:144`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:248`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:360:- **Objects:** the hired cart and its load are lost (the ferryman's rope, blankets, kettle, canvas, flour); the smith's six is the only priced debt and the rope's replacement is open and unpriced. The three boxes are recovered; Brom's hedging bill and his older ash wedge survive. Cael's coat has lost both sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:375:- **Shale-backs, as seen (flag):** as long as a cart and broader; four legs like tree-stumps; back and flanks roofed with overlapping horn plates, keeled, the largest along the spine, lichen growing on the old ones; a small head carried at knee height, ending in a beak like a tortoise's, with which they scrape lichen and moss off rock; they shed plates (grey, the size of a shield, built in leaves like a hoof). Half-grown calves have paler plates and keep to the inside. They mow a shelf in a line, a length and a half apart, and the line moves about forty yards an hour without any animal being seen to walk. Sounds: rasping beaks, and plates clattering "as a slate roof settles".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:382:- **Oryn, as seen (flag):** not tall, square in the shoulder, broad in the hand, hair cropped as short as a boy's; a man's brown coat made for a bigger person and taken in ("off a peg that nobody was using any more", given by a hold in her first year); a low hoarse voice; a small brown mule with a pack-saddle, two panniers and a crossbow in an oiled cover lashed on top. She stops a stride and a half from people. Cael puts her at about ten years older than himself; no age is stated. Her right heel is rubbed raw and taped, and has been for two rounds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:386:- **Oryn's route, as told (flag):** seven holds, twenty days, "thirty-odd" rounds; late eleven times and this the twelfth, five days behind; nobody has put a coin in her hand past the Line; holds feed her and the mule, one shoes the mule, one gave the coat. She began with four holds and added the fifth in her third round, the sixth "for an old woman's sake" and the seventh because of a child. In a city she was one of six on a healing house's staff, where the morning list is kept by tier. No city is named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:388:- **Oxhollow (flag):** on the north bank of a broad shallow river, in a hollow, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate on the south side toward the ford and another on the north; a ford of a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of water; a yoke of red oxen with a chain, for drawing carts through; the keeper's low stone house with a turf roof between the gate and the water; a long house; a well; the healer's table of scrubbed ash on two trestles under a lean-to against the gate-house, with a bench along the wall. The keeper is old, bent, with a notched hazel tally-stick, and cannot read; he keeps his coin under a hearthstone. He would have sent three carts over the top at the next dry spell with a Force walking behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:393:*Updated after repair r1 (see `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md`) and the coordinator's ten audit fixes. Facts changed: Teague knows only that the healer was late, not why, and cannot set his lost day against the five; Brom's older ash wedge was not lost and lies with the three boxes; Oryn's sum now reads whole on the 99th, the long reading on the 100th at the earliest, four days of work at Thornwater's table beyond her stop and one day put on the route; Seln asks leave to go ahead and leaves scratched pebbles for word, and finds a clawless footmark at the first seep; Oryn washes her hands before she kneels, and checks her hands on Brom's wrist in the middle of the first reading; the ankle is asked for from the mule's back on the morning of the 82nd; chapter 18 has three scenes. Word counts, metrics, line numbers and flags elsewhere in this report are the pre-repair figures; the movement now stands at 41,346 words.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:407:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. No Path lifted. Unmarked. He asked leave on the 73rd to go ahead each day and find the sleeping place, and left scratched pebbles for word; he found every one, noted the pale rock, crossed the live slide for water, walked the lip twice and marked forty-four stones, and went down for help on the 80th, coming back alone ahead of the healer. He lent Karis his coat from the 78th. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.** He gave reports unasked on the 72nd, the 75th, the 79th and the 80th, since each bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:432:- The cart, ferryman's rope, blankets, kettle, canvas, and flour are in the gorge; the rope's replacement obligation has no price and must not silently alter the 116-in-hand / six-owed account (`manuscript/chapter-19.md:179-189`; `manuscript/chapter-20.md:35-37`; `manuscript/chapter-22.md:325-331`). The ash wedge survives with the boxes after fix 10. Cael's coat has no sleeves (`manuscript/chapter-20.md:17-23`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:479:- **The floor (flag):** on the crown of a low swell, twenty-six of Cael's strides across by two diameters; colder than the turf; soundless under a boot; its rim rolled over like the rim of a dish, a hand above the turf outside and a finger above the floor within; level under a taut cord and under the brass and thread. Eleven squared grey uprights stand a stride in from the rim, each as thick as a man's body, the tallest twice Brom's height and the shortest knee-high. Round each, at chest height, and round the inner face of the lip, a band a hand wide of fine sunk lines, straight, hooked and crossing. None of Karis's forty-odd chips matches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:480:- **The rubbings (flag):** one sheet to an upright, pencil rubbed over paper held flat by Lira; ten whole and the eleventh half done; numbered, with each stone's height and facing. They are in the middle box (the eleventh rolled inside Karis's coat).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:489:**Where everyone is.** Six people at the den under the eastern lip, ten cords and half a floor inside the line: Cael, Lira, Brom (with the middle box on his back), Karis (with the eleventh sheet rolled in her coat), Seln (on one knee, the locked case on its strap across his chest) and Oryn. The lane trodden by the cord runs from the floor's western lip two hundred yards and more down to the grey stone. Oryn's mule is hobbled on the western side of the stone with her panniers, six of Thornwater's blankets, the carter's left boot and the long shape in its oiled cover still lashed on the pack. No hound has been seen. The sun is half under the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:497:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Refilled: one point on a wick on the 93rd, "three parts full"; nothing since (roads). Inside she can see every place and set none. Holds: the perimeter table (sixty-two stations, one offset); the six-row table of the trials, with the sixth ruled on his statement; **eleven finished rubbings**, ten in the box and the eleventh rolled inside her coat; the carter's map with three pencilled rings, two marked *guess*; the registry's sheet; the long cord and the short one; the brass; forty-odd chips; the leaf with her nine crossings. She has said "A circle is a made shape." once, and nobody took it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:505:**Objects and money.** The middle box: chips, lamp, paper, ten rubbings (the eleventh is in Karis's coat), the two maps, the brass, the short cord. Seln's case on his strap. Two boxes in Thornwater's loft, the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. The Oxhollow cart has gone home with grain. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and eight in hand.** Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman, with no price. A hundred marks lie in Pike's box against the broker's mark on the Fallow paper. Cael's coat has no sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:518:- **Rubbings after fix 7:** eleven complete Ring sheets exist; the last is rolled in Karis's coat and the others are in the middle box (`manuscript/chapter-30.md:99,131-163,237`; `BOOK_MAP.md:35`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:523:- **Exact party/object placement:** six at the den; Brom wears the middle box; Seln is on one knee; Oryn's mule is hobbled west of the line stone with two panniers, six borrowed blankets, the unused left boot and covered crossbow; two boxes remain in Thornwater, with the reserve sealed in the third (`manuscript/chapter-27.md:13-29`; `manuscript/chapter-30.md:3-7,219-239`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:167,183`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:540:- **Inside the line** no companion Path was used: Oryn's hands had nothing ("Nothing in my hands"); cloth and knees. Nobody has theorized why the fragments answered. *Quieting* has still not been said. "Compact" has been said aloud once, by Seln. Nothing is known of where the file is, what it says or why; dating stops at "five years… maybe six". Seln's map memory (maps with holes) is open; he asked for the carter's map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:551:- **The rain-rut (flag):** natural, on the swell's north-west flank; it begins under the floor's north-western lip and runs down a little north of west, knee-deep and a stride wide; abreast of the lane's sixty-yard mark it lies about forty yards north. Nothing made is added to the site.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:552:- **How the hounds behave inside (flag):** careless. They drift; they keep station on the slowest walker; they do not cut a lane that they could cut; one sits down in the lane behind the file. One comes onto a pole a minute after watching another do it. A man who cannot declare is "a man with a stick" to them. A scream turns no head. With their first mover dead, two stand. Once something declares inside, they come as the ford's seven came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:565:- **Thornwater:** five on the bench under the back room's window on the 99th, one a man who cut his shin with a scythe "at the start of the harvest"; she reads all five and puts them off until after noon on the 100th. The back room: one window in the eastern wall with a shutter, a bench under it, a scrubbed plank on two trestles, a three-legged stool, a peg on a thong for a latch; her things on a strip of canvas along the sill (tape on its stick, flask, shears, linen, horn cup, bone needle). The bowman takes off his cap when Lira comes in slung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:568:- **Small things:** Karis's lantern holds "three fingers" of oil after the hour and "a finger" in the loft. Seln makes Brom's bread to dictation and it does not burn. One of Thornwater's six blankets is returned a hand narrower. Lira climbs the loft ladder with her right hand and both knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:626:- **Bodies:** Lira one-armed, shooting; Brom's string restriction ended on the 100th and the crossbow is on his belt; Cael whole. **New canon KEEP:** the founding stone from Ostrand's old gate; the hold's yard crossbow; the husband and the hold's boy (unnamed). **Names:** none minted. **Vastin:** one window, three sentences, no age.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:641:- **The yard bow (flag):** the hold's old stirrup crossbow, kept under the gate-walk in a goatskin "for the fox"; a belt hook; eight blunt bolts fletched with goose feather; it throws left. A straw sack on a stake outside the west gate, forty paces and then sixty, shot at noon. Lira's scores: none of eight in the ring (the 104th); two (the 106th); five from the rail and three from the knee (the 108th); four of eight from the rail and one of one from the knee at sixty (the 110th). The bowman's rail is worn hollow by thirty years of his left hand; nothing has come up to the white stone for fifty-seven evenings; he leaves Lira the walk at dusk on the 110th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:652:- **Oryn's lateness (flag):** nine late at Thornwater and eleven at the next on the 104th (meaning to go on the 106th); she stays to read the arm at the fortnight; "sixteen out of this gate… A round never gives a day back; it takes one." (the 110th, in the yard); "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor" (the loft, the protected line), and in her window the odd day is the old woman's knee and Oxhollow is the 121st. Her window: the child was last heard on the 89th, looked for on the 109th, and cannot be heard before the 126th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:666:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm slung and under the order until first snow; all eight marks taken at the fortnight (the 110th).** Right tibia released: it may run. **Right ankle taped, not mended; she walks on the whole sole.** She can shoot the hold's yard bow braced on a rail or across her knee, one-armed, when somebody else spans it: four of eight in a plate-sized ring from the rail at sixty paces, and one of one from the knee. She cannot span. She kept the gate-walk alone at dusk on the 110th. "Later" is discharged: she was told first. Her long knife is still blunt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:682:**Vastin.** One window: a courtesy copy, read twice, not understood, filed as such, returned unannotated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:726:- **The second Anchor hand (flag):** the empty-handed one at the gate. The course is three lines, each as thick as a wrist, at the knee, the chest and over a tall man's head, thrown from forty paces across the old road from the old stake to the newer and on round the edge: "the same trade in a different hand… this hand threw", where the woman on the Ostrand road threaded nineteen fine points from fifteen feet. He keeps his hands inside his sleeves through the day. At the end Cael sees a pair of hands: a man's, broad, short in the finger, with a pale band where a ring was taken off before the work. Nothing else of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:731:- **Karis's leaf:** on the night of the 113th she takes the leaf of the trials (with the struck sixth row) out of the middle box and carries it inside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:739:- **Millrace (flag):** an hour below the fork, in a narrow green valley; fast brown water over a weir of stakes and stone; a race along the bank to a tall grey mill with a wheel as high as a barn door; a fence of split posts and a dozen roofs; a ford below, with a flat stone on each bank; four chimneys. The ford-keeper is a woman who sits on the nearer stone with a net across her knees and counts people off the ridge. The healer's place is a lean-to of three board walls and a turf roof against the mill's southern wall, with a plank on two kegs and a bench along the wall. The mill's carter is a round floured man; a second carter, long and stooped, takes her word north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:749:- **The ledger (flag; changed in repair r1):** limp hide, no bigger than Hesk's book, ruled in faint blue, a page and a half written in a small upright hand, standing, on a knee. A head line of four signs from an index that Seln does not know, and the number; eight distances by the compass points and half-points, "to floor", every one the same; a line headed *Reach, edge to centre.*, gone over twice (its figure is read aloud to Karis and not printed); a few lines on the floor and the stair, not read out; and on the second page the last line and a stroke of the pencil going off toward the edge. **Its eight stations are the old irons:** Karis found an old scaled bar at each of those eight points that morning, within a boot of her turfs. Seln therefore dates the book with the old iron at the stone, **five cold terms**, which is what lets the closing log's "five years ago" stand. **The stroke-and-bar is two visits:** it lay on the unused bar in this man's case five cold terms ago, and somebody set the same mark on the newer bar at the stone "a good deal later"; Seln will put neither a meaning nor a date on it. Seln puts the ledger inside his own coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:766:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point set, on her lamp, outside the stone on the night of the 114th. Holds: **forty sheets** (eleven of the Ring, twenty-nine of the Stair); **forty-one kinds, the same at both**, entered and underlined; **the line between the courses** (not on the Ring; on every wall of the chamber; recurring at ten cords and a piece, two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, the perimeter's radius), shown on the eastern wall's sheets; the Stair's perimeter table (the Fallow's round, to the stride); the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, written down from Seln's reading; the leaf of the trials, inside her coat; the calfskin, with *guess* struck out beside the Stair's ring and still standing beside the far south-eastern one; the Ternhall brass, out of its cloth and held over the hide. Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid. She has said that the floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size, and nobody took it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:768:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Unmarked. Angry once: a stock broken across the stone, and "No faces." **The case is on its strap, on him, shut. The kit's ledger is inside his coat.** He has said that he wants a face. He has read the lure, the trap and the kit. He has dated the ledger with the old irons (five cold terms) and has said that the stroke-and-bar stands for two separate visits, the later one undated; he has refused to name the index or the mark, or to say what became of the surveyor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:790:- **Research custody:** Seln has the field ledger inside his coat; Karis has eleven Ring sheets plus twenty-nine Stair sheets, forty-one kinds, the trials leaf, the perimeter table, the calfskin with the Stair's *guess* struck and the south-eastern *guess* still standing, and the Ternhall straightedge (52:165–209,247–305,355–369).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:812:- **Oryn** went north from the fork on her own terms, three weeks late at the oxen ford; an old woman's knee at Thornwater is promised "after the thaw"; her letter is printed once. Her brown mule is with the crew; the mill's grey jenny is with Oryn (she left by the left road on it and reached Thornwater on it). [Corrected at close from the Sol recheck.]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:823:- **Stake thirty-three (flag):** one, at the track's end, a boot's width from Karis's stone; scaled in leaves like the old ones; the registry's stamp and two figures, "and nothing beneath them". Seln: five cold terms, "of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone… the same mallet drove all three"; no second. **A second stake on the kerb**, a few yards from the causeway's head, bent over at the height of a knee "like a nail that's been struck from underneath"; not examined closely and not numbered on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:828:- **The fight's ledger:** Brom thrown toward the gap and onto the low wall beside it; about an hour on the walls; his right arm out of its binding as his boot slides; Oryn across thirty yards of plates, hopping, never two counts on one; Karis's cheek scraped raw on the wall's edge (washed, not mended). **Lira's two shots are one-handed:** seated on the wall-top, the great boot planted whole, the stock across her drawn-up right knee and its butt in the hollow of her right shoulder, which is set against a standing length of the wall's old coping; her left arm lies in its sling and does not stir. Seln spans standing on the wall; his two are from the eastern wall, the second held a hand high since the bow throws low. Karis has twelve sheets, all from the inner face of the one wall that she lay on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:833:- **Karis's leaf of the three places (flag):** one leaf made four times: one for each box and the first in her coat with the trials leaf. On it: the three names; perimeter and radius; the stake numbers; the sheets (eleven, twenty-nine, twelve: fifty-two); *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more*; *The lone line is present. Divisions seen; not counted; not measured.*; the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, copied from the book held open by Seln; *The three middles lie on one straight edge, as near as this skin can show.* One line of the leaf is left clean. She tried to make the brass miss and could not; she had laid the ruler herself, before the third place was known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:836:- **Seln's book-keeping:** the ledger was in his coat from the 115th to the 127th. In his window: no name in it; "a field ledger is signed for at a counter, and the name stays at the counter"; his own issue sheet read *Returned or accounted for*. He gives it to Karis on the fourth day of the road home with "It is a field book. It ought to lie with the papers of the field." She will enter where it was found, when, and by whose hand it came to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:843:- **Oryn, days 122–128 (flag):** four days through the gap with the jenny; Thornwater at dusk on the 125th; the bowman's two fingers, for Lira. **The old woman of the hurdle winters at Thornwater, at her daughter's hearth; Oryn had told her at the mill, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee there after the thaw.** She rested it ten days, as bid, and then walked the day and a half over the low place in two, with a grandson to carry the bread; she is first on the bench on the 126th; Oryn reads the knee (mended at Millrace on the 113th) and it has held. Seven on the bench, two mendings. Word by the stooped carter on the 127th: the mill a crushed thumb; two gates no burials; at the ford a finger lost to a chain and the hand kept; the child's breathing no different. On the evening of the 128th the steading's woman gives her bark, ink and a quill; the carter goes down at first light on the 129th and will not come up again before the thaw. She means to go north by the wicket when the bench is paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:857:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Points set on a fire and a lamp only, always outside a line. **Fifty-two sheets** (eleven, twenty-nine, twelve), in three heaps in the back room. **Forty-one kinds** at the two sites done properly; thirty-three of them, and none strange, on the Hall's one wall; the lone line seen there and not measured. Three perimeters of one size and the fourth site's radius by the bend, from outside: four hundred and forty yards and two strides. **The kit's field ledger, given her by Seln.** The leaf of the three places, in four copies (one to a box, one in her coat with the trials leaf). **The carter's map with four rings, the third moved and the fourth unlabelled, and a pencil line through the three paced centres and on off the hide.** The Ternhall brass. She has said "Someone made this pattern." once, aloud, and nobody answered. **Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid.** She paid six marks without counting and keeps the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:861:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. At Thornwater, "on the route". **She has been paid what she set**: three readings, and all of it told, with his hand on her wrist; she has said "It's paid." **She has not forgiven him, and the answer that she kept is still kept.** Her finding stands: she has never felt such a thing in anyone, and does not know what right would look like in him. She has no theory and has been offered none. She knows the mechanism, and the nine by Path. She has the mill's grey jenny, her pannier and rope. Her own brown mule is with the crew. She was three weeks late at the ford with the oxen on the 125th. The old woman's knee, mended at Millrace on the 113th, has held. Her letter: *Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:915:**Movement 8 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: Lira's left arm inert at the Hall (the bow braced on her right knee, right shoulder and the old coping); the coda's re-explanations removed; the approach to and exit from the protected inventory eased without touching it; every join made to agree with the nineteen audit fixes. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE**, no line fixes: every brief item resolved; BOOK_MAP §1 "Ending" on the page item by item; the consent thread's end sound; zero tracked passages and none introduced. The recheck also verified every bullet of the coordinator rulings above against the surviving prose and corrected one (the mule and the jenny). Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected / 45 protected. Movement 37161 words. **Book 7 drafted complete: 60 chapters, 307181 words. The text is NOT locked:** the book-completion pass (whole-arc read, texture lanes, listening proof) follows. Stale Book 8 plan entries the recheck listed (named seasons, "nothing owed", the reading still owed, 400 m, "his Path") are handled in Book 8's reconciliation.

