editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:"People noticed yesterday. People notice everything; they forget it by Thursday." She put her finger on the line again. "This is for Thursday. And next year. If you stop coming to my table, there are three hundred people who'll want to know why, and some of them will come and ask me. I'll open this and show them that line, and tell them I don't know where you've gone. And they'll go and ask somebody else. And then somebody else." She closed the ledger, gently. "That's all a book's for. It doesn't keep anybody safe. It keeps them asked about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:193:He signed it, and dated it, and sanded it, and folded it, and sealed it, and laid it on the case beside the recorder's fair copy, ready for the delegation's courier at first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:230:He sat down across from her and opened the Log to the morning's page and turned it round, as he had turned it round for her a hundred times in a year, and she read the notice. She read it with her lips not quite moving. When she got to the *Note* she read it twice, and then she looked up at him with an expression he had not seen on her since the day, a year ago, when he first turned the old Log round on a table and let her read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:37:"And under all of them," he said, "there's one I don't count. Session nine, at fifteen: a piece of something like Tide, from nobody. There was no Tide practitioner within a day's ride. There was nothing at stake and nobody against me, nothing the rule says it needs, and it came anyway." He moved his hand on the cloth. "I don't know what it is. I've kept it out of the count for three years, because the day I count it I've decided something I don't know. I've got three words for it. *Still open. Still real. Patience.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:167:He had been standing at the edge of the wash, with his hair lifted off his neck and the air pushing at him. And he had looked back up the lane at her, through all of it, steadily. He had looked at her feet first, and then her hands, and her shoulders, and last her face. She knew that look very well. She had worn it every day for six years when she looked at anything. She had never once seen it turned on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:75:She marked the form for the next level up from the office that had sent the return, and she signed it, and dated it, and checked the date against the return and against her own Greyvane reference, since a referral with a wrong date could be sent back for nothing but its date. Then she rose, carried it down to the foot of the table and laid it in the shallow wooden tray, where the records officer would find it, and came back to her chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:113:He dated the fourth and wrote it in the same dry hand as the others, because the sameness of the hand was the point. It was one line long: the Greyvane referral returned from the chain above, eleven months on, and refiled a level higher inside the recess, both sheets logged and cross-referenced in his own hand. It was true, and it said no more than he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:225:"So it's dated today," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:227:"It's dated today. It's in the file." She tilted her head back against the wall. "With a dot next to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:It was the last day of the academic year. Cael had known it when he woke, as one knows a date that has been set against one's name for a long time. Somewhere behind him, six switchbacks up from a river, a bell would ring that evening and the house would close its books; and at some hour between, on a north road short of a toll-bar, a stout fair man would unlock a windowless building. He did not know the hour, for Withrow's sheet had given only the day, and he kept looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:481:He had thought he knew what large was. He had seen the continent drawn on a sheet. He had crossed a good part of it, at a wagon's pace, to stand in a hall at Norhold, and he had looked down from a bluff at a river valley every day for two years and thought it a wide thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:239:"*Review for advancement of tier. The panel convened under this office's docket of the fourth day is confirmed: three assessors and a sigil frame, at the district seat. The practitioner's record is received and entered as it stands, the title included. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year.*" He turned the sheet to the light. "And at the foot. *Outcome anticipated: advancement, per title.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:That was all that the last line meant. A registry had read him for years, and written down what it found, and had never once told him beforehand what it was reaching for or what the reaching might cost him. He was about to reach for somebody else in the same way. The one thing that he had which that office had not was a leaf with tonight's date at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:13:Coss had been a field agent of the Guilds Compact for eleven years, and he was forty-one, and somewhere around his fourth year he had made his peace with not being a brilliant man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:Coss had watched all of that from the other side of their desk and taken a lesson from it that he had never once had cause to regret. Brilliance found things, but it found false things nearly as easily as true ones. Attention, put down patiently day after day and year after year, found the true things in the end, and very seldom found the false ones, because it was too slow and too dull to be fooled by its own shine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:The third sheet was a gate entry. *Denvash, Outer gate, eastbound, on foot.* It was dated the third day after the Kindling. It was passive, which meant that a guard had read the card in the ordinary way and the record had marked it and nobody had been asked to do anything about it. Eastbound from Denvash, on foot, was the merchant road, and the merchant road came here in two days, and anybody who knew the road would have guessed this city. That part was not strange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:73:"Dated the day of your arrival."
