editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:He then had before him a ruling, given under the Compact's hand two days earlier, and an enrollment which that ruling revoked from the year's close; and the enrollment had ended of itself before the close arrived. He turned to the code. It had a word for a matter that has lost its subject, and he wrote the word in the proceedings' last column, small, and blotted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. There has not been a First-day like it in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:27:She had come up the bluff road at the ninth bell in a hired chaise from Ostrand, with one bag, and Cael had never seen her before. The inspection had brought a counsel up the hill once, a woman who talked by every account from the bridge to the gate and signed for her chests with a face like a cat in a bath; she had been the Compact's. This was the house's own. In eleven years nothing she had advised the house had ever carried her name; that was her price, and the house had paid it gladly. People on the bluff called her counsel and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:77:Bracken did not know it. He said so when Cael passed him the leaf. He knew every procurator who had stood before a registry seat in this district in thirteen years, and most in the two districts beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:39:"Every time you do anything at all, until it's worn in. I had a book for it, on the hill. What to take late, and what to give. I refused something or other every minute of every Fifth-day for two years, and I thought it was the price of being let stay. I didn't know I was learning a trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-43.md:183:She had not told them to. She was in the step, turning, ready to meet it moving and let it go round, and her hands went up to the height and closed, square, standing, the way the barn master had taught them and the way they had done it every day for years. They caught it, flat, and stopped, before the rest of her had any say in it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:31:The wing's weekly summaries hung on it in their rows, from the first week of the year before last to the week just ended, each of them one sheet in the same small square hand, pinned at its two top corners. The floor hours were there, and the cards, and the figures by cohort, and the corrections, with a margin wherever a correction had been made and the reason for it. The last sheet was dated the Seventh-day. It was finished, and totalled, and ruled off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:233:There was one slip for each. Lira's first meet off the hill, three weeks away now, on a road, against Silvers from other houses. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The next sitting at the wool exchange. And a fourth slip with nothing written on it, for the re-evaluation, whose date the seat would fix when it ruled. He set them in a row, as Karis had set out her six on the long table, and sat looking at them with Hesk's book open on his knee, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:"Four, in the Compact's own digests, in all the years they go back, who carried this word. I've read their files, one by one, at the long table downstairs. The first was ordered out of the place they lived nine days after the Kindling that flagged them. The second in six. The third in eleven. The fourth in four." He kept his eyes on Yorlan's. "Not one of them had a hearing. Not one of them was asked anything that their file sets down. In every one, an order went out under an authority nobody looked at, because the word sat on the form in the place where a classification goes, and everybody who handled it trusted that somebody before them had checked. Three of those files don't end. They stop. I'm the fifth. I'm the first who's ever been let stand in a room like this and put the question out loud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:39:Her chain of proof ran through thirty-odd volumes, from the founding schedule that held the clause to the charter article that took it into Halcenvane. At Greyvane she had built it out of her own transcriptions, careful ones, in her small upright hand, and Bracken would not use a single line of them. For every link in the chain a clerk went to the actual volume, in whatever library held it, and copied the passage whole. Then the librarian who kept that volume read the copy against the page, word by word, and sealed the foot of it. Where the volume was not on the bluff, a letter went down the river by the first coach to the house that held it, asking for a copy under that house's seal. A dated note of the asking went into the box in its place, so that even the gap was on paper. That protected against the first thing a hostile reader would say, which was that the whole chain rested on one girl's handwriting. By the end of the third day there was not a word of Karis's handwriting anywhere in it. She had stood at the table and watched the last of it go, and Cael had watched her face, and it had not looked like loss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:He looked for the anger, because he thought he might be owed some, and found instead something older and more tired that had his own face. He had kept a great many things in a great many neat columns for a great many years: Tuesdays, the provisions, the preliminary assessment, and a file he had once seen opened in a room above his own clearance. He had kept them from the boy until the boy found them out for himself and came to him angry, across the workshop bench, on the second day after Kindling. *A few years,* he had said in the side room at Weaver's Row, and he remembered the boy's face. If there was a figure missing from this letter, it was missing for the boy's reason. He would tell it when it was worth telling, or he would not, and either way it was not Hesk's to dig out of a letter with a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:161:The reviewing officer read out the notice in full, in the voice in which such things are read: Statute 14, Section 9; the residence; the standing; the paragraph that said what keeping the person close would cost. He read Hesk's own account after it, the one Hesk had written and sent and been asked for a copy of. *The person named in the registry notice of the second day no longer resides at this address. He left on the morning of the third day for another city, where he is lawfully resident, and he goes with my full support.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:27:*Evaluation: the Fourth-day following. The last day of the academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:"In the week after the close a clerk will come up our road with a stamp, and he will write here what the ruling says of the respondent institution. *Respondent institution compliant; no finding.* I shall give him tea. I told you a year ago that the best day this folder could have was a clerk writing *sustained*, and I was wrong, for this is better. It is the cleanest remedy column I have seen against a contested entry in twenty years at the registry and all my years in this chair." She took the finger away. "And in Bracken's enrollment book, under your name, there will be five words in his hand. Under three other names as well, I gather, though I have not asked how many and I shall not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:263:He did not choose to. That was the shame of it, and he felt it even as it happened. A year of Fifth-days in wing three had put the waiting into his body as a drill puts a landing into a foot. Twelve hundred people were looking down, and a grey man with a pencil, and the coat was on him before he had so much as thought of it. He stood in his own bout and waited, so that it would look as if he had found the answer when a good Iron would have found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:He knew what lay in that pause as well as he knew his own Log. It was the true argument. She had just told a seat, with perfect accuracy, that its instrument had failed to read him, and she knew why it had failed, and what was appended to the reading, and how long it had been so, and whose hands had signed for it. She could have dated the thing to within a renewal of Greyvane's charter. It would have taken her nine minutes, and she had the nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:217:"The seat reserves," said the hood. "It will give its ruling upon the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the hour after noon. From the First-day of that week the parties will keep within the bell, at the seat's call and the evaluation seat's. So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:"You are setting your cord beside his, and his figure is in a registry book. I have spent fifteen years learning what that office's paper is worth. I should not care to see you rest a finding on a number of theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:He had been sitting there a quarter of an hour, after the grey half, with two loaves from underneath and a twist of the fruit woman's apricots and nothing at all in his head, which was a thing he had been learning to do. The market was loud and slow around him. It was the first warm Saturday of the year, and people had come down to it who did not need anything, only to walk about in the sun and look at things and be seen looking. He had been watching them without meaning to. He had been watching the way a man in a brown coat came down the second row between the stalls, unhurried, reading the bread woman's high cracked lantern as if it were a notice, and he had thought, idly, *that man reads things the way they're written*. Then the man had turned out of the row toward the pie stall, and Cael had seen the set of his shoulders, and the brown coat had stopped mattering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:105:Ephram had the Crown yard to himself on the First-day at the second bell, as he had had it on every First-day for three years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:217:It was not hard, as it had been hard once, in a corner, with a lane coming. It was only strange, tonight, because the man in the dark was the man it had come from, and the whole of what that man was teaching them this week was how to be seen plainly and written down as what they were. He held it shut, as he had held it shut every day for two years, and counted Karis's steps instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:91:"Leaving. What it costs, to the day and the line; not what it feels like, but what the code writes down." Karis shifted the volume under her arm. "I know what happens if a respondent walks out in the middle of a proceeding, because I read the coastal default to you in the dairy, and you all remember the word. I don't know what happens to the rest of us, or to the house, or whether there's any day in the year when it's different." She looked at Lira. "Give me two days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:63:The first went to a man who kept the mould room at the south mill. Eleven years ago Seln had filed one true sentence about him instead of a sentence that would have been more useful to somebody else, and the man had never known which office to thank, but he had known whom. The letter asked one thing. *Was the registry mould mended this year, and if so, when, and was a run made off it after?* It did not say who wanted to know. It did not have to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:On the First-day Karis read the year back to herself, one card at a time, and Cael sat at the end of the long table and listened to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:He had copied it a month ago from the foot of the registry summary page, under the four-line entry he already knew by heart. It was a footnote, and it told anyone whose Kindling came out non-standard to present themselves at a Compact district office inside two days for something it called standard compliance processing. He had gone and found out what that processing involved, written it underneath, and struck both lines through. He never struck anything through. A wrong figure stayed on the page with the right one beside it, because Hesk said an erased mistake was a mistake you got to make again. This one he had crossed out anyway, and he still could not have said whether he had done it to be rid of it or so that he would always know where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:347:His mind went down the day without his asking it to, as it would run a column of figures to see whether they summed. Alis on the step at Weaver's Row, folding her certificate in half and pressing it flat again. Joren on his back in the grit, laughing up at the sky until he wasn't. The woman on the grey sheet, dropping under a strike that never came, rising into a forearm, touching her fist to her chest. And Hesk: at the record office asking about provisions; a man who had seen this once, long ago; a man who had sat in a folding chair in the dark every Tuesday for years, learning how a body moves when there is a Path in it, and never once said why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:123:It never stopped, Cael understood. Not at supper, not with a cup in his hands, not in a room with two people he had come to eat with. Brom had told him once, on the floor, that he did not know where to be hard without it. He had not understood until now that it had been like this every hour of every day for two years. Brom could not put it down any more than a man can put down his own hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:71:Karis wrote it, exactly like that, at the very top of the page, above Brom's three sentences, so that the page now began with the last thing anybody said. She dated it and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:243:Then she looked at Cael, and Cael looked at the notebook on his knee, at the question he had underlined twice on the thirty-fourth evening, with the empty space under it. The space under it stayed empty, since this was not an answer yet. He wrote it on the next page instead, and dated it, and put Lira's name beside it, and then, because it had been his sentence and he wanted it honestly filed, his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:25:*Three. Nothing served on this house since the eighth day of the year speaks of moving him anywhere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:"At every meet." Gault sobered, a little. "The tournament charter has the registry validate every tier result. A Copper win goes onto a Copper fighter's record only once a Compact officer has watched it and signed that the tier was what the paper said. So there will be a validation seat at every floor, every meet, a man or a woman with a badge and a book." He reached for the next form on his tray, and then did not pick it up. "Nobody will have to come up this bluff road to look at you this year, enrollee. You are going to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:115:The room was high up in the Compact's house, two days from Norhold by the post road. He had worked in it for nineteen years. It looked down into a paved court where a porter fed the pigeons from a paper bag every morning at the same hour, and there was a long cabinet of shallow drawers down one wall, and a seal-press he had screwed to the desk's corner himself, crooked, on his first morning, and never straightened. On the desk stood three trays: in, held, done. He had kept them in that order since before he had a desk worth the name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:203:*Deposited for safe keeping, the first day of the year's last month. Three boxes, roped. Depositor: K. Dellenmoor. Contents: the depositor's own papers. To be released to the depositor, or to her order.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:*First-day. The year's last month.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:93:"The second finding is about time," she said. "How old each piece of it is. I'm going to tell you how I dated it first, because if I don't you'll think I'm guessing, and I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:137:It was the usual half-sheet in the usual clerk's hand, dated twice and initialled twice in its corner, as everything the wing put out was. *Supervised floor allocation, week following.* Four lines. Cael read it standing at the board with the traffic of the passage going by behind him, as he read every issue, and on the fourth line he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:233:Lira drew the second seed's line, a coastal Mire Path, Rank Eight, who stood below the top seed only by the registry's dates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:The pen lay where Lira had left it. It was a plain steel pen in a dark holder, with the house's mark burned into the wood, and it had lain at that end of the counter for longer than Cael had troubled to notice. Bracken had said so on a landing two nights ago. *There is a pen on my counter. There has been one there since the fourth day of the year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:*Enrollee: Caelen Hesk-ward.* It was the name the registry had given him at Denvash, the one that stood on every instrument and that nobody who knew him had ever used, and Bracken had written it out in full, because a form was an instrument. *House: Halcenvane. Basis of enrollment: the demonstration provision of the assay clause; granted at Greyvane; twice renewed.* And on the line for tier, where Lira's leaf said *Iron* and Brom's said *Copper*, the registrar had written the only true thing there was to write, as he had seen it written on a door log at a wool exchange every Fifth-day for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:195:"He has dated it for today," said Bracken. "He was particular that it should carry the same day as the leaf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:On the sill lay the enrollee's copy of the instrument. It had lain there, first leaf up, since the eighth day of the year: nineteen leaves on one side, thirty-seven paragraphs, three schedules, whose words he had counted on the first night as a man counts the guards on a wall. He left it where it lay, since it had been addressed to the house as much as to him, and the house could have it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:At the foot of the road the two coats stood in the lee of the wall, as they had stood every day of the year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:87:It was the registry's pocket calendar for the year, printed small on stiff card, every day in its own square. Cael had watched him take it out a hundred times across his counter to settle a question about a fee, and put it back without looking at anybody. He laid it under the lamp now and took up his pen. When the weight on the table had grown too great to look at, Bracken had reached for the one thing on any paper that weighed exactly what it weighed, which was its dates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:"Dated the first day of the year," said Bracken. "The morning of convocation. Somebody a week's road from here pressed that wax while the Chancellor stood on her platform telling this house to be what its papers say it is. I don't suppose they knew. I don't suppose it matters." He touched the second mark. "Served this morning. Seven days on a road the post does in three, at a walk, with three horses and a man in the middle who'd been told the pace. It wasn't sent to arrive. It was sent to be watched arriving. I watched it for a full minute from my window, and so did the gate, and so did the coats at the foot of the hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:75:*The seal on the instrument constituting the facilities office named in the order presented at Halcenvane, and on the order itself (registrar's rubbing): absent from the district's impression book; absent from the registry's schedule of seals of office, this year's and last; its rim number absent from the cutters' issue ledger, where the numbers one below it and one above it stand on consecutive lines with no line for it between. Checked three ways. Absent from every registry of seals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:191:"Then they'll have to argue their way past a stack of dated pages first. I've backed worse odds than these, Cael. I don't back anything I expect to lose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:The old man was not there. Cael had not thought he would be. The old man stood in his gate at the top of the lane of yards on every day of the year, and whatever happened in the district went past him or did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:3:The house kept no rest day in its last week, and the closing seminars sat through the Seventh-day as they did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:327:He had climbed the east stair to the public gallery to look at the Compact's row close to, after four days of charting it from below. The crowd in the public gallery next to the row was thick, and a stout man with a broadside and a meat pie had pushed along the end of the public bench until he was at the rail-cloth with the registry's grey sigil on it, and beyond it, a single empty chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-22.md:247:"A man with a Compact pin walked the food market yesterday morning," Cael said. "He stopped at some of the stalls and said things. I'd like to know what he was asking about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:17:At the bottom of the bundle was the minute of a first sitting at Ostrand. It was dated yesterday.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:Lira read it aloud twice, the second time because Brom asked her to. Then she lifted her cup to the ledger-keeper of Ardenmere, and they all drank to her, and Karis said that Vell would certainly have entered the toast in the ledger as an item, dated and stamped, if she had been there to see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:He had been in the Compact forty years, and he knew that kind of quiet. He had sent it down the stairs himself, once or twice, to offices whose questions he had not wished to hear a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:307:Her opponent was an Ash Path fighter from one of the southern houses, a middle seed, a compact quiet man a few years older than her with ash-grey wraps on his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:"Entire." Gault's hand was still on the board. "I was asked for the notes upon the enrollee. I have taken that to mean every note in which he appears, and not only the notes of his bouts. There are seventeen panel notes, one to a supervised session, with the three cards and the figure. There are also five notes that are not of any panel." He lifted the hand. "I mention it so that the seat is not surprised by the leaf for the year's seventy-sixth day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:189:Brom's gift did not care what a foot felt. It read weight and what bore it, and it came back from the boards under him as flat and dry and sound as it had been every Fifth-day for a year. There was no depth. There was a pine plank, an inch and a quarter thick, on a joist, and the clay was in his ankle and nowhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:159:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:175:On the fourteenth day Karis closed the year's papers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:121:He had ticked that box two hundred times without opening the manual, as everybody did. A handling marker was a line in the classification block, entered by somebody with a right to enter it, signed and dated in the right-hand column. To confirm it against the manual meant to read the line and see that it was the kind of line the manual allowed, and every line on every file he had ever drawn had been the kind of line the manual allowed. Every one had initials beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:He could tick the box, and nobody would ever check it. He had stood in this room every Tuesday for four years and been told, in the supervisor's flat voice, that the sheets were for the registry's comfort and not for anybody's reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:Cael had fought in the room every Fifth-day for a year without once looking up at the west wall above the door. There had never been anything there worth looking at: a narrow timber balcony, a rail of dark old oak, and behind the rail a stack of spare benches and broken frames that the porters had been putting there since before he came up the hill. The house had built the gallery in the days when wing three was its examination room, so that visiting assessors could look down on a candidate without standing on the same boards. Nobody had visited for a long time. The benches had gone up into it one at a time, the way things go into a loft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:"It's comfortable," he said. "That's the trouble with it. Comfortable for years, and then one day not, and all at once." He held up a finger. "Three costs. One: the line's a document. It goes into the file in Gault's hand, and one day an inspector opens the file. Bracken's warned me twice, and Withrow wrote *sooner* on her sheet with her own pen. Find a feeble line in there and you've found proof the provision lets feeble people in. Somebody uses that against the provision, and I've lost the only floor I stand on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:183:"I watched the floor all evening. Ephram does it to everybody." Brom folded his arms. "He did it to me in the first month last year. Three days of it. I thought it was my fault."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:229:There was no hearing notice, no ruling, no seal and no letter from anybody with a title. There was a single sheet from the regional qualifying registrar's office, printed on a press that was short of ink, headed *Deficiency memorandum: entries returned for correction*, and listing five houses in a column. One had dated its fee note to the wrong year, one had used last cycle's form, one had sent its schedule acknowledgment unsigned, and one had a practitioner whose rank on the entry did not match the registry's extract. The fourth on the list was Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:129:It was like calling a dog by its name in a yard full of dogs. He had called the Wind as he had called it every day for years, without thinking, and something else lifted its head instead. It was the new thing, raw and enormous and not yet sure where it lived, answering to a name that was not its own. The Wind did not come at all. The foot came down on the stone with nothing behind it. Nothing was wrong with the stone. The man with the level had set it true, and it was true still. It was the leg that had been given the wrong instruction, by the wrong part of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:197:"Three: this house has not qualified in eighteen years, which matters to me only as a sum, and what I care about instead is that the Compact sat in these buildings for eleven days hoping to catch us out, and did not, and so we know now that this house can do a difficult thing correctly with people watching it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:"I told you. Three lines." Lira considered. "The third one was the only one with any of him in it. *He won't have told you.* As if he'd known for years that one day somebody would write and ask, and was glad it was me." She looked away at the lamp. "That's all. Now eat something, before Brom cries."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:243:*Shadow Path, Bronze, fifteen years' craft if he's a day. The Compact finally sent someone worth watching. So I'm watching.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:25:She opened her desk drawer, and took out a paper, and laid it on the folder on his knees. Then another, and another, without a word between them. The program's charter, dated the first morning of the year. The page of the house's accounts with Karis's line on it. The intake's roll, longer than any he had seen pinned to a board. The tournament's sheet with the house's placing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:She had borrowed Brom's docket out of his left inside pocket to copy its date, and Brom had stood in the annex doorway until she gave it back, which she did inside a quarter of an hour. She had the two dates on a slip, one above the other. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The seat's ruling, before the year's close, and there was only one Fifth-day in the year's last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:The sittings went up on the covered walk's middle board that afternoon in Bracken's hand, under the house's one-line answer from the eighth day: *Sittings at the registry seat, Ostrand, on the seat's Fifth-days. First sitting: the sixth Fifth-day of the year.* Underneath it, smaller, Bracken had added a line of his own, which was a thing he never did on a public board. *The house will attend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:45:It occurred to her that a registry clerk, three years ago, had written this girl down in about the time it took to read a short page, and that nobody had read the page again since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:*Standing.* These were the people he would carry at full depth all the time, kept live and updated every day. Eight of them at the most, and never nine, because nine was where the failing started and he meant to keep one place clear between himself and the edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:187:*Sit out four sessions running and you slip one anyway. Year's end: the best four in each column are seeded to the semifinals, which run in the second term's closing weeks. The final comes on the year's last day of competition. Faculty sit as the panel. The registrar keeps the record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:133:Then the sheet itself. Not Rooke's red book, which was the program's and nobody else's, but this: a figure in a meet record, under a hall's seal, filed with a district. One point under a man who had been Silver for eleven years. One day a clerk at a counter in Ostrand would take her file out of a drawer for the advancement sitting, and this would be in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:19:It had started on the third day with a second-year in the corridor outside the north hall who had stopped talking as Cael passed and then started again a little louder, and it had gone on from there, because people here did not wonder what he was, the way they had at Greyvane; they told each other. Each of them had a version, and each version was finished, and no two of them were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:*Instructor Rooke: the provision may be sound; objects to the attention it draws; asks that his objection stand dated before any notice of inspection. Chancellor: so minuted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:155:The left column was what he would cost her: first an inspection, sooner than her charter's cycle required, from an office with power over that charter, asking questions it would choose and she would not; then a public failure, if the enrollment failed, which would be counted as Halcenvane's failure and not the clause's. Withrow noted in the margin that a tournament committee did not forget a school that dropped a thing in front of the whole registry, not for ten years at the least. And the plain running cost of him: a full panel every semester, faculty hours, apparatus, and the whole of Bracken's winter, which by rights had belonged to four hundred and twelve other people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:"My knees are the third term. If they go, I'll say. You'll hear me say it." He held his arms out, the blue forearms, the swollen hands. "This isn't them going. This is me finding out what you are. And for an hour now I've been finding out what you are when you're being careful with somebody, and I don't need that. I've got that already. I watched you be careful with twelve-year-olds for twelve days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:There were eleven names on the manifest and he had pages on nine of them. The library had given him three of the four escort riders out of a dozen years of attendance lists, the counsel out of her published findings, and the three clerks out of a registry yearbook that nobody had borrowed in a decade. Havel had a page with a question of Lira's at the foot of it. Ilsev had the thickest page and the least use, because six findings told him what she would do with a document and nothing at all about what she did with a morning. The fourth rider was too new to be anywhere. And the eleventh name had one line on its page, and under the line, in his own hand, a single word: *Lira*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:143:The second stood beside the stretch where the boy had counted the schedule's places out loud for the panel, five of them and then a sixth bound in at the back, and had treated the count as a wall. He had understood that a list which says *these and no others* shuts out its own author as firmly as anybody else. Men with long registry careers forgot that every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:147:And the third stood at the foot of the record's first sheet, beside two dates the Compact's own clerk had entered there: the day the challenge was filed, and the day the first sitting was called. He had drawn two lines under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:Three weeks was in the record, and no one had argued with it. Both dates were in the Compact's own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:123:She had read the term's sheets the day before, in one of the registrar's boxes, all of them, in date order. Every one was initialled, and every one bore a receiving stamp from the registrar's office dated the same afternoon as the hour it recorded, and that had told her something about the office. It had told her nothing yet about the hand. Now she had the hand in front of her, and the hand matched the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:261:*It looks like a man who isn't there. That's the whole trick of it. I've been using it for two years, and today somebody used it on me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:89:"What I am is a puzzle I've been working at alone. I've got one example to study, and nobody ever showed me how studying's done. Asking questions properly is the thing she's trained at, five years of it. And one day somebody's going to try to find out what I am without asking me at all." He was looking at Lira now. "Whatever there is to find, let the finding happen here, among us, on purpose, written down. Before it gets found in some room they won't let me into."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:The eighth day of the year was a First-day, hot and still, and at the second bell Cael was on the covered walk with a book under his arm and nowhere in particular to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:41:Bracken had the enrollment book open on the counter, the great ruled volume the house kept its roll in, at the page that held Cael's own line. Under the entry of the first week and the baseline of the second month, Bracken had already written the new one, dated and initialled, in the small upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:137:The waystation had kept its books as any working house on a guild road had to: for itself, every day, in good years and bad, because a house that did not know how many sacks of oats it had would run out of oats. The yearbooks were the spine, one for every year the house had stood, each closed at its year's end with the clerk's mark and numbered in the house's own years from the first. The other runs hung off them. The toll books said who paid what at the gate. The provisioning accounts counted sacks and barrels and horseshoes. A weather log had been kept daily for nineteen years by one hand, without a day missed. And the traveller registers said who had passed through and when, where from and where bound, and what they carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"We'll be at the front today," he said. "The yearbooks first. Tomorrow the toll books and the accounts. The traveller registers on Saturday, I'd think, at this rate." He looked at the cases behind her. "The archivist has asked for a signed line for every box, at her door. It'll go slower than they're used to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:175:Eleven days later, in a weather log of all places, she had found the overlap. The weather log had been kept into the house's last years, after the guild road had gone quiet. On the last page of the last one, under the last day's weather, its clerk had written a line about the house being given over, books and stones and all, to the people who meant to make a school of it. The line was dated twice. Once it gave the house's own year, and once a year in a reckoning Karis could count back from, because the archive's oldest accession register used it too, on its very first page, in Prynn's predecessor's predecessor's hand. Prynn's register ran unbroken from that page to last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:*Yearbook one. First page. Founding entry: stones laid, roof raised, first traveller lodged. Dated the first year of the house.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:75:Karis did not use Quenna's pen; she took one of her own two from her coat. She wrote slowly, in her small upright hand with every letter closed at the top, stopping twice to look at the window before going on. When she reached what Cael guessed was the second-to-last line she drew a line under it, once, steadily, from one end to the other. Then she wrote one more sentence, signed and dated it, and turned the sheet round so that it faced him rather than Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:85:Quenna turned the sheet round toward herself and read it. Cael watched her come to *source*, watched her eyes stop on it a fraction longer than on any other word and then go on and not come back. She did not ask what it meant. She initialed the margin beside Karis's signature, dated it, and laid it in a folder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:195:In the afternoon, at the sixth bell, she went to the open floor. She went because the floor was the only place she had ever been sure of, and because she had a review in three days and two years of rust in her shoulders, and because she wanted to find out what the drills felt like with no question sitting on top of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:He knew the grounds. He had sat forty feet from a table for a year of Fifth-days and had not looked at it. He had looked at that boy before, on a pump step and at Greyvane and on a sprung floor at the bluff; he remembered every one of the looks, and had sworn off another, and had kept to it for a year by looking at his own hands. He was tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hours, and he wished to work in a room where the leaves were only leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:119:It was true, every word. He read it over once and signed it and dated it for the morning, and laid it on top of the kit, where he would see it when he woke, to hand in at the district seat on his way to the exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:There was no trick in it. A year of Fifth-days had been walking toward exactly this leaf, and every door on the way had been real law and had opened one way, as Karis had told them on the first night. He had read it better than they did, and so had she, and it had made no difference, because it had never turned on the reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-04.md:119:"Keep your address straight in the lodgers' book," she said. "Keep your name spelt the way the registry spells it. Pay your rent on the day. If anybody wants a reason to trouble you, make them work for it." She stood, and put the book under her arm. "I'd hate to have to write *unrated* beside you, after all this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:225:"Not as evidence. I know it isn't evidence. A road-house register isn't a document of the Compact, and on Monday it'll be under the Compact's own seal anyway, and you can't produce what they're holding." She put her hand on the top notebook. "But you could say it. You could stand in that room and tell them the word they've built everything on was put there by somebody, once, over another word, on an instruction nobody even bothered to name. You could show them it was never something anybody found. It was something somebody wrote." Her voice was very level. "And it's so, Cael. Nothing in the schedule is as plainly so as that page. You know it isn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:209:"No," said Wray. "Not closed. I can make you see it at the speed it matters. You can see it now better than anybody I've taught in twenty years. I can't make you fast enough to close it." She did not soften the next part, because she never softened anything, and Cael understood that this was its own kind of respect. "Nobody on this hill can. I can build you an imitation of Silver for four rounds out of four students who'd rather be eating their supper. To close it you'd need somebody who's Silver all day long, every day, for a year. That isn't here." She tucked the slate under her arm. "I'll keep looking. I said I would. I don't say that twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:She unrolled the paper on the canvas and laid the brass along its top edge to hold it. It was a straightedge, an old one, which she had had from Ternhall. Cael knew the sheet before the firelight had found the whole of it, for it had hung on her reading-room wall on the first day of the year, and she had taken it down in the first week to make room for a code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:145:To the right of the line there was nothing. The printer had left the paper bare for a hand's breadth, to the sheet's edge, and he had used the space: the key to the symbols was set in it, in a neat box, and under the key the registry's seal and the year of the impression.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:At the regional registry office, on the Wednesday after the girl came back up the hill, a clerk with a cold that had not gone away since the autumn entered one line in a book under the heading for Greyvane Academy. The notation sheet had come down from the town station by the station's own courier on the Monday.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:53:The first was dated a little more than a year back and written in a district office with one window on an alley. It concerned a market, and a boy of fifteen who had answered every question exactly and not one word past it, more settled than the officer questioning him. Then there was a marker in the boy's file that Havel had not placed and could not trace. *File and subject do not match. Marker origin not found. Noted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:193:"*The candidate demonstrated, at his own election, an ignition-point capability, contact-adjacent, single channel. Three times, at a size stated in advance, with a rest between. Consistent, observable practice. No safety violation noted. Assessment satisfied.*" He laid it down. "Entered as of its own date. The Compact offers the record of the sittings as the academy's own account of what the respondent can do. The Compact does not dispute a word of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:It was the plainest reason of the four. Since the year's eighth day the registry had had a petition before its seat in the town below, asking that he be stood in a frame and read again. If the frame could not read him, the code had a chapter for that: his enrollment struck out, and after it, a letter at a time, everything else with his name on it. And a respondent who left the hill while the thing was pending would be entered as fled, with a note on three friends' files to say whose friends they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:243:When he had blotted it he went to the window. Across the dark court the wing's counter was shut, and behind it one lamp was burning at the copying table, as it burned every Seventh-day night of the year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:117:He had felt the framework go slow before, at the end of a long night and under a fast quarter and yesterday in the third exchange with the ground full of fire, and this was not that. This was a place where the slip had always been and was not, like putting a hand into the coat pocket where you have kept the same thing every day for years and closing it on lining. It lasted half a second, no more. His body moved without it, too late and too wide, and Lira's push found the point of his right shoulder, where the old Reydan complaint lived, and turned him half round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:89:Today the second-years had done a different sum. They had come an hour early with bread in their coat pockets and climbed to the back, because a full yard would shut the late half of the bluff out in the covered walk, and the back rows were the ones nobody could take from you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:Cael did not answer. A man who ran a registry had spent his first hour of the day building something that would stay true whichever of his friends walked off the boards with the crown. There was no sentence Cael could have made about that just then which would not have come out wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:15:Then the rest, which was ordinary, as Karis had said. *The seat at Ostrand gives notice that it has convened formal proceedings upon the petition served on the eighth day of the year. The seat will sit on its Fifth-days, upon the schedule of the academic year, and will rule before the year's close. Counsel of record will confer with the seat's clerk upon the schedule on the twenty-fifth day. The first sitting will be held on the sixth Fifth-day of the year. Senior evaluators of the district will be called to the evaluation seat on a day to be notified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:157:"Only the order of business." He put the inkwell in his coat and stood. "Enter that the petitioner takes no point on the clause, or the papers, or the renewals, and needn't be heard on any of them. Put me down for the registry's discretion, on whatever days the seat gives me. First, whether there's a question of integrity at all. Then the remedy." He waited while the clerk wrote. "The respondent will want time to answer each. I'd give it to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:125:The box from the second cart was the fourth she had opened since the fifth bell. Its slip said *Founding stock, case eight, shelf three. Traveller registers, nine volumes.* Under that were the nine marks in a clerk's careful hand, and the clerk's name, and *Counted at the door* in Prynn's, dated a Saturday nearly three weeks gone. She looked at the slip. She broke the wax. She cut the string. She lifted the lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:225:She took the registry's sheet back from the corner of his day-book. She did not take it up the table. She stood where she was, at his shoulder, in the lamplight, and looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:247:Then he dated the third, the day and the month and the year, and wrote it in the same dry hand as the others, because the same hand was the point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:257:He did not know what he was building. He knew that he was building something, one dry dated line at a time, and that he could not have said what it was for. He knew that he had never yet written an entry that he could have shown to anybody without being asked what he meant by it, and that he could not have answered. And he knew he had just watched the one person in the delegation most faithful to the registry's text meet a line that text could not explain, and do with it what he himself had once done: nothing, done correctly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:91:"I have." Coss brought out a second paper, a thick packet tied crosswise. "The enrollment is invalid as categorized. The category's own gloss reserves it for a candidate who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. You were assessed, at the station in Denvash. The registry holds the date, and I hold the entry. The provision's drafting records show what it was written for, and it was not written for you." He set the packet on Naveth's blotter and squared it to the edge. "That is the case against the category. The case that makes it urgent is this one, Provost. Keep an enrollment on your rolls that you know to be invalid, and a chartered academy exposes its own charter to review. I'm not threatening you. I'm telling you why this can't be left alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-15.md:193:The confirmation went in the way it always went in, in words he could have written in his sleep: *Visit made. Subject found in district, morning. Address confirmed by keeper's book. Compliant. Nothing further.* He dated it and initialled it. Then he took a green slip from the box in his drawer, the supplementary compliance marker from Tuesday's standing instructions, and turned to the classification block at the front of the file to enter it, as instructed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-15.md:211:He knew one other thing, and did not like it. Passive monitoring was a district officer's work, and in four years he had done it on files of every class that called for it. Not one of them had ever carried a marker from above a district officer's grade. The four old files of this class in the archive had carried markers enough, but every one of them signed, dated and explained, the way the registry did everything it meant. This line, by its form alone, had been written by nobody at his grade. Somebody above him had put it there, or somebody had put it there and not signed it, which no officer at any grade was supposed to be able to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:179:Cael was not watching for it, or so he had told himself. He was writing the last Iron bout of the day, two Shield fourth-years walking their panes at each other like men carrying doors through a crowd, when the knot of six that had stood on the upper west tier for an hour came apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:87:She did not speak at first, and Lira did not push. Karis was at the other end of the table with her notebook shut, and Brom was opposite, and the long room was full of the noise of a Saturday, second-years arguing about tiles and somebody laughing by the hatch. Cael had begun to think that Gerda had come only to sit near people for an hour and would go away again without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:179:He was wearing his good coat, the dark wool one he wore to the Inner gate, not the workshop coat with the burn on the sleeve, and his boots were clean and his hair was combed. In his left hand he held a flat leather folder, and Cael knew without being told that it held his registration papers and Cael's, and that it had been waiting by the front door. Possibly for days. Possibly for years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:She was the best Copper fighter Cael had ever seen. Her certificate said Copper Rank Eight, and had said Rank Eight for two years. Rank, like tier, was a word an Arbiter wrote on a certificate after an advancement evaluation; no bout on any ladder could add a number to it, and while her paper said Copper, the Copper bracket was the only bracket she could fight in. No registry had written a word about Fiske in two years, because nobody had asked one to. Lira had worked out in a wash-house, over a pair of ruined shirts, why nobody had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:189:"That's Gerda. Ash Path. She's been on the track most of a year." Lira held up her slip. "She told me to get the assessor's initials before the sixth bell, every time, or the clerk dates the result the next day, and if three of your results are dated a day late, somebody writes a note in your file asking why you can't keep a schedule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-10.md:177:*You say you think you took the way you set your feet from me. I expect you did. I'll tell you something. I took it from the man who had the bench before me. I laughed at him for it the whole first year. Then one day I looked down and I was doing it. I never knew who he took it from.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:61:The young man was at the duty board, reading the week's rota with his case in his left hand, ready to go out. He was standing as straight as he wrote, with nothing in his face at all, and he did not look like a man who had been told to stop asking a question the afternoon before. He looked like a man reading a rota, which was what he was doing, and Coss understood that this was what he had looked like every day for four years, and was the whole of his skill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:101:It was very thin. Between the registry's stamp and the green slip on its first leaf, the classification block still carried the line with nothing in its column: *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.* No officer had entered it, anywhere in the file or in the duty records of any floor. Nobody had dated it, and nobody had renewed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:181:Her mother had gone inside to ask the clerk about the guild enrollment inquiry. Cael knew the order of it from the procedural summary in the community hall: certificate, family notification, the residential registry updated, and then a visit to the guild of your Path to ask whether they had room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:215:The lamp burned down a little as he sat. Out in the main room the sweepers went over the stone, and the eel-market men's handcart went out at the side door with its pots knocking, and voices went past the arch saying *tomorrow*, and went on. He turned the pages back, past the fortnight's tallies and the clerk's account and Ansel's four words, and past the two columns, with *Going right* crossed out of *Shape* and written into *Habit* with its cost. He went past the three entries with their notices at the head, Wind and Pressure and Iron, and further, to the eight unasked instances carried from the old book, and the farmer, and *copies of real people, taken on real days*. A year and more of it. He had come down the hill with a bag and a direction, and this was what he had to show for it, and it was not nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-55.md:219:Vell read it, and then turned the ledger round to a clean page, dated it, and set down the terms beneath: the purse, the split, the same as any Sunday, which Cael had heard her say to a hundred fighters and said back to her now, word for word, standing at the table. It went until somebody put a hand up, or could not go on, or she called it. Once he was over the rope, nobody stopped it but her; what happened inside was his to carry, and nobody was making him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:155:*When a tool breaks with a noise, you learn where its edges are and keep your hands off them. When it breaks without one, it isn't broken. It's lying, and it lies in the same calm voice it uses for the truth. I've leaned on this one in the dark, in crowds, with my back to doors, for two years. Today it went blind to an entire Path and stayed perfectly calm about it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-41.md:211:It was the Thursday officers' table, the weekly one, in the long cold room on the second floor where the river-side and the Ranked-side officers sat for an hour together and read each other the week. Coss had sat at it every Thursday for eleven years and said, by his own count, about four things worth saying at it in all that time. He was there to be seen to be there. Most of the men round it were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:187:She did not read it all that night. She read it at the table, slowly, from the front, with the lamp pulled close and her hand flat beside the page and never on it. The three of them sat with her and did not talk. She read the first pages from Denvash. She read Lira's section and Brom's, and stopped at Brom's, and Brom looked at the fire. Then she came to the pocket at the back of the binder. She drew out what was hers in it, one sheet at a time, from among the papers that proved things, and laid each one on the table in the order it came. There was her own consent in her own hand with Quenna's initials in the margin. There was her paragraph with its scraped-out ghost of a sentence, and the page she had cut out of her own ledger with the penknife. Last came a loose sheet dated the Sunday before the hearing: the second copy of her finding about him, two sentences in her small closed hand, which she had pushed across the archive table that afternoon and told him to put somewhere she would never know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Last term he had stood up in a room full of people and read their own schedule back to them, from the first page to the last, and he had won because he had not reached past the text by a single line. If he let himself start wanting this clause to be alive, he would end by reading it the way the Compact's clerks had read his own file for two years: hoping, and writing the hope down as though it were a finding, and never once noticing the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:171:There were three places a repealed clause would have had to go. The first was the directive's own repeal schedule, the long numbered list at its back of everything it struck by name, and the second was the registry's consolidated list of repeals, which some patient clerk had drawn up twenty years later out of everything struck by anybody. The third was the guild digest, in which the member guilds had printed, with some bad temper, every clause they had ever lost. Karis gave him the guild digest because it was the worst printed of the three and the likeliest to hide something in small type. He read it with a ruler under each line, every entry in order and none of them softer than any other. It took him nine evenings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:73:So Cael sat on the stool at the end of the bench with a loupe in his eye and looked at keys, and the mender was right. A key that had turned every day for years had soft bright edges on its wards where the lock's own pins had worn it. A key that had never been used was sharp all over, and a key that had been used for a while and then put away had both kinds of edge at once, the bright ones going dull again. By the end of the afternoon he could sort the pocket keys from the drawer keys, and could not have told anybody how.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:3:At the regional registry office, on the fifth day after a brown envelope left a hill town on the third-day post, a clerk with a head cold opened it, read the sheet inside, and entered one line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:37:Put the same boy's name on the roll of an academy chartered by the Compact, and he was something different. The day that line went into the regional book, the boy had stepped off ground where a Warden could only look, onto ground where a Warden's writ could bite.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:69:Cael looked at the line about guild status, *pending Compact review*. He thought about all the years Hesk had spent being precisely himself, an Iron-tier instrument-maker, quiet and able and dependable, with canal gauges and survey levels and a clock inside a walking stick, and a name on the guild roll that nobody had ever had reason to look at twice. Three lines of print could put all of that in question, over something Cael had not chosen and could not have stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:129:*Of the four (4) recorded instances, disposition is as follows: three (3) practitioners are recorded as deceased within weeks of classification, cause of death listed as "practice-related incident" in Compact records. One (1) practitioner's disposition is recorded as unknown, last registry update dated 32 years prior to the present record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-60.md:97:He was very careful with himself about what it was, because he did not know. In the ordinary way a slip with that head meant that somebody above his floor, and above his section head's, wanted to know when the file moved; who, or why, it did not say. Whether it had anything to do with the mark Ilsev had found in the spring, he could not tell, though this one could at least be traced as far as a floor he had never been allowed to stand on. Whether it had anything to do with a chalk line in a clerk's copy of a board, or a Bronze lying down on raked dirt in front of three hundred people, or nothing at all, he could not tell either. A thing could be dated the day after another thing without being caused by it; he had told a great many people that, over the years, when they had come to him with two dates and a story.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:159:"Seven today." Brom sat down at the end of the bench, a little red along the jaw. "Wray says it's still not proof of anything. She says one column going down for a fortnight is a column going down for a fortnight, and the ceiling's a thing you prove across ranks and years, not days." He picked up his spoon. "Then she wrote the numbers on her own slate. I saw her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-10.md:77:*The person named in the registry notice of the second day no longer resides at this address.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:It was one of the small ones from the tray, from the desk that had gone for firewood, a little brass box key that Cael had put on the pile of keys that had lived in somebody's pocket every day for years. The mender laid it on the bench in front of him without a word, and beside it a small tin cash box with a dented lid, of the kind people kept under a floorboard.

