editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:113:[[TIER-B: the technician's sheet — the Compact date that stands after "Sequence initiated" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
   100	
   101	"The third is in the lid," said Vastin.
   102	
   103	Karis carried the lamp back up the table. The bare end went dim behind her, and the leaf lay there in the half dark on its square of oilcloth, where Cael could see that it was pale and no more.
   104	
   105	There was a pocket stitched inside the flat's lid, and out of it came a sheet of thin stock, folded once. It was newer than anything else that he had brought. It had a printed head, and under the head a few lines in a quick sloping hand, which leaned a little to the right, as a man writes who is used to writing on his knee.
   106	
   107	"It is a technician's return," said Vastin. "The stations' office keeps a few men whose business is the function; they are the only people in the Compact who are sent as far down as that. When one of them finds a thing that he was not sent to find, he is bound to write it up on such a sheet, on the day, and send it in. This is one of those, and I do not understand it; I have read it at every halt for three hundred miles, and I understand it no better than I did at the first."
   108	
   109	He laid it before her, and the lamp showed it to the whole table.
   110	
   111	*Deep layer. Sequence initiated*
   112	
   113	[[TIER-B: the technician's sheet — the Compact date that stands after "Sequence initiated" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
   114	
   115	*Unreadable. Not halted. Running. Correlated with classification event —*
   116	
   117	It stopped there, at a stroke of the pen. Across everything that had followed, somebody had drawn a brush. The bar was as broad as a thumb and as black as a hearth-back, and it had been laid on so thickly that the ink had cracked in drying, like mud in a rut.
   118	
   119	"What's under it?" said Lira.
   120	
   121	"I do not know. I held it to a window, and to a candle, and it has been laid on both sides." Vastin put one raw finger beside the bar, as Brom had put his beside the leaf. "This is the one thing on that sheet which I can read, and I had better tell you why. A clerk who withdraws a word strikes it once, with a pen, so that it can still be read beneath the stroke; the code requires that, and I taught it to every junior that I had. That is not a clerk's stroke. It is a brush. I have seen it on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years, and I never once saw initials beside it. It is laid on by somebody who has the authority to make a thing unsaid, and who does not care to be known for having used it."
   122	
   123	"What's a classification event?" said Brom.
   124	
   125	"The office's phrase for any occasion upon which a person is classed. A Kindling is one. So is a rank confirmed, or a second reading. There are some thousands of them in a year." His hand came away. "A technician found a thing in the layer that had begun on a certain day, and was going on. That is the whole of what those lines say, and I can add nothing to them. He had begun to set down what it went with, when the brush came down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:29:The stewards at the north tunnel let him through without a word, as they had let him through every evening since somewhere in the second week, when they had decided that the boy who walked the empty floors at night was part of the building. He went up into the dark of the lower tiers and along to the rail above the north break, where the five of them had stood the day before. He put both arms on it and looked down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:185:He had kept his hands at his sides, and he would go on keeping them there. On the day the question became properly his, if such a day came, he would ask it in a room with the door shut and tell the boy first. Not before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:She came at him high and low, with forearm and knee and a low hardened shin to the side of his knee that would have folded a fair-day man. She had a great deal, and all of it was good.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:221:"That it isn't sure yet what you are," said Karis. "And that it's trying very hard not to show it." She made a small mark in the margin. "Look at the third floor's day board. They chalked the word on it last night and somebody rubbed it out this morning and chalked it again. Smaller."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:253:"No." Ephram hung the towel round his neck. "They're here for the entry. Every one of them has a second-year entry, and every one of them throws it the old way, shoulders first. Somebody in that knot is going to go home tonight and stand in front of a glass for ten days." He seemed pleased by the thought. "And then the next time we meet, she'll throw it at me with her shoulders still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He did not put it in the book. It was only a column in his head, and each day it was shorter than the day before. On the first day it had run to a dozen things: metalled stone underfoot, ditches, dressed milestones with the district's device, a keeper at the ford with a stamp, a chartered sign, a board of hours. On the second day there had been a post bag on a hook, sealed in lead, and a notice of tolls in the registry's print, and a rider in district grey going west at a trot, who had not looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:83:"Who stamps the carters' sheets?" said Lira. "They told me the stamps ran as far as the station. That's a day on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:195:"Rooke told me." Brom laughed, one short breath. "Day three. Before Ephram had even found it. Told me over the water jugs." He hesitated, and then did something Cael had never seen him do in two years of roads and boarding houses and yards. He unwound his left wrap, a few turns, and held the inside of it toward the last of the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:211:"I've got the sixth day charted," said Cael. "One exchange, from his first step to the touch. Three sales and a delivery. If you want it—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-59.md:135:He knew the hand from the door, but not the date until he picked it up and counted, standing in the kitchen with it in his hand. It had been written on the Sunday before last, after Hesk had sat three days with the letter from the night of the fourth stair, and it had gone into the east bag the best part of a week before Darrow Innes came into the yard. Hesk had not known how any of it would go, and he did not know now. The bout was a day old, and Hesk would not know for a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-59.md:143:*You told me what you mean to do on Sunday fortnight, and who against, and why. I've read it till the folds have gone soft. I'm frightened, and I won't pretend I'm not, because you've never once in your letters pretended not to be, and I'd be ashamed to do less. By the time this reaches you it'll be over, one way or the other, and I'll be sitting here not knowing which. I find I can bear that better than I'd have thought. I'll tell you why.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-59.md:149:*I said I'd say it properly when I'd found the words. I thought they'd be about the fight. They aren't. I don't know how the fight went. I know how you went into it: loud, and told, and with her beside you because you'd asked her, even if you asked her late. That's the thing I'm proud of. It would be the same thing whichever way Sunday went, so I can say it now, before I know, and not have to take any of it back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-59.md:159:The heavyset man had the lodgers' book open on the hall table on the Thursday morning, before it was light, with the lamp beside it and his pen in his hand. He had not been to bed. Cael did not think he had been to bed since the bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:She had gone to Ephram. Ephram, as captain, still held the meal rota the two second-years had drafted on the first night, the one with a column of names and a table chosen for watching how Cael held a spoon. Lira asked him for it. She took it to the common room, crossed out the heading, and wrote a new one above it in her large capitals. *RECORDS HALL STEPS. ONE PAIR AT A TIME. NOBODY SITS. NOBODY ASKS. ANYBODY ELSE IS SENT TO THE FRAMES.* Then she gave it back to Ephram to sign, and he signed it, and pinned it up on the covered walk beside the house's one-line answer from the day before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:The first was Karis's own finding from Greyvane, the long one, the one she had built from the standardization directive's repeal schedules a line at a time until she could say, flatly, in front of a seat, that nobody had ever repealed the clause. It had been left behind, like a child at a fair, when everything round it was swept up and carried off. The second was the house counsel's reconstruction, made the year after, by another road, through the registry's own index of surviving provisions, without reading a word of Karis's first. The third was Prynn's copy and the note on its cover, from the archive at Greyvane, which showed the clause standing in the founding text with nothing written against it in two hundred years of hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:153:It was full; there was no room in it for one word more. But every word that was in it was still there, and he knew roughly where in its thick back half the inspection year began. He turned the pages under the lamp, past fights and figures and the names of rooms, to the long entry for the evaluation day, the one that ended on *wondering*. Then he turned forward a page at a time, counting the days between entries by the bells and the weekday names, until he found two short lines he had nearly forgotten writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:And on the way down the stair he remembered the counter, and the four sentences Seln had spent on him in a queue on the third day of the year. *Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check. Keep the documentation current.* The renewals table was the provision's standing papers, set out in a grid. Whatever went down the hill this week would sit in the registry's file beside the registry's own dates when the half-year check came, and a table that disagreed with them by nine days would have been the first thing the check found. Karis had not heard Seln say it. She had kept the documentation current anyway, because that was what she did with documents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Ephram came off his mark with the entry he had built at the mill town. It was the second-year entry with its two weights, and then, after the heel, the late half-turn of the front foot that had won him a title. Cael had watched him work it in the loading yard at first light the day before, again and again, until it was as smooth as anything Ephram owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:53:He stopped trying to draw the Stone out of the stove end, and went to him instead. He went straight across the floor toward the west rope and the warm flags, and as he went Cael saw him see the thing Karis had not been able to see from the floor the day before.
