editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:*He knows I found him. He must. And he has gone on standing in his crowds every day since, on the same posts, at the same bells, keeping himself hard to see for four hundred people who were never looking. So it was never me the Path was for. It's for everybody else, so that I'm the only one who can see him, and I've agreed to keep my mouth shut. I've been trying for a week to think of a better answer to being caught. I can't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:A year ago, in his first months, the column of things not told had been five long. It had sat in him like a stone in a boot all winter, until, in the spring, he had put all five in one letter and sent it over the hills. He had promised then to tell the next thing as it came. He went through the letter now in his head, line by line, as he went through the exchanges of a bout, and looked for anything he had left out because it was easier left out. The trials: told. The rule: told. The notice changing its story: told, and told straight, with the part he did not understand left in as not understood. The fan and its empty quarter: told. The farmer: told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:121:There had been a pear tree, and a book with a red spine. It was a history of the coastal leagues, and she had taken it off the shelf of the feeder school's library without quite asking. She was thirteen. She was reading it on the bench under the pear tree on the south side of the garden court, in the empty hour after the midday meal, because the court was nearly always empty then, and she was nearly always alone in it. She was already a year ahead of everybody she was taught with, and taller than most of them, and she had found that a bench nobody else wanted was the most comfortable place in the school.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:She had been Auremont's since twelve, a year before the garden. The house had seen something in a set of entrance papers and sent for her, and kept her near, and waited for the rest. Afterwards nothing in her life had been left to happen by itself. Floors were relaid to her measure. An evaluator who wanted a look at her had his station opened before the day began, and came, and looked. Whole classes were assembled round her and broken up again when she grew past them. Other people spoke of climbing; she had never once had to look for the next rung. It was always there, put down where her foot would go, by people who were very good at their work. At Auremont the day of the garden court was a half-holiday now, with a demonstration in the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:97:There were two sheets in it, and he knew them both. He logged them at his place at the foot, with the brass glass lying on its side, in four minutes, because that was how long it took to do it properly. The return had come in the night before, and he had entered it then; but it had moved today, from the file to the senior seat's hand and back, and a thing that moves gets a line. Then the new referral, out, one level higher. He gave the new referral a reference to the return, and the return a reference to the Greyvane referral, and the Greyvane referral, already in the ledger from a year ago, a reference forward to both. Three sheets, each pointing at the other two, so that whoever lifted any one of them would be led to the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/skeleton-r2.txt:168:    [s2 0.71] MS: She kept it old and short: first-year patterns, footwork his feet could have found asleep, the evasion frames from the Ardenmere yard before either of them had heard the word Greyvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/skeleton-r2.txt:169:              SRC: The dusk drills were short, and she kept them old — first-year patterns, footwork he could have run asleep, the evasion frames from before either of them had ever heard the word Greyvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:23:Along the row to her left, Havel was writing something small in the margin of his attendance sheet. Beyond him the other seats were emptying for the midday interval. At the end of the row the twelfth chair stood empty behind the grey rail-cloth, as it had stood every day of the sitting, with its pale square and its two empty screw holes. Ilsev did not look at it. She had a referral of her own still pending somewhere above her desk, the second she had ever filed on a single matter, and it had taught her a great deal about the difference between what a record said and what a record was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:39:The second floor was full again, though not as full as it had been for Lira. The broadsides had found the story the day before, *former partners*, and the city liked the shape of it. The light had changed since the morning. The sun had gone round to the west, and it came down through the high glass now in long pale panes that lay across the eastern half of the floor and moved, very slowly, as the afternoon went on, so that a fighter standing still would feel the warmth come onto her shoulder and go off it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:"Caelen Hesk-ward," said Cael. "Registration 41-7843-V."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:289:She had asked for the case file's supporting documentation after the midday meal, the whole bundle, and the Warden's aide had brought it in three tied stacks and gone away again. She had untied the first and begun at the top. She was reading it as Havel had heard she read everything and had never before watched her do: in order, every page, without skipping, past the place where anybody else would have decided they had the shape of it and started turning faster.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:327:"You recorded on this file before," she said. "I saw your name on a visit record. A district compliance visit, more than a year ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:7:He had his own line for the day already, at the head of the page, under the date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:73:"We're on a five-year round, like every chartered house of our size. Our last visit was a little over two years ago. It was two officers and three days, and they found us in full compliance, but for one remark about how the fire-watch keeps its oil book. By the round, nobody is due here for nearly three years. They are coming anyway, and the notice doesn't say why. It doesn't have to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:229:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Yesterday morning. In the reading room downstairs." He hesitated, and then told her the rest, because it was true and she had asked. "The pages hadn't been cut. The man who keeps it gave me a knife."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:"I don't know." She looked at him over the folder. "I'm telling you that plainly because it's true. Nobody has ever asked them this question. I can't tell you what they'll answer." She wrote something on the top sheet, two lines, in a small upright hand. "In the meantime, you're not required to appear anywhere, or to change anything about where you live or what you do. The order of the fifty-fifth day stands: closed, pending. It goes on pending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:243:"When I put your file into standard procedure at regional, to start this evaluation, it came back from the docket with a flag on it." She touched the corner of the face-down sheet with one finger, without turning it over. "A mark against the record. I didn't ask for it. I'd looked at the file the day before, when it was given to me, and the record was clean. The flag went on when I opened the procedure. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:195:"I watched you yesterday with Ansel. It caught him coming at a pace, and he's quicker than Brom will ever be." She turned the staff in her hands. "And I'm coming at you every morning with nothing before it, which is the whole of what the man on Tuesday does. And you've been holding that thing shut every morning, all week, because I told you not to go into me, the night of Maud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:The queue was four long. A first-year girl stood at the front of it with a folded paper in both hands, holding it the way you hold a thing you have been told twice not to lose. She was small, with a scarf wound three times round her neck against the cold and a Wind badge on her coat so new its pin still shone. The desk clerk was busy with the day book, so it was the teaching assistant who rose from the copying table to take it from her, as he did when the clerk was busy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:129:Bracken brought the Greyvane leaves up the library stair himself on the Seventh-day, at noon, all four flights, which he had not done since the birthday parcel.
