editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:61:She did not go down to the midday meal, all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Seln was interested in the pin. On the walk back from the hall it had filled his head. A tired pin in a nine-year collar, found in a term when an inspection might come at any time, with the posts chained and the warden's draft already on its way to faculty, was the best weather he had recorded since the posting began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:The paper itself was a day gone. It had ridden down the bluff road in a saddlebag yesterday morning, after a minute and forty seconds at the lodge window in front of one man with a glass, and whatever it had to say about itself it had said then, to him, and to nobody since. Seln had worked that out at about the fire-watch's second round, sitting up in the wing with the senior clerk's fair copy under the lamp and getting nothing from it but the words, which was all a fair copy on the house's own stock could ever give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:Three lines, in his best hand, from a long table at Halcenvane, written well over a year ago. *Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first.* And under them, in Karis's wording from the last night at Norhold, with four sets of initials after it: *Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:153:Karis stood on her mark with her palms turned up and open, letting the air at them. Cael watched her look across at the big man and saw that she knew exactly what he was doing, and that knowing did not help. She had nothing left to change into. She had changed three times already that afternoon, and the day before, and the store she drew on was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:203:In the middle of it all, untouched, lay the round of old dark oak where Brom had stood the day before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:141:Karis had proposed it on the Friday night, at the end of the eleventh session, a short one after supper, in the voice she used for proposing a variable. Every session so far had run an hour or less. None had run past the point where her sustain began to cost her. If duration was a condition, it was one they had not yet tested at its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:145:"Until I can't lay a clean point. Then a little longer, so that we know what not clean looks like." She closed the ledger. "Saturday. After the midday meal. Bring something to eat; you'll be standing a long time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:201:"Fourteen," she said, when she had finished. "Fourteen boundaries." She did not sound tired, though she had been grey at the fingertips the day before. She sounded like somebody a long way into difficult country with a map that, so far, was right. "Every one of them is a finding. A boundary condition you know is worth more than a hope you don't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:Dace gave him the barge lad for the Thursday afternoon, with a look at the two names together on his slate that said he knew perfectly well what this was and would not ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-38.md:237:"Thursday week," he said. "Main floor. I'll clear the card."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:"Places at the rope. Somebody on the coast has read my season card and wants to see a name on it with their own eyes." Dace looked at the one word in the square. "You'll be a long way from the coast on a Thursday night, and you'll have people from it watching you. That's what a card's for, I suppose. I never used to think about where it went after it left my hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-38.md:249:"Go and tell Vell," said Dace. "She'll want to know which book. And Cael." He was rubbing chalk off his fingers with the slate cloth, and did not look up. "Sleep before Thursday week. I don't want anybody on my main floor who's been up all night thinking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:19:"Be dull," said Bracken. "Go to your lectures. Train in your hours and sign for them. Hand your work in on the day it's due, and not a day early, because early is interesting. Don't put your name to a single sheet the calendar didn't ask for." He leaned his forearms on the counter. "Strong files are made of dull months, Enrollee. Give me a dull month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:The first sheet was a copy of the registration. *Caelen Hesk-ward. Registration 41-7843-V.* The certifying office was on a street called Weaver's Row, in Denvash, and the officer was a Warden-Adjunct named Pellin, whose signature was small and quick and gave nothing away. The date of the Kindling was in the autumn, fifty-odd days ago. On the line for the classification there was one word in square brackets, stamped, and no tier, no rank and no Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:209:He spent the rest of the day walking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:219:He filed it where he filed such things. Every district had its Sunday entertainment, dice in a back room or dogs in a yard, and it was none of the Compact's business. He did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:53:On the Sunday morning, before the first bell, Karis came out to the training yard with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:Oona found him at the board in the main hall on the Sunday afternoon, in front of the calendar, which had been her office far longer now than it had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:231:He wrote to Hesk on the Sunday evening, at the desk by the window, because Hesk had said to write when something happened, and the last letter had said nothing had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:He read it back. There was nothing in it that was untrue and nothing a stranger could have made anything of, and the whole week was in it all the same, folded small. He wrote the Denvash direction on the outside and set it on the corner of the desk for Lira to take down on the third-day post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:It was a week to the day since the main floor, a Tuesday again, grey and very cold, with a skin of ice on the water barrel by the side door that the night man had broken with his heel. They had the alcove to themselves for two hours before the sweepers. Brom had hung the lamp, and taken his coat off, and stood on his cross and said nothing at all, not even *what are you testing*, because Cael had told him on the walk down from the corner. *All of it. What you are when you're not being kind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:230:He sat down across from her and opened the Log to the morning's page and turned it round, as he had turned it round for her a hundred times in a year, and she read the notice. She read it with her lips not quite moving. When she got to the *Note* she read it twice, and then she looked up at him with an expression he had not seen on her since the day, a year ago, when he first turned the old Log round on a table and let her read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:It was Karis. The words came out of her like a gate swinging on a dry hinge, each with a creak in the middle of it, and all four of them turned to look at her. She had spoken three words on the day before. She touched her throat, and frowned at it, and went on regardless, since it was plain that she had been saving this up since first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-39.md:153:He watched Dellin a third time on Tuesday afternoon, from the rope, near the barrel, with the Log open on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-39.md:257:Dellin came off his mark and the left came high, and Cael went outside it, and the right came low, and found nothing. For the third time in three exchanges it found nothing. Then Dellin did exactly what he had done to the Shield on Tuesday afternoon, and to two other men on a Thursday and a Sunday in the third week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-39.md:337:"While you were writing. She came out to the gate to tell me, because she said you looked busy." Lira blew on her cup. "Thursday week. The last week. The last bout of your six." She was quiet for a moment. "Do you know who Corbin is?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:39:He did not think any further along that road. He had not let himself walk down it in twelve years except on the worst nights, and this was a weekday morning, and there was post to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:167:"There'll be six names in the First-day column," he said. "There always are. Ephram has the Crown yard from the second bell every First-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:183:It was not the last of its kind. He knew that while he was tying the tape. He would write another next week, and the one after, and every week until the year closed, true and empty, in the old pattern, because the pattern was what the office expected and a pattern that changed was a thing somebody wrote down. Nothing in his life was going to look different from the outside. That was the other half of the catechism, the half she had never needed to say aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:Karis looked at her for a moment. Then she looked round the long room, at the second-years by the fire and the hatch and the cook in the doorway. She seemed to decide that a single point the size of a coin, at a stable table at the midday meal, was nobody's business but her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:237:She ran the seventeenth alone with him on the Wednesday morning, before the first bell, with nobody at the door. Halfway through it she did something he had never seen her do in seventeen sessions. She laid a point a fraction early, before her own count, the way a person speaks a fraction early into a silence where somebody has always replied. Then she stood perfectly still for a moment, her off hand motionless at her side, and looked at the empty air across the room where nobody stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:89:"He has kept every rule of a forty-year career," he said, in his counter voice. "If he means to break one for you, the breaking has been paid for somewhere you will never be shown the bill." He shifted the pouch. "Listen accordingly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:53:There was nothing in it; it was the most ordinary line the form allowed. A briefing officer meets that line at the foot of one report in three, the year round, and ticks it the way a man nods to a neighbour. All it would do was leave a Bronze clerk at his copying table, minding other men's mistakes, through another term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:99:He had answered this in his sleep. He had answered it with barley on his chin. He had answered it wrong, once, in the stable on the Saturday night, hot and true and no use at all, and felt the whole argument give under him at one place. Then Brom, in his own voice, had asked him what the code did when what people did and what was written came apart. He answered it now, looking at her and at nobody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:43:They looked at the title, which had one name on it, and at the licences. One of them, while the other wrote, took up a brass caliper from the tray by the window and tried its jaws, and asked what it cost. He was told. It was the price on the ticket, which was a third more than such a thing had cost the year before, for brass had gone up, and he paid it. The old man wrote him a receipt and wrapped the caliper in a twist of paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:201:The Bronze from the town fought on the Second-day morning, and the floor was full for him before the first bell.
