editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:"I'm on the card," Lira said. "Second bout. Sunday, before the lamps." She was looking at the post, not at him, as she did when a thing mattered more than she wanted it to look. "I asked Vell on Friday morning, while you were at the mender's being told something worked. She told me last night it was on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:The yard was fuller than it had any right to be for a second bout on a cold Sunday afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:The Tuesday card was an ordinary Tuesday card, and that was the best thing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-58.md:152:The other three had come as strangers. Wind had arrived after a winter of Lira's mornings, and he had not known what it was or how to call it, and had spent half a year learning. Pressure had arrived after a hand on his shoulder in a yard, and he had spent months finding out it had two faces. Iron had arrived after three mornings of Brom hitting him for science, and he had spent a whole autumn learning to knock. Each of them had been a door in a wall he had not known had doors. He had opened each one and found a room he did not know, and had to learn its size in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:133:She filed the transfer request before the midday bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:193:The lodge-man stood as straight as he stood for the Chancellor. The intake crowded the north wall to watch them take the first switchback, and the second, and the bend above the trees. Lira was on the wall with them, and Brom, and somewhere behind Cael on the walk a third-year said, half laughing and half not, *Is that it? Is that the whole answer?* and somebody else said, *That's eleven pounds of it,* and nobody laughed after that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:139:The office numbered its halls on paper, as offices do, though nobody on the bluff ever said the numbers aloud. Hall one was the north hall, on stone. Hall three was the east, on timber. And his Sixth-day slot, the coming Sixth-day, the eightieth, which had been hall three every week since the allocation began, said hall one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:Under his knuckles, this time, it was not like the floor over the river. It was like the gate of the market on a Saturday morning, when the carts have all come at once, and the first is still wedged in the gateway when the second runs up against its tail, and the third against the second. Nothing about it was weak. It was only full. Everything he had put into that place was still in it, trying to get down to a foot, and the ninth had nowhere to go but on top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:400:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page at Greyvane and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:424:"I asked Hesk. In a letter. A year ago." With her good heel Lira dragged the next chair back from the table. "Karis wanted it for a column, at Greyvane, and came to me, because she thought of course I'd know. And I didn't. All that time, and I didn't know what day you were born, and you'd never once let it slip, and you never would, not unless somebody put it on a form in front of you with a pen. I was so angry about that I couldn't sleep. So I wrote to the one man who'd tell me. He answered by the next coach. Three lines: the date, and *thank you*, and *he won't have told you*. You people treat information like it's rationed."
