editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:421:"We came up here to qualify," he said. "That's what this year has been. Four meets, and get over a line before the year turns. Three meets are done. I think we're close enough to see the line. I'll believe we're across it when it comes up the road on a circular, and I'll not believe it a day before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:They were the speckled brown beans the kitchen soaked for the Second-day stew. He had taken them out of his coat pocket without any hurry. He laid them a hand's breadth apart, in front of his own place at the middle of the bench, and now he sat back from them with his hands folded and looked at none of the four people across the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:145:"The question is what we do the day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:51:"The weighbeam." She pulled the strap again, gently. "At the foundry, before every pour, the master had them prove the beam. There was a set of brass weights in a box with a lock on it, and only he had the key, and before anybody put a single ingot on the beam he'd hang the brass on it and see what it said. My second year there, a beam had been filed. Just a little, on the pivot. Some man had been selling us short for months, and nobody knew, because everybody trusted the beam." She shifted the leg and winced and went on. "After that the master wouldn't believe any number at all until he'd watched the beam proved. Not even his own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:*The case came back to the wing with his stamp on it and nothing else. Seln, at the copying table, writes true lines about me that can't hurt me. Then the Archmarshal takes them, reads every line, and hands them back clean. That holds as long as he goes on not marking them, and not one day longer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:Karis had found the clause most of a year ago, at Greyvane, not in any academy's charter but further down, in the old schedule of powers that sat underneath every chartered academy like a foundation under a house, carried up into each academy's charter by a single standard line. So Bracken began at the top and went down. First he laid out Halcenvane's own charter, open at that line, with a tab in it, and read the line's number aloud for the record. He had done it before the counsel had finished turning to ask for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:163:"He knew it was there. He's known for four days." She heard the edge in her own voice and did not try to take it out. "He just wanted to see it from where Edran's fist was. Because he doesn't trust the fourth day anymore."
