editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:*Day eleven. It idles, and idling isn't off. The first thing to learn isn't use but holding ordinary, and that is paid for from waking to sleep.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:193:"It's like watching somebody blow on a coal," said Brom afterward, at the post, while Cael fed him exercise one again. He had finished all nineteen and begun them over from the start, and he said exercise one was a holiday now. "For three steps you're nothing. Grey. Then you start reading, and you glow. From where I stand you're the brightest thing on that walk the moment you start trying to be part of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:*Shadow-adjacent, Bronze tier: working ledger. Day one. Deployment: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:117:"Somebody sat where I'm sitting," she said. "Once. Some keeper somewhere, with some old shelf, and the first article open, and *relevant to the matter* under her finger. And she won. She took that word and wrung it until it gave, and the clerks went home with empty boxes. And the next year, or the year after, a man in a drafting office read the digest of what she'd done, and sat down, and wrote this." She touched the amendment, very lightly. "It's a scar. I taught a boy that at this table, a long time ago. A patch sits exactly where somebody once broke through." Her mouth moved, though nothing came of it. "I'd give a great deal to know who she was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:157:"With what?" She got down off the chair, slowly, one hand on the table's edge. "You can't stop a seizure with a deposition. You've one of those tomorrow, as I recall, and you'll want to come out of it with your hands as empty as you took them in." She went to the high desk and set the enforcement volume on it, square to the edge. "The girl's on those shelves. Go and tell her what's coming before the clerks come up the hill and tell her themselves. Thursday morning, at the front of the stock, and they'll box as they go. She'll want every hour she's got. Tell her I said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:209:"Thursday," said Karis, before he had sat down. "From the front. And box as they catalogue." She did not look up from the paper, though her finger had stopped moving on it. "The traveller registers are at the back, against the wall. I've three cases of them left. If they start at the front and work at the speed a clerk works, I've until Saturday. Perhaps Friday night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:39:In the second she changed her plan, and it was a good change. She had watched Brom for a week, and she had learned what every fighter learns who watches him long enough, which is that he does not chase. So she stopped coming to him and made him come to her. She stood off at the very edge of his reach and waited, and every time he came she was gone, a step to the side and back again with the shin. It was a fair-day trick, Cael thought, the kind a fighter learns in a ring of hay bales when the purse depends on never being caught. She took a touch with it, low on the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:77:Brom thought about that. "She earned the semifinal. I only gave her the rating." He folded the wraps over his arm. "Somebody ought to file for her. Somebody ought to have done it a year ago, at the wool town, and I'd like to know why nobody has."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:"A corridor," he said, "in a district station, a year on, with green distemper on the walls to the height of a man's shoulder, and benches. I was waiting for a file. A keeper came out of the frame room with a mother and a girl, and he was pleased, the way they are when a reading is a good one, and I heard him tell the mother that her girl read strong, and read into a second family as well, and he had not seen it in years." He looked at the father's letter, folded under its clip in the third row. "A week later I was back for another file, and the station's out-tray was on the counter, and her certificate was in it, waiting to go. One family, first rank." He took his hand off the table. "That was when the paper turned into children. It had been only paper for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:125:*To CAELEN HESK-WARD, registration 41-7843-V, resident at the lodging house known as Torvin's, Unranked District, Ardenmere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:191:"I ask what they mean *here*. It's slower." She almost smiled, and then did not. "Thursday morning. Before the post. I'll come to Torvin's step at first light, and you'll read me whatever you've found, out loud, the way you did before, and I'll tell you which bits sound like you're frightened."
