editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:181:"The rest stays where we put it," said Karis, when he had blotted the page. "The minute from the year before. Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first." She looked at him steadily. "A bigger fight doesn't change a word of that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:201:Nobody wrote it down, and nobody ever would. What the four of them owed the man at the counter would go on being owed, as it had been since a bad night on a stair a year ago, near enough: kept off him the way you keep rain off somebody by holding a coat over them without saying you are holding it. They had only ever had one coin to pay it in. They paid it again tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:33:*Caelen Hesk-ward. 41-7843-V.* He wrote the number slowly, digit by digit, and checked it against the copy of the ruling in the binder, because he had learned that the number you were surest of was the one you were likeliest to get wrong. He wrote *Denvash* and the date. He wrote the designation in brackets, *[SHATTERED]*, as the station had written it, and under it a line saying that the panel at Greyvane had found it did not appear anywhere in the enumerated schedule, with the page of the ruling where it said so. He wrote *Hesk*, and then, because Bracken had asked how it was spelled, he wrote it again in capitals, and then felt foolish, and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:She read it once, and looked at him over the top of it without saying anything, and then folded it into her answers with the rest. When the bundle went down the hill on the Wednesday coach, that line was in it, in his own hand, on its way to a man he had never met, and he found he did not mind at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:The new Edran's third came up whole. Cael had seen it on the last free afternoon of the winter term and seen it every day since. But that was not what kept him awake. What kept him awake was the lower stance and the off arm carried forward, and the fact that Edran no longer drove his shells out to meet anything. He let them come to him. He had given up perhaps a hand of reach to do it, and Cael could not see what the hand had bought him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:15:"All of us," he said. "Before the year closes. By a door that's in the code, and in an old enrollment book. Karis found it in the term before the recess, and it leaves nothing in that folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:25:*Notice of date, to the Chancellor, by a district officer: upon the ruling, or the day after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:27:*Evaluation: the Fourth-day following. The last day of the academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:87:"No. I said in the term that I'd be annoyed if you thanked me, and I should be." She had her hand on the door. "The captain came to me at supper to ask for the Crown yard tomorrow at the fourth bell. I told him the yard was Rooke's to give and the bout was yours, and I see by your face that he has since asked you. I shall be in the stand. I have sat under a rug in other people's halls for two years to watch my own fighters, and I should like to see one thing on my own oak before the year closes."
