editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:*Caelen Hesk-ward, 41-7843-V, enrolled practitioner; see demonstration provision, enrollment basis, filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:He did it the same way for everybody. He took the form. He read it, every line, at an even pace, from the name to the signature. He stamped the received line, tore off the copy-slip along its rule, and gave the slip back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, and the Mire third-year what the first-year got, down to the second it took to read the reason for the hours. Cael had worked out in his second month on the bluff what that was for. A man who read every form from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:223:Before he wrote the day up, he wrote to Hesk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:5:The river went down from brown to green. The pear tree in the corner of Torvin's yard opened its small hard buds into flowers, white, with a smell like a clean shirt. Then a frost came one night and turned half of them brown at the edges, and Torvin's wife stood in the yard in the morning and looked at them and said a word Cael had not heard her use before, which was *ungrateful*. The barges came down every day now, and the errand boys did the lists together on the bottom step, arguing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:39:It had lain on the corner of his desk at regional for four weeks, under the window that looked onto the wall of the records building. It sat squared to the edge, as she had squared it when she put it there on the morning after she came back from Ardenmere, and he had read it on the first day twice, and again on the fourth, and then once a week or so since. Each time he had told himself that he was reading it for its procedure, which was very good. Each time he had read the procedure quickly and come to rest on the last paragraph but one, and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:63:"Legal will take the rest of the year." The section head had looked at the report a while longer. "The boy fights, I hear. In some ring in the Unranked District. A Sunday thing. A book kept by somebody nobody's licensed." He had said it lightly, and it had not been light. "It isn't the law that troubles anybody. The law's with legal, and legal will say what it says. But nobody above this floor wants a classification like that standing up in an unsanctioned ring every Sunday while they're saying it." He had paused. "If the subject were to remove himself to somewhere smaller, of his own accord, the file would close itself. A closure report on voluntary relocation. Nobody would need to wait on legal at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:93:The man sat down across from Cael at the narrow table by the pie man's stall on Saturday morning, and Cael knew him by his walk before he knew him by his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:He had been sitting there a quarter of an hour, after the grey half, with two loaves from underneath and a twist of the fruit woman's apricots and nothing at all in his head, which was a thing he had been learning to do. The market was loud and slow around him. It was the first warm Saturday of the year, and people had come down to it who did not need anything, only to walk about in the sun and look at things and be seen looking. He had been watching them without meaning to. He had been watching the way a man in a brown coat came down the second row between the stalls, unhurried, reading the bread woman's high cracked lantern as if it were a notice, and he had thought, idly, *that man reads things the way they're written*. Then the man had turned out of the row toward the pie stall, and Cael had seen the set of his shoulders, and the brown coat had stopped mattering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:They did the last pass after breakfast, at the trestle by the fire, with the stable empty and the second-years gone up the hill to the town for the Sunday market. Karis sat behind her bench of books. Brom sat at the end in Coss's stillness. Lira sat on the bench with her boots up and the stable clock in her eye. Cael stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:99:Prynn was on her high stool with her ledger open. She watched him set the two armfuls down on the end of her desk, and checked each spine against its slip as she had checked the others the day before, and then she did not say anything at all. She only looked toward the back of the room, past the pillar, where the code's case stood by the old wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:111:She took the top volume off the stack, the newest, the one that had been buttoned inside her coat on Thursday night, and opened it at the back. She did not open it at the copies of the register but at the very last written page, and turned it round, and slid it across the table to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:179:They did not fight, exactly. There was nobody to throw anything and nobody to count, and the frozen ruts were uneven under his boots where the broom had not reached. They went through the oldest patterns there were, the first-year forms, his evasion framework against her Wind at the speed of somebody walking home. Then they went a little faster, and then, without either of them saying so, at the speed they had used on the circuit, when there had been a ledger and a crowd and a purse. His feet found the ruts and stopped minding them. On the fourth turn the pivot that had been a hair late all week came exactly on time, and he felt it come, and she saw him feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:3:Author: Monroe Jackson (seat `oconnor` 1.3.0, model `claude-opus-5-5`) · 2026-10-02 (one session; see AUTHORSHIP.md)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:16:| 34 | Efficient | 4,622 | Day 56, the grey half. Cael lays out the arithmetic (at most six bouts, more honestly five). Lira's terms: **"Faster doesn't mean reckless. It means efficient."** Lose cheaply; never get hurt in a way that costs the next one; the Log turns round every night; she says what is survivable. **The mender** (Wednesday): a crate of hinges sorted into true and scrap; *IF IT'S STILL HERE* read differently; "Monday… And after?" / "I don't know yet." Day 57: **Vell names Tuesday's opponent**, an unnamed Copper 2 Ember kiln boy, "because you've already been taught Ember once". Cael watches him on Thursday: the shimmer, the breath stopped **every** time, and he looks where he means to hit. Day 58: **"Gone" begins**, chalk marks on the post; the girls on the wall mimic it. **Lira demonstrates his mistake back at him**: his private nod before he moves ("Anybody who's watched you twice could time you by it. Renn will have."). Day 60, the turn of the month: Renn is held above the weir; **the hold lapses**; Vell: "Then ask him. Not me." Watched a second time. Day 62, **the opener (~650)**: called in the third exchange, inside the held breath, the one strike stopped at the jaw. "How'd you know where I'd be?" / "You look." Result read as procedure, with no extra lines. Lira: easy happens, it is not a method. Log entry 7: *It worked because he had never been told… Don't learn the wrong thing from this.* |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:17:| 35 | The Second Look | 7,278 | Day 64: the barges come down. Renn, half-bearded, takes "full credit" for the kiln boy and spots Lira's work in Cael's stance. **Cael asks him himself** for the rebook (coordinator note); Renn: "Come and look, if you like… Because it won't help you." Vell cards it for Sunday; Renn has half-shaved. Day 65, **Renn's public preparation** in the middle yard, thirty at the gate: a friend plays Cael, nod included. Renn drills the no-probe figure from the mark and the **false heel** ("If he goes when the heel goes, I get him landing. If he waits, I get him waiting"), and promises "no third exchange". Cael sees Renn's **shoulder roll** still there. **The counter is built first**: go sideways left on the word, never watch the heel ("Gone"), read the shoulders, make a plan built to end in two pay for five. Lira: "That's not a plan. That's a promise to have another plan." Day 66: Lira plays Renn; twenty-six chalk marks. The old man: "Poling. That's arms and back. It isn't legs." **Hesk's Dessa reply** arrives (by the salt crock); its line sits exactly over the unwritten fourth exchange. Day 67, **Renn II (~2,800 + aftermath)**: (1) sideways; Renn turns the figure; the flat lands across the old burn. (2) The false heel; the old groove pulls; **"Gone," out loud**; flat-footed, he takes the flat on the right thigh and goes down on one knee. (3) The second false heel; the shoulders roll and the sweep comes; he steps straight back out of its reach. (4) **Renn learns** and throws the figure where the sideways boy will be; Cael goes right, toward the blade; Renn drags the figure back by main force and lands the flat across both shoulder blades. (5) **Decisive**: Renn's weight is on the ball of the front foot, heel a finger up. Cael goes straight in under the fold, sets his foot behind the **front** heel and puts the hip strike flat into Renn's chest. Renn goes over; "Called. Unrated." "I *let* him"; "I came back for a second look… I didn't have a plan for you having a plan." "What's gone?" / "The heel." "I'm keeping that." Vell: "You said a word… In my circle." She writes a private line. The old man: **"Five."** |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:18:| 36 | A Week Behind | 6,405 | **Hesk cutaway (~5,100).** (a) About day 58: the Dessa letter, read at the vice; the missing fourth exchange noticed ("It did not look empty. It looked very slightly too neat"). He recognizes his own neat columns, does not ask, and writes the reply Cael read in ch 35. (b) **Joren at the workshop, working at patience**: Hesk lists the true things, the O, the oats, the mender, the friend, "and he won a fight". The word sits between them unsaid; no summons. Joren holds a survey level steady with his stance for fifty minutes: "Tell him my heels are down." (c) Day 63: **the summons letter, a week late**. Read on the workshop step; fear arriving after the danger is over ("That was the post"). A broken spring in the drawer cuts his palm. (d) **Alis at the healers'** on Merchant Row, shown, not announced: grey apron too big, carrying basins, washing the cut four times. No Path until spring. "Tell him I carry basins." He tells her true things and not the word or the summons. (e) **The guild review sits** (about day 72), in the guild hall by the canal: the reviewing officer and two members by lot. "I write to him every week… I intend to go on doing both." Correspondence is "not specified". **"No present cause to act on the member's standing… The matter will remain on the book. Open."** The unnamed gauge-maker and the drawer of springs. (f) He writes the fear into his reply because they promised "as it comes"; he leaves out the hand. **Cael, day 69**: bruised across the back and thigh, forearm swollen; Lira's rest days. Vell books **Sarel, Bronze Rank 1, Blade**, "not to win"; "You've won three of seven." **Lira: "Survivable?"** / Vell: "If he's sensible. And if you've taught him to fall." Sarel watched twice (about thirty, a scaffold worker, short dark hair, an old white scar on the left forearm): closing speed, a page of crossings-out, and one line, *she looks at the space next to you, where you're going to be*. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:19:| 37 | Nothing to Look Up | 6,722 | Day 73: **falling lessons** in the soft corner by the drain ("Don't argue with the ground"); a second chalk column for falls. *Gone* has become a hinge. Lira at full speed, tired: **her weight goes a hand past the backhand onto her right foot**. That is twice. He tells himself "after Sunday" and eats his bread. Day 74, **Sarel (~2,700)**: (1) watching the hips, he sees her start and not cross; the flat on the right shoulder; a proper fall. (2) His one line goes against his own habit; she is there anyway; the flat on the Renn ribs. **"Gone" said of the whole method** ("The watching was too slow for her. Not wrong. Too slow"). (3) **He moves without deciding** (Lira's way, stiff in the hips) and she lands nothing. (4) **The opponent learns**: she stays close and makes the space small, walking him to the long side; he drops and rolls on purpose; the flat lands on the forearm. (5) The hip strike thrown unplanned only brushes her sleeve, and the rope gasps; the flat across the back. (6) His legs are gone; the flat on the ribs; the blade laid on his collarbone. "Called. Blade Path." **Her crouched respect**: "How old are you?" "**Two**… You quit in two. You said it out loud. I heard you." "Don't ask me for another one." Vell: "**Six.**" Lira: "I did what you do." / "You did it, though." Log entry 9, the longest in the book: *I need a second way… this might be the one thing watching can't teach*. Day 77: Lira performs his Sarel bout back at him (the nod at a Bronze; the hips square), then **the habit seen a third time and named**: "When you're tired, you go too far on your right." She is startled and glad: an academy bout lost in the seventh exchange, never understood ("That's *mine*"). Then the cost: "You said *three*… How many times have you seen it?" He tells her the truth, Brenna three weeks ago. "**You *ruled*.** I said no ruling… You put my fight in the back of your book." "You don't get to decide when I'm ready to know about my own feet." "It's the best thing you've ever given me." She leaves the bread whole and **does not say "same time tomorrow"**. He thinks of the four things on the back page that Hesk does not know. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:20:| 38 | A Door | 6,106 | **Lira cutaway (~3,400)**, day 77. At the board her anger is let be big; the desk at the academy recalled, though nothing before the academy. *The same eyes* see the skipped middle and the right foot. She remembers her own kept row of three and her eleven solo drops ("a bucket of water takes some of the heat out of a forge"). **She takes the room**: over a chandler's two streets from Torvin's; a widow who "doesn't let to fighters" and then does, two weeks paid first; the throat-sweet tin of weeks of door; a fourth step that talks; a green-gone-grey door that locks from both sides; Torvin's chimney in the cracked window. On the floor, the six weeks from her side: twenty-two days gone, twenty left; being his teacher; **being seen accurately**, "better than *good*". Day 78, Cael: she is at the post. **The rule**: "You see a thing about me, you tell me. When you see it. Not when you're sure… mine are mine." He says it back in his own words. "It was still the best thing you've ever given me." "I took a room." **The door**: she shuts him out and makes him knock; "Yes?"; "It's yours." / "It shuts." / "It *locks*." / "You can see Torvin's from here." / "Can you? I hadn't noticed." Vell books **Talis, Copper Rank 4, Stone** (Lira: "Survivable. If he doesn't go down on the ribs"); watched once, with nothing about the ground. Day 81, **Talis (~1,100)**: four exchanges in which Talis keeps planting where Cael did not see him going, and the gatepost forearm strikes from the plant. Fourth: the strike he never saw building, under the left ribs; "Match." **Cheek on the dirt, he feels the hum come before each of Talis's steps.** Lira felt it through the iron rope stake in the third: "Nobody looks at the ground. That's why it's such a good place to keep something." |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:21:| 39 | The Plan That Survives | 6,615 | Night of day 81: Log entry 10 on the cot (the ribs catch). *I lost to a thing I wasn't looking at*. Ruling: **"before I look for the tell, look for where I haven't looked."** His palm on the floorboards feels Yeni's steps ("it's got plenty to say"). **Hesk's review letter** (arrived day 79), reread: the review, the gauge-maker, "proud of the reading", and **the fear, as it came**. Cael cannot picture Hesk afraid; the four kept things; laughter at Joren's heels and Alis's basins. His reply tells Renn, Sarel, Talis, the room, and "kept it three weeks too long" (not hers by name), and does not tell the four. **The last weeks**: the river freezing at the edges, second blankets, ribs mending. Lira trains her right side down by finger-widths ("Tell me"), and he tells her small things as he sees them (the left hand when she laughs; the post to the right; the hair, which she keeps). The moving comes by hairs; **the fortieth *gone* is the one he does not say**, and she rules a line under the column. **Dellin**: two old entries found and a third watched on Tuesday, ground first by hand on the iron stake. A carter, about twenty-five, "a face like a friendly loaf". Left high, right low, the shove, and then the collar. He asked for Cael. The plan: block nothing; make him miss twice; under the collar in the third. Lira: "the first plan you've ever made that doesn't need him to be stupid." Day 85, **Dellin (~1,230)**: a hand on the ground at the mark; exactly as written; under the reaching hand, sending it a quarter turn on; the strike to the jaw hinge; Dellin sits down; "Called. Unrated." **"You knew what I'd do before I did it."** Cael does not correct it ("a column of figures, and there was no room in a circle for a column of figures"). Vell: "That was a plan… Write down why this one did." Entry 11: *A plan holds when it's built on what they are, not on what you hope they'll do.* Lira: Vell has given him **Corbin**, nearly forty fights, "the one people use… for measuring", the one they will repeat. Day 88: Corbin watched. Shield, a plate-sized shine spent only when needed; nothing in the body; **he reads feet at the mark**. *He's me.* The plan from Renn's false heel: *He'll take full credit. Let him.* |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:22:| 40 | Tier Equivalent | 7,358 | Day 92, **Corbin (~2,300 + aftermath)**. Renn, Dellin, Brenna and **Dessa on the benches**; Marrow cannot set an over/under. (1) Feet set honestly *left*; Corbin is there first; a sensible fist to the chest. (2) The same set, a lie; he goes right; Corbin is on the wrong side, the shield comes across in time, and **a small clean surprise** shows on his face. (3) **Corbin learns**: he stops reading feet and waits; finds the Talis ribs. (4) Patient pressure on the ribs, down on one knee, walked to the long side. (5) **Decisive**: tired, Corbin reads the feet again, because tired people do not lie with their feet. Cael sets the same *left*, Corbin reads the lie he learned, and **Cael tells the truth**. Inside on the shield's wrong side; the strike stopped a finger from the jaw; the shield a breath late; the left hand flat on Corbin's ribs. **Corbin's open palm: "Called. Conceded. Unrated."** "Forty-one fights. Nobody's ever done that to me with the *truth*." "Five." The yard repeating *past Corbin*; charcoal on the board: *CORBIN PUT HIS HAND UP / five*. Lira's arm through his: "That's the one they'll repeat." **The thirty-ninth night (day 94), at Torvin's**, reviewing entries 7–12 by lamplight: the quiet before a thought, before any word; then from the dark place behind the breastbone, the sigil still unlit, **the protected notice**. Read three times; **he knows at once it is Lira's** (the grey half, the drop, Dessa's fourth, Sarel's third). He imagines her green door and does not go; her "mine are mine". ***Not yet*** in the back, boxed, **not sure it is honest**. **The step by the cot**, on purpose, slow and clumsy: the boards "very slightly less firm" by a hand's width; breath and the left hip "cost something"; worse the second time. *It's started to answer when I ask.* **Short letter to Hesk** with nothing of the notice; the count is now **five**, and the person meant to be first has not been told. Day 97, **Vell, short: assessed-Copper**. "It's not a real rank… Inside it, it's the difference between a boy people bet on for sport and a fighter people plan for." "Is it worth anything to you?" … **"For what it's worth. I do."** Lira: "You're past me." "It's the forty-second day. You've spent them all." The well, "Same time tomorrow," and the unsaid thing getting heavier: *Nothing that has to be held is free.* |
