editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:143:Seln took each form the same way. He read it from the name to the signature at an even pace. He stamped the received line. He tore off the copy-slip along its rule and handed it back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, to the second. A man who read every line from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:"Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check," he said. "Keep the documentation current." He set the edge of the stack true against the counter. "There have been senior men at the foot of the road since the recess. The office mentions it only because the office has eyes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:"It's comfortable," he said. "That's the trouble with it. Comfortable for years, and then one day not, and all at once." He held up a finger. "Three costs. One: the line's a document. It goes into the file in Gault's hand, and one day an inspector opens the file. Bracken's warned me twice, and Withrow wrote *sooner* on her sheet with her own pen. Find a feeble line in there and you've found proof the provision lets feeble people in. Somebody uses that against the provision, and I've lost the only floor I stand on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"His south road lies under your edge for a day's ticks beyond this stone. After that there is a single stroke, which is how he draws a track that he would not take a cart along, and it keeps beside the edge for a day more. Then it stops, and there is nothing under the brass that a wheel made."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
