   123	## New canon minted (flag where marked)
   124	
   125	- **The ferry's rig (flag).** The scow hangs from the cable by two running blocks, one over her bow and one over her stern, each on **a strap of plaited hide as long as a man** to a post at her rail. In slack water she crosses "set a little aslant on her two straps, so that the current itself shouldered her along the cable"; the hauling rope from her bow turns through a block lashed to **an oak post at the landing's head** and so into the shed. The drum has **an iron dog** (a pawl) hinged to the shed's king-post. The ferryman had two hauling ropes "before the snow"; the second, sent across by a skiff when the first strands, is the sixty yards owed. The old slip's **sill** lies under the mud two strides south of the post. In flood the cable bellies to the south; she crossed light in three minutes and came home in twenty-two. The rope has "two yarns gone where she bore on the sheave". The new stern strap is rove out of the tail of the stern line. The fare is a copper a head.
   126	- **The first boat (flag):** first light on the 277th; eight passengers (a man with a ledger who carries the bag; two carters; a woman and a child; a drover; a lad; Vastin) and no goods; **the cordage stays on the western landing**. The bag had eleven pieces and none for the five. After noon she goes over again with twelve carters on foot, the bag and Ephram's letter; still no wheels.
   127	- **The week (flag):** seven fires on the 271st, nineteen and thirty carts on the 276th. The river up a foot between the 268th and the 271st; the last of the shelf on the 275th; rain on the night of the 275th; water over the landing's lowest plank on the 276th; down a hand on the night before the boat and another by noon.
   128	- **The mule's stool (flag):** a three-legged milking stool from the keeper's kitchen, set at the board's end on the 271st with the sixth bowl. Oryn will not sit on it; Lira names it; Vastin asks whether the place is taken and is told whose it is.
   129	- **Seln's account of the gate (flag):** a man in the gate asks each person "for a name, and where the name is registered", and writes it; "every counter in every district keeps a list", read against each name as it is written; **the man who writes the name is the man who serves the paper.** What it holds: Lira's and Karis's certificates, kept for a slip, pending ("You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract"); Brom's Copper and the unsigned finding, "frozen as they stand"; and of Cael, "the thing that it holds is you… You would be produced", to a frame. Seln is not one of the four ("I was employed"). **Vastin, from the inside (11:75–79):** the instrument is drawn on the code's fourteenth chapter, ninth part; it does not wait upon a frame and does not lapse; he could not have lifted it when he held the seal; nothing lifts it "short of the settling of his status; and his status can be settled only on a plate."
   130	- **Oryn at Lowmarch (flag):** her bench is a form set along the wall under the eave, a yard past the right-hand post of Pike's plank, by the door of the keeper's wash-house, where she works with the door open. Nine on the first morning. She takes payment in kind (a cheese). "I'll not spend on what grease will do." She does not go up the bank to the older healer.
   131	- **The new cloth (flag):** Pike paints a new cloth at the thaw, with a hog's-bristle brush and a black of his own making; each crew's season figure is carried over and the cloth hangs in the order of the figures; "A hold reads down from the top until it comes to a crew that it can pay." The five have no figure, "So I carry the place": eleventh on the new cloth. Teague is first.
   132	- **Teague (flag):** the wrist was unbound on the 272nd, a hundred and twenty-four days after it was bound. He has forty sacks of salt for the first boat and sees it over himself; then the block on the second ledge of the cold road. He did not put a day in his term; if the six are gone when he comes down, Cael is to "leave it with [Pike] which way". The tall man has put the steel back on his pole.
   133	- **The bout (flag):** the 273rd, in the silent yard's square; a stave a hand taller than Lira against Cael's short one; 2–3 to her. Her left hand comes a beat behind her right. The arm "told" her once, in the third exchange, at a square block. She fell beside it, not on it.
   134	- **Ephram's letter (flag), sent on the 277th:** *Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
   135	- **Vastin as he arrives (flag):** a drover's coat of brown frieze, too short in the sleeve, bought "at the last market inside the Line"; the grey one left on its hook; an ash stick cut out of a hedge; a pack with one strap mended in cord; a small purse on a cord; the flat carried under the pack against his back and the leaf inside his coat in oilcloth. **The knee** "had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth"; no side is given. He can do ten miles in a day on it. He left "the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it". He crossed the sill at the last station on the 265th and reached the western landing on the evening of the 276th. His left hand would not open after the strap and has not lain flat since. He has paid a copper for the fare, a copper and a half for a loaf (a copper and a cut half), a copper for a bowl and a copper for the loft. He sleeps in **the loft over the tap, up the keeper's stair** (a copper the night).
   136	- **What Vastin says of his own road to the papers (flag).** After the borrowed room he asked the house's frame-shop mender what lay under the function: "There is a thing beneath the plate which is not the office's work… he does not go below", a rule of the shop told to every mender in his first year. The office that has the charge of the stations keeps schedules for the housing, the plate, the frame and the function; "Below the function it has a single page. The page says that the layer is not to be opened, and it gives as its reason that it cannot be." It "has been attempted, more than once, by the people whose business it is". In the oldest register of housings that he was let to see, the line for the layer has no maker's name but a citation. **He cut that leaf out of a bound register with a knife, and says that it was wrong.** He could not go to the archive: an archive on the Line keeps a book at its door. The completion report reached him as a courtesy copy with no routing note; it took him the cold term to find the file it belonged to, "which stands in no register"; the abstracts "went up every year by seat courier, and… were received by nobody". He does not know who had them held, and looked. He does not know whether the copy was sent to him on purpose (Seln's question; left open).
   137	- **The flat (flag):** a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, "as broad as a man's shoulders and no thicker than two fingers", stamped *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.*; six rows, a year to a row, the newest uppermost, each in a tape; a pocket in the lid. **The abstracts:** printed forms; four signs and a number at the head (the Stair's sheet has the dead surveyor's four signs and thirty-two); a column for the day in the office's reckoning; **a measure given in lengths, knots and strides**; a narrow right-hand column with a stroke or none on each line, its heading in the unread signs, and *Total* with a figure at the foot of the row's last sheet. Karis counts more than four different groups of signs in the newest row. **No sheets are counted and no figure is given.**
   138	- **The page (flag):** one leaf of thick brown stock with a binder's holes down its left edge and a cut right edge; a schedule of housings, each with a number, a date of setting and a mender's mark; the protected line breaks into one entry "in the same brown ink and the same hand"; the entry goes on to "a housing on the coast". Nothing about its age is said.
   139	- **The sheet (flag):** thin stock, newer than the rest, a printed head, "a quick sloping hand"; a **technician's return**: the stations' office keeps a few men "whose business is the function", who must write up on the day anything they were not sent to find. The bar is as broad as a thumb, laid on both sides, cracked in drying. Vastin has seen such a bar "on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years" and never with initials.
   140	- **Karis's receipt (flag), 12:169:** *Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, the sheets not yet counted, and one broker's report; one leaf cut out of a register; one technician's sheet, copied this night. Examined by nobody. — K.* He carries it where the leaf was.
   141	- **Names given at the board (10:57–77):** the six tell Vastin their names; he had Cael's and Karis's from paper and "took some trouble never to learn" the others. Seln gives his.
   142	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the courier with the cut hat-brim and the old carter with pale eyes (both from Book 7's road); the boy with a tally stick on the western landing; the landing broker and his clerk; the man with the ledger who carries the bag; the passengers; the woman with the loaves; the keeper; the frame-shop mender (from Book 6).
