editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-131-It came on as they went down, thinly, out of no wind at all. Cael walked through the meadow to the river's edge, to the planks of the landing, and stopped there and counted what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-135-Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-137-The snow was coming down on all of it alike, and on five people and a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-139-He noticed where he had begun. He had started at the eave, and gone on from there. It was how a person counts a place when he knows already which roof he is going to sleep under.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-141-The man with the mallet had straightened his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:143:"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-145-"No. We've not forgotten it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-147-"Nor have I. Sixty yards, four-strand." He knocked a last cake of ice into the river. "I'll not want it this side of the thaw. There's one more crossing in her, tomorrow, if that shelf lets me; the post comes down to the far bank for it. Then I take my lines in."
