editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:He had been thrown toward the gap, and he had gone on as he was pointed, wading, with the water flying from his knees. The wall beside the gap, on its right hand, was the lowest in the room; it was broken down to the height of his waist. He threw himself across it, chest first, and dragged his legs up after him, and lay there.
