editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-107-It was cover, and it was a trap, and the same yard of stone was both. Cael understood it: nothing could come at them where they sat, and they could not leave it. He watched the plates lift below him, in a line that went round the hole and came back; and when it passed beneath his boots he could see a length of back under the clear water, as broad as the ledge itself, going by for a great deal longer than he liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-109-Along the wall from him there was a rustle of paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:111:Karis had turned herself about on the wall-top. She was on her knees, facing outward, with her boots toward the hole; and then she lay down along the stone on her stomach, and put her head and both arms over the inner edge, and hung there. She had a sheet spread on the cut face below her, upside down, with her left forearm across it. Her right hand began to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-113-Lira looked at the soles of her boots, and then at the back of her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-115-"You're copying," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-117-"I'm not getting another chance," said Karis, from under the edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-119-"No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:It was the only thing that was said about it. Cael heard the pencil going, steadily, on thin paper against stone. He heard Karis say *sheet*, in a voice that had the blood in it, and Lira let go of the belt, and tore one from the roll with her teeth, and passed it down, and took hold again. A finished sheet came up over the edge and was pinned under a pebble. The two of them moved a yard along the wall on their knees, and Karis went over once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-123-It went on for the better part of an hour. The mist thinned over their heads and went from grey to white. Seln stood on the tall wall and said *there* each time that the line of lifting plates passed below him, and it passed a great deal oftener than once in a quarter of an hour; the thing had given up its round, and was going about that one room as a dog goes about a tree. Cael's breeches froze to the stone. He counted the sheets as they came up, and then he made himself stop, since a man who is counting paper is not watching a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-125-He looked away from the two of them, across the hole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-127-Brom had got himself up to sitting. He sat astride the low wall as a man sits a horse, with his bound arm against his chest, and he was studying the gap beside him. Cael could read what was in his face. The gap was two strides wide. On its far side the wall began again, low, and ran on to the corner, and round, to where the others were; and at the foot of the gap, between the two ends of wall, lay the sill. It was the old work. A man who stood on it stood on nothing that the thing below could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:129:It lay a little lower than Brom's knee, and to reach it he must let himself down the end of a wet wall with one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-131-"Stay where you are," said Cael.
