editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-209-There was one road for it, and it went down that road: through the bones of his legs, and his hips, and up his back and down again, and out through both heels into the ledge. He did not give. The plate, which had been tipping, was driven flat under him with a crack like a mallet on a post; and something that had taken stones off that floor for longer than there had been carters, and had never once met one that did not come away, stopped against the soles of a boy's boots.
