editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md-157-Cael walked last, and he catalogued the six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md-159-He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
