editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-59-They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-61-The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:63:Seln went a quarter of a mile in front, where he could be seen against the sky. Karis walked behind him with the hide under her arm, and at every halt she unrolled it on her knee, and laid the brass along it, and looked from the skin to the country and back again. Lira came next, on the whole of her foot, and a road gone soft was no better for her than a road gone hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-65-Brom had the brown mule and two of the boxes, and the kit on his own back by its strap. The healer came last with the grey, which carried the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-67-"You're listing," she said.
