editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-75-"It's not meant to hurt. It's meant to be let alone, and those aren't the same thing. Ask her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-77-"They're not," said Lira, without turning her head. "It's a deal worse when it stops hurting. You forget that it's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-81-On the second morning the road swung away south toward a hold whose smoke they could see, and the brass did not go with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-83-There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-84-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-147-"Thirty-three," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-149-"I shall enter the figure," said Karis. "And that you said, in a barn, that one does not stamp a bar thirty-one and stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:151:Seln had gone down on one knee. He looked at the scale, and at the way the turf had closed about the foot; then he got up, and walked ten yards along the reeds on either side of the ruts with his eyes on the ground, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-155-"The man with the book wrote that it was confirmed," said Karis. "He did not write that he had stood in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-157-"He wrote it in a room under a floor, three days from here. I can tell you nothing more of him than that."
