editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:235:"I don't know," he said. "There's a thing that stands before the bearing, and I've not ruled on it, and I'll not rule till that boat's in. It may be days. It may be a good many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:281:"She said not to fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:129:No grey came on those hands. Nothing stood up out of the deck, or the air; nobody in that boat declared anything. Cael would have known, since he had spent every year since his Kindling learning what a declaration looked like from the wrong side of it. There was a man, and a strap, and a post, and that was the whole of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:249:"May I know whom I am thanking?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:217:"May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:253:"I shall not say tonight. I have not turned every sheet in six rows; and the clerk's figure and my own count do not agree, so that I have two numbers and no finding. He may have been counting visits, or instruments, or men, where I was counting places. A stroke in a column is a stroke, and the heading over this one is in the signs that I cannot read." She lifted her hand from the deal. "Both of them are more than four. That is what the paper says, and I shall say the rest when a sheet tells me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:97:Wind had him half way to her before the fall was finished, and he told it no, and walked the four strides. He touched the toe of her boot with his stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:99:"Two all," said Lira, from the ground. "I didn't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:133:"She saw you not fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:41:"That is the first," said Vastin. "It is yours now. It is a great deal of paper to say that an office has been walking round your four places for six years, counting, and has told nobody. I should like the second laid apart from it. At the bare end, if I may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:183:It was not a fall. Brom was off the door before it could be one, and had him by the upper arm, left-handed; Oryn's two hands had come up, and stopped in the air, since nobody had asked her for them. Vastin hung in a large man's grip, open-eyed, and as pale a brown in the face as the leaf itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:1:# Chapter 8 — Both Hands
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:3:Lira took the longest stave in the bundle, on the two hundred and seventy-third morning, and nobody who knew her could have mistaken why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:5:It was a hand taller than she was, and as thick as her wrist. A person could not have swung it with one arm, or even carried it far. She took it up from the wet grass by its middle with her right hand, and then, slowly, as she had set her fingers to a reed the night before, she put her left upon it a yard lower down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:7:The square was as Teague had left it, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner and the bench along its western edge. The snow had gone from it. What lay there now was last year's grass, flattened and brown, with a skin of frost on it that would be water by the middle of the forenoon. Brom sat on the bench to keep the count. At its far end the tall man had come down by himself, as he had on most mornings of the cold term, with his spear upright between his knees and its new leaf of steel in a leather cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:9:"Three touches," said Lira. "Whoever takes it calls it. Over the pegs is a touch. And that's all the rules there are; I told you about the other one last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:11:"You told me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:13:"Then don't look at my arm. Look at the wood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:Cael had his own stave, which was no longer than his forearm and hand, with a rag bound round the grip. He stood four strides off. Wind was in his feet, as it was at the start of everything; it leaned him away to the right, with a burst ready behind the lean, and he told it no and felt his own two legs take the weight instead. The read asked next, civilly, for the ground under that stave. He shut it. No Paths meant the ten, whatever he called them, and he had written so. They were not much. He had bought them on this grass, a touch at a time, from a seven-foot pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:17:She came at him as a staff is meant to be used, with both ends.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:The right-hand end came first, low, at his knee. He took it on his stave and felt it through his elbow. The other end was already coming, over the top, for his left shoulder, and he got back out of its way. Then it was the right again, and the left, and he went round the square before her like a man backing away from a mill. She was quicker than he was, as she had always been. Her feet in that great boot found the ground under the frost as though they had laid it themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:21:But he had been counted by her for ten mornings, a quick beat and a slow one, and by the fourth stroke he was counting her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:23:It was the left. Everything that came from her right side came whole, with her shoulders and her hips behind it, and arrived when her body said that it would. Everything from the left came a little after. He could see the order given: her shoulder turned, and her weight went over. Then there was a wait, no longer than a blink, while a thin white arm that had hung in grey wool for a hundred and seventy days gathered itself to push the lower end of that stave round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:*Out,* he thought, when the right came. *Home.* The left started. He did not go back from it. He went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve brushing along it, while the stroke was still on its way to the place where he had been; and he laid the end of his stave against her hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:27:She stopped as if she had walked into a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:29:"One," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:31:She had called it without any heat, as she would have called it for a stranger. She stepped back to four strides, and rolled her shoulders, and he saw her left hand come off the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:He did not understand at first what she had done. She had slid her right hand down to the stave's heavy middle, and tucked the upper half of it along her forearm, as a person carries a pole through a doorway. She had a short stout stick now, in one hand. She came in with it very fast, and low. The thing darted at his thigh and was gone before his own wood got there, and came again at his wrist; and when he covered the wrist she went past him on the mended foot, turning, and rapped him smartly across the back of the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:35:"One all," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:37:Brom had both feet flat on the ground. On the far end of the bench the tall man had not stirred, but his pale eyes were on the girl's left hand, which hung open at her side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:39:"You let go," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:43:"You won it with one arm." He had not meant to say it so plainly, and having begun he could not stop half way. "You've fought with one for a hundred and seventy days, and you're better at it than I am with two. I can't touch you like that. But it isn't what you came out here for. You said so, on that floor, with a reed in your hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:45:She looked at him with her chin up. She had told him on this bench, in the snow, that he went back along a pole because he had never learned to do anything else. He had not liked hearing it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:47:"You went round my right," she said. "Every stroke. Three times you'd my left side open, and you looked at it and went somewhere else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:"On my arm's lateness. Not at my arm. There's a difference, and you know what it is, because somebody did it to you with a pole all the cold term and you wrote it down." She put her left hand back on the stave, a yard below the right, and shut the fingers one at a time. "I'll keep it on. You stop minding it. That's a bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:53:"It's a bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:55:"Then come and hit me properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:59:He came in with his whole weight, since she had asked for that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:It was a down-stroke, from his right shoulder at hers, such as he would have given Teague's bow or the boy. She did not slip it. She brought the stave up level in both hands and took him square, on the bare wood between her two fists, and the shock of it ran up his own arm to the teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:63:He saw it run up hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:65:Her right arm took its half and held. The left took the other half, and it was the first blow of any kind that had landed on it since the hounds had it on a white floor. Her face went the colour of the frost. The fingers of that hand came open, all four at once, as if somebody had cut a string, and the lower end of the stave dropped a foot toward the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:67:He checked. He had his next stroke begun, at her open side, and he held it in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:69:"Don't you stop!"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:71:It came out of her between her teeth. He had stopped already, and it was half a breath before he could make himself go on. In that half breath he watched her do the thing that she had come out there to do. The hand was shaking; she looked at it, once, as she might have looked at a dog that had run from a noise. The fingers closed again on the wood. They closed in their slow order, the first and then the next, and when the last was shut she heaved with the whole of that side, and the stave's lower end came round under his guard and thumped against his right ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:73:"Two," he said. "To you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:75:She stood breathing. The colour was coming back about her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:77:"It told me," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:79:"What did it say?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:"That it's there. And that it didn't care for that." She opened the hand and shut it. "It's said so. I heard. Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:83:"Lira—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:85:"You've still not struck at it. I felt where that was going, and it was going at my right. I'm not made of two sorts of girl. Hit the left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:87:He knew what she was asking, and it was harder than anything that pole had ever asked of him. He had spent a cold term watching that arm held against her breast in a healer's knot. Every habit that he had was a habit of going round it. The tall man had fought him through a cold term and had never once struck at the taped side; but the tall man had a rule for it, and she had taken the rule away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:89:He cut at her left flank, backhanded and hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:She got the wood there. It came late, with the slow hand driving it, and she had to give ground to make the time; she went back two quick steps, and at the second of them the great boot came down on the skin of frost with its heel first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:93:The heel shot from under her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:95:He saw her know that she was falling, and he saw what she did with the knowledge. She was going over to the left. She threw the stave clear with her right hand and wrenched her whole body round in the air, like a cat that has been dropped, so that the left arm came up and across her chest. She struck the ground with the point of her right shoulder and her right hip, hard enough to bring a grunt out of her, and lay there with a thin white hand held up off the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:97:Wind had him half way to her before the fall was finished, and he told it no, and walked the four strides. He touched the toe of her boot with his stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:99:"Two all," said Lira, from the ground. "I didn't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:101:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:103:"I fell beside it. That's allowed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:He held down his hand to her. She looked at it, and she put up the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:107:He did not take it. He had pulled her to her feet a good many times, on floors and on gravel, and always by the other. She lay with her arm stretched up to him, shaking a little with its own weight, and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:109:"It's an arm," she said. "I've been told so by somebody who knows."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:111:He took it by the wrist, and she took his, and he leaned back. It was like lifting on a rope that he could feel every strand of. But it held; she came up off the frost, with wet grass all down her right side, and stood, and let go of him without hurry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:113:She did not pick the stave up by its middle this time. She took it as he had seen another person take a pole on that same ground, with her two hands a yard apart about its centre, so that she had as much wood before the leading hand as behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:115:"Last," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:117:The left end came at him. It was slow, and he could see every part of it: the shoulder, and the weight, and the blink of waiting while the arm gathered. *Home*, said his legs, which had been taught to go in on exactly that. He went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:119:It had never been meant to arrive. She had sent the slow hand out to be seen, as a person holds up a lantern, and while he was walking down the outside of it the right-hand end came round from behind her hip with all her quickness in it and stopped an inch from his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:121:She tapped him there, lightly, with the end of the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:123:"Three," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:125:"It's slow." She was grinning, with her teeth showing and her hair full of frost. "So I'll use it slow. It can't hit you yet. It can make you look at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:127:Brom had got up off the bench. Beyond him the tall man lifted his spear's butt a hand's breadth from the turf and brought it down, and sat as he had sat before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:129:Lira saw it. She did not turn her head toward that end of the bench; but she stood a little straighter in her one great boot. Then her eyes went past Cael, sixty yards down the meadow, to the long eave. A square woman was standing there in an open doorway with a child's wrist between her fingers. She had plainly been standing there for some time. She looked at a girl with grass down one side and an arm hanging whole, and she said nothing whatever, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:131:"She saw," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:133:"She saw you not fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:135:"I know what she saw." Lira bent for the stave that she had thrown, and took it up left-handed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:139:The last of the shelf went on the two hundred and seventy-fifth day, in one piece as long as the landing, and after it the river began to rise in earnest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:141:Cael wrote his letter that afternoon, at the meal-sack end of the deal. He had owed it since a wash on the Stair road, to a tall exact person on a bluff who had written that he still lost every time he ran it. For the whole of a cold term he had not known what to say to a friend who was walking a bout through by himself in an empty yard. He knew now, since a girl with grass in her hair had shown him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:143:*Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:145:He put no place on it. He folded it, and laid a copper on top, and left the two together inside Hesk's book for the boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:It rained that night, for the first time since the snow, and in the morning Lira came to the board with her left arm held in against her and her mouth shut. A healer had told her that it would ache in the wet for a year. She did not speak of it. When Brom wanted the new hurdle lifted into its place between the shed's posts, she took one end of it in both hands before anybody else had stirred, and walked it round the drum with her jaw set, and lashed her end to its post herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:By the two hundred and seventy-sixth the water was over the landing's lowest plank. It came down the colour of a ploughed field, with a noise that could be heard in the inn's kitchen, and it brought whole trees with it, and once a drowned sheep, and once the better part of somebody's footbridge. The cable hung over it in a long belly. The ferryman sat on his keg from first light with his arms folded, and for an hour he did nothing but look. Half the meadow stood on the bank and looked at him, for there were nineteen fires along that grass now, and thirty carts with their shafts down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:Seln had forty-six notches. Brom had asked Karis for the purse, and had it in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:"He will not take silver," said Karis. "He said so on the far bank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:157:"The ferryman won't. The broker will. I buy it of the one and I carry it to the other, and then there's a line gone out of your book that's had no figure on it since before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:The ferryman had got up. He walked to the end of the planks, and spat into the river, and watched where the spittle went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:161:"Tomorrow," he said. "Light, the first time, to see what she does with me. Then people. I'll not put a wheel or a bale on her in that, for the broker or for anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:163:Brom looked at the purse in his hand, and across a hundred yards of brown water at a stack under tarred sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:165:"So it lies there," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:167:"It lies there till she drops. It's lain all the cold term; it'll not spoil." The ferryman went by him toward the shed. "You've waited a cold term to pay me. I can wait a week to be paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Twelve days before that boat, when the count at Lowmarch stood at two hundred and sixty-five, Vastin had come down to the last ford on the east road with a stick in his hand. He had never heard of the count.

