51:There was one leaf in it. It was old, of a thick soft stock that had gone the brown of weak tea, and along its left edge ran a row of small holes where a binder's thread had been. The opposite edge was clean. Somebody had cut it there with a knife, close and straight, against a rule.
55:"I cut it," said Vastin. "I shall say so before I am asked. It stood in a bound register, in a room where I had the right to read and no right to take, and I took a blade to it. I sat once upon the inquiry into an archivist who had done the same, and I found against him, and he lost his place." He did not look away from the leaf. "I was right to find so, and I should find so again, against myself. I do not offer it as a thing well done. I offer it as the only way that this line was ever going to leave that room."
59:Karis had brought her stool down to the bare end. She bent over the leaf with her gloved hands behind her.
61:Most of it was a schedule. Cael could read it, since it lay the other way up to him: a column of housings, each with its number, and against each a line of small faded writing that gave the date of a setting and a mender's mark. It was a clerk's list, as dull as a tally of sacks, and it ran from the head of the leaf to its foot.
75:"It is a title. It is not a name. A name is written as a name is; it does not take the article before it. A clerk writes *the registrar*, and *the keeper*, and he writes so of an office that somebody holds, and he does not trouble to say who held it." She drew breath. "I have read the Compact's paper from this year's forms back to the oldest charter that a certain house will let a person see. I have dated it by its stocks and by its seals, and I know four strata of it by the feel of the leaf. I have never met that word in any stratum: not as a title, or as a trade, or as a thing that anybody was ever called."
79:"No. I have one line, and I shall not say more about it than one line will carry. It is a title, and it stands where a maker's name should stand. That is all that I can read off this leaf, and I am not going past the leaf."
85:He had put his thick finger down on the deal beside the leaf, not touching it, under two words. Cael did not need them pointed out. They were the name of a town, and the name of the part of it that lay lowest. A small pale woman had said both at a board nine days ago, with her palm on a strip of oilcloth; and at noon a man who had never heard of her map had said the first of them again.
89:"Whatever stood before the first stroke," said Karis. "It is not on this leaf."
91:"It is not on the leaf before it, either," said Vastin. "I read the leaves on both sides; the entry begins here, and it ends here, and it goes on directly to a housing on the coast. Whoever wrote that line supposed that his reader would know what was meant. I did not, and I am the reader that he had."
95:Cael looked at the brown leaf, which lay a hand's width from the ends of his fingers. He had been sent for by paper before, in his life, more than once: by a summons, and by a challenge under a seal, and by an order for a room and an hour; and each of those had carried his name. This one had no name on it but a town's, and it had been written a long while before he was born, by a clerk who was thinking about housings.
103:Karis carried the lamp back up the table. The bare end went dim behind her, and the leaf lay there in the half dark on its square of oilcloth, where Cael could see that it was pale and no more.
121:"I do not know. I held it to a window, and to a candle, and it has been laid on both sides." Vastin put one raw finger beside the bar, as Brom had put his beside the leaf. "This is the one thing on that sheet which I can read, and I had better tell you why. A clerk who withdraws a word strikes it once, with a pen, so that it can still be read beneath the stroke; the code requires that, and I taught it to every junior that I had. That is not a clerk's stroke. It is a brush. I have seen it on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years, and I never once saw initials beside it. It is laid on by somebody who has the authority to make a thing unsaid, and who does not care to be known for having used it."
131:She had her notebook open at a clean leaf already, and she copied the sheet as she had copied forty-one kinds of mark off three floors, a line for a line, with every stop where the technician had set it. At the date she wrote the office's own figures down exactly as they stood, and did not turn them into anything. When she came to the bar she ruled a box of the same length, and looked at it, and wrote two words within.
159:She had turned to a fresh leaf. Vastin, who had begun to draw the flat's straps together from mere habit, stopped with the buckle in his fingers.
163:"I do not give it because you ask. I give it because three things have changed hands in this room, and one of the hands was mine." She wrote steadily, in the small upright hand, and read it over once under her breath, and tore the leaf out along its fold. "You will find that it has no seal."
169:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, the sheets not yet counted, and one broker's report; one leaf cut out of a register; one technician's sheet, copied this night. Examined by nobody. — K.*
175:"Yes. I was told so at a door." He folded the leaf in three, along no crease but his own. Then he put it into the breast of the brown coat, into the place where the oilcloth had ridden.
179:Cael saw the thing happen from the far end of the table. The man had nothing on his back now. He had carried that flat for three hundred miles, under a pack, and a leaf against his chest; both were on the deal, and he had a receipt for them. He took his hand from his coat, and reached for the table's edge to steady himself, and his fingers came down an inch short of it.
183:It was not a fall. Brom was off the door before it could be one, and had him by the upper arm, left-handed; Oryn's two hands had come up, and stopped in the air, since nobody had asked her for them. Vastin hung in a large man's grip, open-eyed, and as pale a brown in the face as the leaf itself.
225:Karis had come back to her stool. She took off the gloves a finger at a time and laid them on the flat. Under the lamp lay a courier's leather with six rows in it, and a broker's sheet, and a technician's sheet with a bar across its foot; and at the far end, in the half dark, there lay a leaf on a square of oilcloth, with a title on it and a place.
227:Cael's right hand was on the deal beside that leaf. It had been there since the lamp was first carried down to him, and it had not gone a finger nearer.
239:"It's in. It brought no paper against us, and it brought those." He did not look up from the leaf. "I'll not rule at night, in a store, over a thing I've not touched. I'll say it at the plank in the morning, in daylight, and I'll ask for hands."
257:Oryn had stood by the hinges through the whole of it, with her sleeves down. She had heard a town named at noon, and she had read a leaf tonight from where she stood, since her eyes were as good as her hands. Cael saw her look at that leaf, and at the hand that lay beside it, and he saw her mouth open.
267:Lira got up off the meal-sacks. She stretched her left arm over her head, slowly, and brought it down, and looked at the hand. Then she came along the table to its bare end, and stood beside him, and looked at the leaf, and at his fingers, and at the strip of bare deal between.
