editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:Nobody started, since they were not people who started. But Karis's right hand, which had been lying on her notebook, had gone flat upon it as if the leaf might blow away; Seln's eyes had left the yard, and Lira's fingers, on the board, had drawn in. Brom had laid his crust down on the board with great care. And at the board's end a healer who had swallowed a sentence nine days ago, at this same table, when a station was spoken of, stood with her bowl in both hands and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:247:Cael could guess how a sitting had ended for him, on every day of forty years: with a leaf to be signed, or a seal to be set, and a clerk at the door to gather what was to go down a stair; and then with the next thing, drawn toward him across a desk. There had always been a next thing. Cael had been the next thing himself, once, on a bluff, for three days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:87:Cael had watched her do it. Twice in that hour she had drawn breath, with her pencil lifted, and twice she had let the breath go and bent to the leaf again. She had a question. It was not a question about paper, or she would have asked it; she asked those as other people breathed. It was the other kind, which she thought ill-mannered, and she had been brought up in archives, where a person does not ask a keeper why he has kept a thing so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:111:Karis did not write it down. She sat with her fingers laced, as still as she sat before a leaf that she had not expected to be able to read; and Cael, who had kept his own question, did not grudge the keeping.

