     1	# Chapter 9 — The First Boat
     2	
     3	Seln cut the forty-seventh notch on the landing as the light came. It was day two hundred and seventy-seven, and half the meadow stood behind him to see a boat go out.
     4	
     5	The river had dropped a hand in the night, and it was still no river that Cael had ever seen at that place. It ran from his right hand to his left, out of the north, brown and loud, a good deal wider than the hundred yards that it kept in the dry months. The cable went over it from a post above the landing to its fellow on the western bank; and the weight of the flood had dragged the cable's middle so far down the stream that it hung in a long bow, with its belly well to the south of both posts.
     6	
     7	The scow lay under the eastern end of it. She hung from that cable by two running blocks, one over her bow and one over her stern, and from each block a strap of plaited hide as long as a man came down to a stout post at her rail. Cael had crossed in her a good many times and had never looked at those straps. He looked at them now, since the ferryman was looking at them; the man went from the one to the other and hauled on each with his whole weight, and then stood chewing his lip.
     8	
     9	"I've the one rope," said the ferryman.
    10	
    11	He said it to Brom. The hauling rope lay along the planks in wet coils, as thick as a wrist. One end of it was made fast to the scow's bow. The rest went up the landing to an oak post at its head, where a block was lashed, and turned through that block into the round shed, to the drum. It was the rope that had drawn a cart out of the ice.
    12	
    13	"I'd two, before the snow. A man sends the second across by a skiff if his first one strands. I've no second."
    14	
    15	"I know where it is," said Brom.
    16	
    17	"So do I. I'm not saying it for that. I'm saying that if I shout for a line this morning, there's nobody to throw me one."
    18	
    19	He stepped down into the scow by himself, and took the long sweep out of its crutch. His boy was at the shed's door with one hand on the gelding's collar.
    20	
    21	"Give her rope," said the ferryman. "All she'll take."
    22	
    23	She did not cross as Cael had known her to. In slack water she had gone over at a walking pace, set a little aslant on her two straps, so that the current itself shouldered her along the cable. This morning the current took her like a leaf. She slid down the cable's curve into its belly with the hauling rope running out behind her, faster than the gelding could back, and for a count of twenty she hung in the middle of the river with the brown water heaping against her northern side. Then she began to climb the far half of the curve, slowly, with a man leaning on a sweep; and she struck the western landing hard enough for the sound to come back across the water.
    24	
    25	"That is a third of the time," said Karis. She had Hesk's clock in her palm. "Three minutes. It has always been nine."
    26	
    27	"She went down a hill," said Lira. "Now she has to come up one, with people in her."
    28	
    29	On the far bank the carts had drawn up to the planks' edge in the half light, and Cael watched a long thin figure walk along them with one arm held out stiff, turning them back. A wheel went onto the landing and was made to come off it. Then the people went aboard, one at a time, and were placed. He counted them as they stepped down, since his far sight was as good as anybody's. There was a man with a ledger under his arm, and two with the walk of carters, and a woman who lifted a child in after her; there was a broad one in a drover's hat, and a lad. Last came an old man in a brown coat, who gave his stick to the lad to hold while he got his leg over the rail.
    30	
    31	"Eight," said Cael. "And himself."
    32	
    33	"No goods," said Brom. He had the purse inside his shirt, and he put his hand flat on the place. "He said so."
    34	
    35	Seln had not spoken since the notch. He was watching the man with the ledger.
    36	
    37	"There will be a bag," he said. "That one has it."
    38	
    39	Across the river the long figure lifted its arm.
    40	
    41	"Walk!" cried the boy, and the gelding leaned into his collar, and the drum began to groan.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	It was a raft of shelf ice that did it, as broad as the inn's door and a foot thick, and it came round the upper bend when the scow was a third of the way home.
    46	
    47	Half the bank saw it and shouted. On a river a person might shout as he pleased, and it made no difference whatever. The ferryman had his sweep out over the northern rail, and he set its iron against the thing as it came down on him, and Cael saw him bend like a bow. The raft did not turn. It rode up against the scow's side and stood on its edge there, with the whole river behind it; her deck tilted toward the south, and eight people went sliding down it into the rail.
    48	
    49	Something parted over her stern with a crack like a carter's whip.
    50	
    51	It was the strap. He saw the upper half of it fly up and hang from its block on the cable, twisting; and he saw the scow's stern, with nothing to hold it now, swing away down the river like a gate swinging on one hinge. She hung by her bow strap alone. The current had her whole length to push on, and all that kept her from going round that one hinge and tearing it out was the rope at her bow.
    52	
    53	The rope came up out of the water along its whole length, and went as straight as a rule from the scow to the head of the landing, and creaked.
    54	
    55	"Stand!" screamed the boy. "He's going back! Stand, you—"
    56	
    57	The gelding was being walked backward round his own ring. Seln was at the drum before Cael had turned; there was an iron dog hinged to the shed's king-post, which dropped into the teeth on the drum's rim, and he kicked it down. The drum stopped. The gelding stood trembling in his collar, and the whole weight of a boat and nine people came onto one rope and one post.
    58	
    59	Cael looked at the post.
    60	
    61	It stood at the landing's head, as thick as a man's body, with the block lashed to it at the height of his knee. The rope had been rigged to pull on it squarely. It was not pulling squarely now, since the scow hung far to the south of where a scow ought to be, and so the rope leaned on that block sideways, and at every surge of the river the post's head nodded a finger's breadth toward the water and the south.
    62	
    63	Three of the ten spoke to him together, as they had on this river once before.
    64	
    65	Pressure had begun to gather under his breastbone at the first sight of the raft. It was the narrow hard push that he had from the Bronze from Ardenmere, and at sixty yards it would have gone through a foot of ice as an awl goes through leather. He had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It went on, into whatever stood behind; and behind that ice were a deck, and a child.
    66	
    67	*There?* asked Anchor, of the post. It was a fair question. A point laid on that oak would have held it as a nail holds a board, for as long as he could hold the point. He had held one for sixty counts once, and a second time to thirty with his other hand shut on a drum to keep himself upright. While he held it he would have nothing else. And he would be kneeling at a post with his palm on it, working the registry's own craft, before forty people who meant to cross that river to the west as soon as it would carry them.
    68	
    69	He told both of them no. Wind wanted his feet, and he did not trouble to answer it.
    70	
    71	He gave the read the ground.
    72	
    73	He had expected the bank to tell him about the post, and it told him about itself first. Frozen earth had rung under him all the cold term. This did not ring. A whole thaw and a night of rain had gone down into it, and it came back to him as a spoon comes back out of porridge, soft and slow, with no edge anywhere. In that softness he could feel four feet of oak. Its packing stones still held on the north side. On the south side there was nothing that deserved the name, and at each surge the post's foot shifted in its hole, by a little, and did not shift back.
    74	
    75	"It's the foot," he said. "Not the head. The foot's walking, to the south, and there's nothing on that side to stop it."
    76	
    77	"Then we put something there," said Brom.
    78	
    79	He had gone for it already. The scow's blocking timbers lay stacked beside the slip where they had been thrown on the night of the two hundred and seventieth, squared oak, each as long as a man. He got one up onto his left shoulder with a heave of his back, and came along the bank with it; and no grey showed on him anywhere.
    80	
    81	"Where does its foot go? Say it quick."
    82	
    83	That was the question, and the read could not answer it from where Cael stood, since its whole world was a pace and a half across. He went south along the bank a stride at a time, feeling. There was mud, and more mud. A load set down there would have gone into it like a heel into a marsh.
    84	
    85	At the second stride the ground changed under his left boot. Something long and square lay a hand deep, bedded on stone, and it sent his weight away evenly into the bank beneath, as the blue ice had on a good day. It was the baulk that the scow had sat on from the first snow to the thaw.
    86	
    87	He had written a line in a shed about what Compression needed. He had not known that he was writing it about a ferry.
    88	
    89	"Here. It's the old sill. That's ground worth sending a load to." He stamped on the place. "Foot here, against its near edge. Head on the post, on the south face, under the block. Not a hand further out; it's soup."
    90	
    91	Brom let the timber down off his shoulder into the place, and walked its head up against the post, and leaned. The tall man was beside him. Cael had not seen him come down from the salt; he had the ferryman's maul in his big chapped hands and an oak wedge, and he set the wedge between the timber's head and the post and drove it with three blows, saying nothing.
    92	
    93	The river surged. Cael felt the load come down through the post, and turn, and go along the slanted oak into the sill and out into the bank. The post's foot stayed where it was.
    94	
    95	"It's holding," he said. "Wind her."
    96	
    97	---
    98	
    99	Lira had the brown mule out of her stall.
   100	
   101	She brought her round the drum at a trot, with a trace over her own shoulder, and Cael heard afterward how she had made it fast: a turn about the end of the beam, ahead of the gelding's collar, and two half-hitches, tied with both hands in the time that it took Seln to lift the dog. The left had been slow. She had sworn at it by name, said the boy, in front of a healer.
   102	
   103	"Walk!"
   104	
   105	Two beasts leaned. Teague's bow and the boy from the upper inn had their shoulders to the beam behind them, and three carters besides. The drum turned by a tooth, and held, and turned by another.
   106	
   107	Cael did not see it. He stood at the post with the read under him and his eyes on the river, since the read wanted nothing of his eyes; and so he saw what was done on the scow.
   108	
   109	She lay almost end-on to him now, sixty yards out, with her bow toward the bank and her stern trailing down the stream. The ice had slid off her and gone. On her deck the ferryman was at the sweep, with his feet against the rail, heaving her head round. He could do that or he could do anything else. He could not leave it. The torn strap hung from its block over the place where her stern had been, with its tail whipping in the wind a yard out of anybody's reach.
   110	
   111	Then the hauling rope drew her a little way up the cable's curve, and her stern swung in under the block again, and one of the carters jumped for the tail.
   112	
   113	He caught it. Cael saw him get both fists on it, and saw the river take the stern away once more with a man on the end of the strap. He was dragged down the deck on his knees to the rail-post, shouting, and he had the sense to let go with one hand and seize the post.
   114	
   115	A brown coat came down the tilted planks behind him.
   116	
   117	Its owner did not hurry. He had one leg that would not bend, and he came down that deck sideways, as a man goes down a stair with a bad knee. He stooped to the carter, and took the strap's tail out of the one fist that still held it. Cael watched him pass it round the rail-post in a single turn. He took the end behind his own back, and brought it forward under his other arm, and shut both hands on it against his chest.
   118	
   119	Then he set the soles of his boots against the post's foot, with the stiff leg straight and the other bent, and sat back upon the strap.
   120	
   121	He did not haul. It was that which held Cael's eye through the whole of what followed, with the read going dim at the edges and his own two hands beginning to blur. A younger man would have fought that rope. He would have pulled, and gained a foot, and lost it at the next surge, and worn out in a minute. The man in the brown coat pulled at nothing. He had made himself into the thing that the strap was fastened to. When the river took the stern, the turn on the post took most of the river; what was left came to his back and his heels, and he gave it nowhere to go.
   122	
   123	The stern stayed under its block.
   124	
   125	"Walk! Walk on!"
   126	
   127	She came up the curve of the cable a yard at a time. It was nothing like nine minutes; Karis told him afterward that it was twenty-two by the clock, and that she had not known that she was counting aloud. A tree went by between the scow and the bank. The woman had her child's face pressed into her coat. The ferryman worked his sweep without once looking behind him, since a man who stands so on a strap does not need to be looked at. The two carters and the drover stood about the old man with their hands open, wanting to help him and finding nothing of him to take hold of.
   128	
   129	No grey came on those hands. Nothing stood up out of the deck, or the air; nobody in that boat declared anything. Cael would have known, since he had spent every year since his Kindling learning what a declaration looked like from the wrong side of it. There was a man, and a strap, and a post, and that was the whole of it.
   130	
   131	The scow's bow struck the landing.
   132	
   133	The boy had a line on her before she had finished shuddering, and the ferryman had another, and the drum's dog dropped for the last time. Seven people came over the rail onto the planks as if the planks had been bread.
   134	
   135	The man in the brown coat had not moved.
   136	
   137	"You can let go," said the ferryman.
   138	
   139	He was standing over him with the stern line in his fist. The old man looked up, and then down at the strap across his chest.
   140	
   141	"She's fast, both ends. Let go of it."
   142	
   143	The right hand opened. The left did not. Cael was too far up the landing to see fingers, with his eyes as they were by then; what he saw was the man sit a moment longer, and bring his right hand over, and work at the other, as a person unpacks a parcel that somebody else has tied.
   144	
   145	---
   146	
