173:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Her case stands as she made it and is "not by a line" altered. She has, received and receipted, **the flat (six rows, the sheets not counted, one row turned over once), the completion report, the leaf and the technician's sheet**, and her own exact copy of the sheet with a ruled box and *not read* in it. She has said that the word on the leaf is a title and not a name, that she has never met it in any stratum, and nothing more. She has said "More than four" and will not say how many more. She cannot read the index; it is now an office's index and not only the dead man's. She has not connected anything to anything. She keeps the lamp.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:41:"More than four," she said. She did not say how many more. She put the sheet down at the end of the sixth row and did not start a seventh, and looked at the rows, and Cael saw her decide not to say the number, because a number said once was a number said, and she had not checked it. "The Ring's here. The Stair's here — that's the hand from the kit; I'd know it in the dark. The Hall's here, surveyed from the marsh side; they never went in. The fourth." Her finger touched a row and came off it. "And the rest are theirs. Places we didn't find. Some of them are on the bearing and some of them aren't and I'm not going to say which tonight, because I'll say it wrong, and a wrong site is worse than no site."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:69:"*—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—*"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:99:"*Deep layer. Sequence initiated* — and a date." She gave the date. It was a Compact date, a registry year and a day in it, and the room did the arithmetic without being asked to, because the room had lived by the Line's count for nine months and by the Compact's for their whole lives before that, and a date was a date. Six years back. Near enough six — some months before he had begun staying out of files, which he noted, and did not follow. "*Unreadable. Not halted. Running. Correlated with classification event* —" and then the bar.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:7:Cael knew the stance before he knew the face. He had stood in front of that stance in a hall at sixteen, and at a table at seventeen, and in a borrowed room at eighteen with nothing anywhere deciding what he was, and a body learns a stance the way it learns a stair. Feet apart. Weight down. The hands still. A man built to hold a line, standing at the bow of a rope ferry in a coat that had never been grey, holding a stick.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:9:"Vastin," Seln said, beside him, in the voice for an inventory, and it was not an inventory.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:33:"Archmarshal."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:35:"No." He said it without weight, as a correction to a ledger. "Not that. Not for some months. Vastin will do; it did for my father." He looked at Seln, and Seln looked back, and neither of them said what they were to each other, because it had never once been said aloud and was not going to start on a landing. "I'd like to speak to the five of you. Six," he said, and Cael saw him count, and saw him arrive at the number, and saw him not ask about the sixth, which was a thing the man he had a catalogue for would have asked about at once. "When it's convenient. I've come a long way to say a short thing, and I'd rather say it once, to everyone, than twice to anyone."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:83:"You came through the board," Seln said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:101:Brom spoke before she could. Brom priced things, and he had been pricing this one since the landing, and he said it to the table and not to the man, because that was how a price was said. "He walked here. Alone. In spring mud, on that knee, with a pack he mended himself." He looked at the boots once more. "Three hundred miles is a demonstration. Out here that's the only kind there is." And that was the whole of Brom's price, and it was, from Brom, an argument for hearing a man out and not one hair past it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:107:"Under every station on the continent," Karis said. "I said so at a table in the autumn." Her voice did not change. "That's not news, Vastin. That's a thing I've carried since Ostrand, and said aloud at Thornwater with the ring flat on the table. Say the part that's new."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:109:Vastin looked at her for the space of a step, and Cael watched a man revise an estimate. "No living official can reach it," he said. "That is the part that is new. Not *will not*. *Cannot.* There is no key. There has been no key in living memory, and the Compact has stopped noticing that there is no key, because a thing you cannot open becomes, after a century or two, a wall, and nobody files a complaint about a wall."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:133:"Then I still haven't asked."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:137:"What follows you," Lira said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:139:"Nothing that can cross the Line." He said it as fact, in the ledger voice, the one thing at the table he was still certain of. "The restriction on him is pending jurisdiction. There is no jurisdiction here. There is no one behind me and no one after me, and if there were, they would not be after me — I am a man who resigned, and men who resign are filed, not followed. What follows this boy is a piece of paper that stops at a river." A pause. "What followed you to the Stair in the autumn is another matter, and it is not mine, and I do not know whose it is, and I would give a great deal to."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:145:He did not say any of that. He said, to Karis: "Ask him the thing you want to ask him."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:151:"Because a council would file it," Vastin said, "and I spent forty years being the man they sent to make sure the filing held. I know exactly how deep a thing has to be buried before no one goes looking. This is deeper than that." He was quiet a moment. His hands did not move on the table. "I helped bury smaller things. I'd like to dig one up before I'm done."

