editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:25:"He verifies it," she said. "He's an evaluator; it's the whole of what he is. He doesn't believe a thing because it's on a table; he measures it. So he goes home and pulls a frame reading, or a station sheet, or a fresh reading on one of the forty, to see whether the case is true." She turned her pale fingers over on the cloth. "And the frame's theirs, and the station's theirs, and the reading runs through the function. Every instrument he could check it with comes back and says the case is wrong. And he believes his instruments, because he's believed them for forty years. And somewhere it gets minuted that a forgery was offered to the Archmarshal by persons connected with the respondent at Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:31:"He believes it," she said, "all of it, at once, the way you'd want him to, and it breaks him." She was looking at the case. "He's been measuring true for forty years because he thought true was the point. We show him the point was never true. And he goes home, and sits at his desk, and does nothing, because there's nothing he can do. He signs what he's given, or he doesn't, and either way he's a broken man signing things." She lifted her chin. "And we'd have done that to him. We'd have spent the one good man in the whole building for a bad night's sleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:191:"I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:"The third is certification." For the first time he looked at Karis, only at Karis, and Cael understood that the not looking at himself was being chosen, and chosen again, every moment. "Before this year is out the seat will send to my desk for my certificate on the protocol for a re-evaluation at Halcenvane. I have known it was coming since the convening. I meant to sign it, because the protocol is sound, and in forty years I have never refused my name to a sound protocol." He lifted his hand off the table. "When it comes, I shall decline. It is the only sentence I have left that they still need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:273:He would not write the man down. He would not write any of them down: not the girl with the turned ankle who had asked him a straight question, not the big one with the bad shoulder, not the researcher who had held out her gloves, not the boy he had not let himself look at. A description is a file with the name left off, and he had spent forty years learning how easily the name comes back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:He had forty years' practice in saying what was so, and he had learned in this one year that a true sentence, once it left his desk, belonged to whoever picked it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:231:*Recovery, Fallow road, broker's posting. Taken. Witnessed. Completed: incomplete — instruments not recovered — crew returned. Six went in. Six came out. No price paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
