626:- **Bodies:** Lira one-armed, shooting; Brom's string restriction ended on the 100th and the crossbow is on his belt; Cael whole. **New canon KEEP:** the founding stone from Ostrand's old gate; the hold's yard crossbow; the husband and the hold's boy (unnamed). **Names:** none minted. **Vastin:** one window, three sentences, no age.
682:**Vastin.** One window: a courtesy copy, read twice, not understood, filed as such, returned unannotated.
31:She did not have the forty out. She did not need them. She had them by heart, every station and every margin, and she gave them in the order she had sorted them into, which was not the order Seln had laid them on the table.
105:"And the leaf is the same age as the first memorandum, on the same paper. The hand is the hand of the first one's top signature; I'd swear to that in front of Ilsev. Somebody sat down two hundred years ago, wrote out what the function was and what it was for, signed the first memorandum to every station, and put the note that explained it in the bottom of the box. That's the middle layer."
117:"Under the policy there's something older: the reading, whatever it is that wakes behind your breastbone at Kindling and reads you. The function sits on it. It's older than the function by however long, and the case doesn't say how long, and I don't know." She laid her hand on the cloth. "But the case does show me one thing about what was there before the function came, because the first memoranda had to clear it out of the way. The first three issues, about eleven years apart, in the oldest paper. And they're not about the forty. They're about the old custom."

