editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:"Dated the first day of the year," said Bracken. "The morning of convocation. Somebody a week's road from here pressed that wax while the Chancellor stood on her platform telling this house to be what its papers say it is. I don't suppose they knew. I don't suppose it matters." He touched the second mark. "Served this morning. Seven days on a road the post does in three, at a walk, with three horses and a man in the middle who'd been told the pace. It wasn't sent to arrive. It was sent to be watched arriving. I watched it for a full minute from my window, and so did the gate, and so did the coats at the foot of the hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:"Don't thank me. It's a docket." Bracken picked up nothing, because there was nothing to pick up, and turned to go. Then he turned back, as if he had forgotten something. "The last Fifth-day of the year," he said to the table at large. "Somebody write it down. I'd hate for the house to be told I let it slip."
