editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:And on the way down the stair he remembered the counter, and the four sentences Seln had spent on him in a queue on the third day of the year. *Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check. Keep the documentation current.* The renewals table was the provision's standing papers, set out in a grid. Whatever went down the hill this week would sit in the registry's file beside the registry's own dates when the half-year check came, and a table that disagreed with them by nine days would have been the first thing the check found. Karis had not heard Seln say it. She had kept the documentation current anyway, because that was what she did with documents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:The pen lay where Lira had left it. It was a plain steel pen in a dark holder, with the house's mark burned into the wood, and it had lain at that end of the counter for longer than Cael had troubled to notice. Bracken had said so on a landing two nights ago. *There is a pen on my counter. There has been one there since the fourth day of the year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:On the sill lay the enrollee's copy of the instrument. It had lain there, first leaf up, since the eighth day of the year: nineteen leaves on one side, thirty-seven paragraphs, three schedules, whose words he had counted on the first night as a man counts the guards on a wall. He left it where it lay, since it had been addressed to the house as much as to him, and the house could have it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:"Dated the first day of the year," said Bracken. "The morning of convocation. Somebody a week's road from here pressed that wax while the Chancellor stood on her platform telling this house to be what its papers say it is. I don't suppose they knew. I don't suppose it matters." He touched the second mark. "Served this morning. Seven days on a road the post does in three, at a walk, with three horses and a man in the middle who'd been told the pace. It wasn't sent to arrive. It was sent to be watched arriving. I watched it for a full minute from my window, and so did the gate, and so did the coats at the foot of the hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:The eighth day of the year was a First-day, hot and still, and at the second bell Cael was on the covered walk with a book under his arm and nowhere in particular to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:"It's the eleventh of Sowing," said Lira. "Hesk told me. Two years ago. I wrote to him from Greyvane to ask, and he wrote back in three lines, and one of them was the date and one of them was *thank you* and the last one said *he won't have told you*." She took her chin off her fists. "You weren't going to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:He had been sitting there a quarter of an hour, after the grey half, with two loaves from underneath and a twist of the fruit woman's apricots and nothing at all in his head, which was a thing he had been learning to do. The market was loud and slow around him. It was the first warm Saturday of the year, and people had come down to it who did not need anything, only to walk about in the sun and look at things and be seen looking. He had been watching them without meaning to. He had been watching the way a man in a brown coat came down the second row between the stalls, unhurried, reading the bread woman's high cracked lantern as if it were a notice, and he had thought, idly, *that man reads things the way they're written*. Then the man had turned out of the row toward the pie stall, and Cael had seen the set of his shoulders, and the brown coat had stopped mattering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:She had borrowed Brom's docket out of his left inside pocket to copy its date, and Brom had stood in the annex doorway until she gave it back, which she did inside a quarter of an hour. She had the two dates on a slip, one above the other. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The seat's ruling, before the year's close, and there was only one Fifth-day in the year's last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:The sittings went up on the covered walk's middle board that afternoon in Bracken's hand, under the house's one-line answer from the eighth day: *Sittings at the registry seat, Ostrand, on the seat's Fifth-days. First sitting: the sixth Fifth-day of the year.* Underneath it, smaller, Bracken had added a line of his own, which was a thing he never did on a public board. *The house will attend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:15:Then the rest, which was ordinary, as Karis had said. *The seat at Ostrand gives notice that it has convened formal proceedings upon the petition served on the eighth day of the year. The seat will sit on its Fifth-days, upon the schedule of the academic year, and will rule before the year's close. Counsel of record will confer with the seat's clerk upon the schedule on the twenty-fifth day. The first sitting will be held on the sixth Fifth-day of the year. Senior evaluators of the district will be called to the evaluation seat on a day to be notified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:She unrolled the paper on the canvas and laid the brass along its top edge to hold it. It was a straightedge, an old one, which she had had from Ternhall. Cael knew the sheet before the firelight had found the whole of it, for it had hung on her reading-room wall on the first day of the year, and she had taken it down in the first week to make room for a code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:"Don't thank me. It's a docket." Bracken picked up nothing, because there was nothing to pick up, and turned to go. Then he turned back, as if he had forgotten something. "The last Fifth-day of the year," he said to the table at large. "Somebody write it down. I'd hate for the house to be told I let it slip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:125:"I do not ask what the room is being used for," he said. "I ask when a thing is wanted, and never why, and I have found that it saves me a great deal. I have come about the district's evening bag, which reached my counter a quarter of an hour ago with one item in it. It is addressed to the practitioner, care of the registrar, and so I have read it." He held out the packet. "I walked his docket up to a refectory table on the fourth day of the year. I thought I had better finish as I began."
