editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:It was what Gault had promised at the end of last term and every time since: the enrollee measured against himself, and nothing else. *Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* The basis of the enrollment was read into the minute and found unchanged. The term's supervised floor hours were certified from Rooke's sheets, which Gault had brought in the case, signed. The two meet records were laid on the table as evidence of performance under the provision, and the registry officer read the exhibition leaves with great attention and asked four questions about the documented ceilings, all of them competent, and Cael answered each one in the language of the documents themselves. The burst's distance; the burst's rate on sprung oak and on other floors; the landing beat; the read that looked like timing. All of it was in the record, and all of it was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:135:The pawl let go. The weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the right shoulder, all of it, with nothing taken and nothing turned. The dial in the housing swung a little way, and stopped where it had stopped at the baseline, and where it had stopped on the twentieth of Reaping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:219:Cael was thinking of a copper pot on the twentieth of Reaping, and four instants of fire, and a pane cracking as it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:"Your next evaluation. The provision's second." Gault tipped his head at the calendar behind him, where a line in his own hand marked the half-month and read *Panel of record: Gault, with two. Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* "It falls inside the qualifying season. Under the old reading of the provision you'd have had to come home for it, wherever the season had taken you, and sit it here, in this wing, on this frame. The chancellor didn't care for that. She wrote to the registry's district office. The district office, to the astonishment of everybody who has ever written to a district office, agreed by return without a hearing." He unlaced his fingers to count on them. "The enrollment basis goes where the enrollee goes. The evaluation goes where the basis goes. So the evaluation goes where you go. If the delegation is in a mill town on the day, then the panel sits in the mill town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:At the foot of the road the two coats stood in the lee of the wall, as they had stood every day of the year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:The letter went behind the front board of the observation notebook with the other two that lived there: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen, on the eleventh of Sowing, and the sheet that had said *Sixteen suits you.* Three now, in the same hand. He sat with the notebook shut on all three before he put it away, and did not write in the Log what it cost him to have them there together, because some lines in a ledger were better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:*Semester evaluation, the twentieth of Reaping. Requirement satisfied. Standing renewed. Panel of record: Gault (presiding), with two. Inspecting delegation present, under the charter.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:There were eleven names on the manifest and he had pages on nine of them. The library had given him three of the four escort riders out of a dozen years of attendance lists, the counsel out of her published findings, and the three clerks out of a registry yearbook that nobody had borrowed in a decade. Havel had a page with a question of Lira's at the foot of it. Ilsev had the thickest page and the least use, because six findings told him what she would do with a document and nothing at all about what she did with a morning. The fourth rider was too new to be anywhere. And the eleventh name had one line on its page, and under the line, in his own hand, a single word: *Lira*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:265:*Ledger, day twenty-two. The twentieth of Reaping.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:277:*Answer, on the twentieth of Reaping: yes.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:17:"*Delegation of the Guilds Compact. Arrival: the twelfth. Sittings on the records: from the twelfth through the fifteenth. Review of facilities: the fourteenth. Faculty interviews: as the delegation directs. Observation of provision-status evaluation: reserved. The twentieth of Reaping.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:17:He went up to his room and put the sheet inside the notebook's front board, behind the cover, where the other one already lived: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen on the eleventh of Sowing. Two sheets now, in the same hand. What it cost him to put the second beside the first, he kept out of the Log. Some lines in a ledger are better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:63:"Next term's sittings." Gault held the calendar out for him. Every half-month had its line, and on the line for the provision's half-yearly evaluation a date had been entered in Gault's hand, and beneath it: *Panel of record: Gault, with two. Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* "Same rules as before. Same instruments, checked on Fourth-days. The frame will have a new second notch, and the man who builds them is coming upriver in person to fit it, which I'm told he finds humiliating." The calendar went back to the desk. "I'll measure you against yourself again. That's all the provision asks, and it's all I'll do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:193:"Final, the nineteenth of Reaping, before a witnessed panel. Won." He looked up from the sheet. "Eleven bouts unbeaten. None lost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:49:"It's the eleventh of Sowing. Sit down," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:221:Last of all he went back up to the head of the entry. In all the Log's pages he had never once written an entry's date out in full. He did it now, with Hesk's pen, slowly, so that every stroke would be the pen's best: *The eleventh of Sowing.* And under the date he wrote the last lines of the half-year.

