editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:"No goods," said Brom. He had the purse inside his shirt, and he put his hand flat on the place. "He said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:161:"I know what's on my bank." But he said nothing about a figure, and he went back down the planks to the last of his passengers, who was getting over the rail with a stick in his hand. "A copper a head. You stood in her, same as the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:241:"A copper and a half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:243:"It's a copper," said Brom, under his breath. "It's been a copper since we came."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:247:Vastin had a small purse on a cord. He counted out one coin, and then a second, which had been cut across with a chisel; he looked at the cut edge for a breath, as though it were a kind of seal that he had not met, and laid the half beside the whole on her palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:121:"Perhaps the meaning of forty-one marks. I shall not promise you more than perhaps. And it wants a door that I cannot open myself, by the leave of a man whom none of us has ever seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:137:Cael read the slips from the left-hand post. The thaw had opened the holds, and the holds wanted hands. There was a mill-race to be cleared of ice, and a byre roof, and on the south road a bridge of two logs that the flood had carried a furlong. *Carts over, the first boat; hands on the landing; a copper the cart.* Under that one there were strokes already, in three different thumbs. Not one slip of the eight asked what a person was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:167:The hide-woman went away muttering, and Cael understood, watching her go, how that healer got through a bench of nine. She had a purse in her that she called her reserve, and a mending drew on it at about the rate of the hurt, a toe for a toe; and so she gave it only where nothing cheaper would serve, and gave words and mutton fat for the rest. Between the child and the carter she sat down on her keg with her head lowered, and her lips moved to fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:"I had a tray for such papers," said Vastin. "I kept one on my desk for forty years, marked *held*, and I had supposed it a habit of my own. A paper in it is neither granted nor denied. It waits. I learned in this cold term that somebody else keeps such a tray, and that it is a great deal deeper than mine was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:61:Most of it was a schedule. Cael could read it, since it lay the other way up to him: a column of housings, each with its number, and against each a line of small faded writing that gave the date of a setting and a mender's mark. It was a clerk's list, as dull as a tally of sacks, and it ran from the head of the leaf to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:131:She had her notebook open at a clean leaf already, and she copied the sheet as she had copied forty-one kinds of mark off three floors, a line for a line, with every stop where the technician had set it. At the date she wrote the office's own figures down exactly as they stood, and did not turn them into anything. When she came to the bar she ruled a box of the same length, and looked at it, and wrote two words within.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:289:"Not by a line. I had a bearing, and an index that I cannot read, and forty-one marks that I cannot read. I have now, beside those, an office's count that I cannot read, and a title that I have never met, and a box that I have ruled round nothing." Her pencil went down another word. "I have more that I cannot read tonight than I had this morning. It is the most that anybody has ever given me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been set down as nothing yet. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:He sat. It took him some time, with the leg, and he did it without putting a hand on anybody's shoulder. The keeper had come to his cask-room door to look at a stranger at the standing crew's board; Vastin lifted a finger to him, and asked what a bowl was, and put a copper down on the wood before Brom had lifted the ladle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:Brom filled the bowl, and set it before him, and looked at the copper. It was a thing that he could weigh, and he seemed to find the weight correct.
