editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:269:The small purse came out on its cord, and a fourth coin went down on the wood. Vastin looked at it for a moment after his fingers had left it, as he had looked at the cut half of the other. It came to Cael that the man had very likely never paid for a bed in his life. An office had sent a clerk before him to every inn on every road that he had travelled, and the reckoning had gone home in a bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:145:He put no place on it. He folded it, and laid a copper on top, and left the two together inside Hesk's book for the boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:Seln had forty-six notches. Brom had asked Karis for the purse, and had it in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:163:Brom looked at the purse in his hand, and across a hundred yards of brown water at a stack under tarred sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He was not an inspecting officer. He had left the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it. His clerk had come for that tray at the usual hour. Whether what he had done was a resignation, or only a man going down a stair, was a question for an office to settle, and he had not waited to hear it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:197:He was the only man within a hundred miles who had ever been such an officer. He stood at the sill in a drover's coat of brown frieze, which was too short in the sleeve and smelled of sheep; he had bought it at the last market inside the Line, and had left a grey one on its hook. He had an ash stick, cut out of a hedge. He had a pack with one strap mended in cord. Under the pack, flat against his back, lay a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, which he had carried so for three hundred miles less a few. Inside his coat, in a fold of oilcloth, there was a single leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:235:A boy came along the carts a little afterward, with a flat cake in his hand, and laid it on the stump at Vastin's elbow, and went away again without a word. It was warm. Vastin had been waited upon at tables for the whole of his service, by people whose names he had taken care to learn, and he had thanked every one of them. He looked along the carts for somebody to thank, and nobody was looking back. So he ate the cake, and put a copper on the stump where it had lain. The copper was there in the morning, with the frost on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:243:There was a man on that landing who was hiring. He was brisk and well-kept, with ink on his cuffs and a clerk at his elbow, and he went along the waiting carters and the men without carts and looked at each as he would have looked at a bale, for the mark. He came to Vastin last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:255:"No," he said. "You'd be off the rail at the first cake of ice. I can't use you. I'd not give a copper for you tonight, and I mean nothing by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:"There are three reasons," he said. "I had a long road to put them in order, and I shall give you them so. The first is a thing that you did not do. Five people laid a case in front of me that would have shaken any officer in the Compact, and when I had read it, not one of them asked me for anything. I remarked on it at the time. I have been asked for something by every person who ever set a paper before me; it is what the paper is for. You asked nothing. You let me go down a stair with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"I should not care to have it thought that I walked here to lay a knee before a healer. It was sound when I set out, and it has carried me, and I have no claim on anybody's purse for it. I offer it to nobody. I had rather that were understood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:81:Cael had told a board that he would rule when the first boat was in, since a desk might have changed the price. The boat was in. He had the price now from the office that used to certify such things, and it was last year's price, to the copper. Nothing about it had altered but this: a second person had come to the same town's name, by another road, and had not known that it was already on their table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:133:Cael took Ephram's letter down himself. His eyes had come home by then, as far as print; he could read the single line under the fold, and the *C.* at its foot, and the date that he had put on nothing. He gave it to the ferryman's boy with the copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:235:"He copied them from this," she said. "Or from the list that this was copied from. I told you that a clerk had looked it up." She did not sound pleased. She sounded like a person who has been right about where a wall is, in the dark, by walking into it. "It is the same index. I can read it no better than I could. But it is not a dead man's private mark any longer. It is an office's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:241:She looked at the figure for a long breath. Then she did a thing that was very like her. She went back through the whole row from its first sheet, quickly, looking at nothing but the heads; and each time that she met a group of four signs which she had not met before, she laid one finger of her left glove down upon the deal. She could not read a group. She could tell one from another, as a person with no letters can still tell his own mark from his neighbour's.

