   133	Vastin looked at her. Then he looked at Oryn, across from him, who had not moved and had not stopped looking at his knee, and Oryn said, in the voice for a finding, "She's right. Go and lie down, and don't take the stairs fast," and he stood up, on the knee, and took the stick, and went, and the door shut behind him, and the room let out a breath that nobody had known it was holding.
   134	
   135	Lira looked at Cael's hand.
   136	
   137	It was still on the table. It was still a hand's width from the page. He had not moved it in all the last thing and all the man's leaving, and Lira had watched it not move as she had watched it not move at a table at seventeen with a man in grey across from it, and she looked at it now, and then at him, and did not say anything, and did not need to.
   138	
   139	"I know," he said.
   140	
     1	# Chapter 2 — No Sanction
     2	
     3	The ferry came in on the two hundred and seventy-seventh day past the Line, at first light, with the river running high and brown under it and the Line's lead on the sack at its bow, and he was on the landing to see it because he catalogued arrivals, and Seln was beside him because Seln read faces off boats.
     4	
     5	He catalogued the ferry first, because it was the first of the year and he wanted the count. The same flat hull, tarred over the winter and smelling of it. The same rope, new-spliced where the ice had frayed it, singing in the ferryman's hands. The boy at the stern pole. Hides, six bales, a broker's mark on four of them. The tarred sack, the lead on it, the season's first mail, which would have in it whatever the Line had decided to say to the edge over a winter of not being able to. And one passenger, at the bow, standing, with a pack at his feet and a stick in his hand, who was not a carter and not a broker's man and was looking at the landing the way a man looks at a place he has read about in a file.
     6	
     7	Cael knew the stance before he knew the face. He had stood in front of that stance in a hall at sixteen, and at a table at seventeen, and in a borrowed room at eighteen with nothing anywhere deciding what he was, and a body learns a stance the way it learns a stair. Feet apart. Weight down. The hands still. A man built to hold a line, standing at the bow of a rope ferry in a coat that had never been grey, holding a stick.
     8	
     9	"Vastin," Seln said, beside him, in the voice for an inventory, and it was not an inventory.
    10	
    11	"Yes."
    12	
    13	"Alone," Seln said.
    14	
    15	"Yes," Cael said.
    16	
    17	Seln did not say anything else. He watched the ferry come in as he watched everything come in, and Cael watched Seln, because Seln had spent fifteen years being the kind of thing that man deployed and had never once, in Cael's hearing, said his name.
    18	
    19	---
    20	
    21	He catalogued the man as the ferry grounded, because a catalogue was what he had, and because the man he was looking at was not the man he had a catalogue for.
    22	
    23	Fifty-three, or near it, and the winter and the road had put more on him than the months since a room in Ostrand had. The hair gone from iron to something with less iron in it. A traveling coat, brown, road-made, the kind a carter's factor wears, with the elbows already through and the hem cut short for mud. Boots that had been good boots a long way west of here and had done three hundred miles since. A pack, one strap mended with cord. A stick, ash, cut for walking and not for show, with the top worn to the shape of a hand. And the walk, when he stepped off onto the landing with the pack on one shoulder — the left leg taking the weight and the right coming after it a half-count late, a knee that had been asked for more miles than it had agreed to and had kept the account.
    24	
    25	No grey. No seal. No clerk behind him with a book. No two men in road-colour a pace back and a pace to either side. He came up the landing alone with the ferryman's boy in front of him carrying the sack, and the boy did not look round at him, because to the boy he was a man who had paid his passage, and that was all a man was on a ferry.
    26	
    27	The sack went up the landing ahead of him. Cael catalogued it going, because a sack was the only thing the Line sent that could not be argued with: the lead, the ferryman's knot under it, the weight of it on the boy's shoulder — a winter's worth of what the west had to say, and none of it, he already knew, for the crew. Hesk wrote to the courier chain in the spring when the chain was running and not before, and Vell wrote when there was a thing to write, and the Compact did not write to a man it had no column for. Whatever was in the sack was for brokers and holds and the two inns. He noted that he had checked, and that checking was a habit he had thought a winter would cure.
    28	
    29	He saw Cael at the top of the landing and stopped.
    30	
    31	It was a short stop. A half-stride. The stance did not change and the face did not change, and a man who had not stood in front of that face for four years of his life would not have seen it, and Cael had, and did. Then Vastin came on up the landing, and stopped again, at the distance a man stops at who has come a long way to speak and has not yet earned it, and said, "Cael."
    32	
    33	"Archmarshal."
    34	
    35	"No." He said it without weight, as a correction to a ledger. "Not that. Not for some months. Vastin will do; it did for my father." He looked at Seln, and Seln looked back, and neither of them said what they were to each other, because it had never once been said aloud and was not going to start on a landing. "I'd like to speak to the five of you. Six," he said, and Cael saw him count, and saw him arrive at the number, and saw him not ask about the sixth, which was a thing the man he had a catalogue for would have asked about at once. "When it's convenient. I've come a long way to say a short thing, and I'd rather say it once, to everyone, than twice to anyone."
    36	
    37	"There's a plank," Cael said. "Everybody who lands here goes to the plank first."
    38	
    39	"So I've been told. Three times, between the Line and here, by three different people who didn't know each other." He shifted the pack on his shoulder. "I'll go to the plank."
