+5. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"Yes. I had certified that reading in every district of the Compact for forty years, and had written two sections of the protocol that governs it, and I had never once asked what the function was appended *to*." He turned his left hand over on the board, and it did not open the whole way. "I asked; and I did not ask by paper, having learned what became of my paper. I went down a stair to the house's frame shop, where an old man in an apron mends the stations' frames, and I asked him what lay under the function."`
+   new: `"Yes. I had certified that reading in every district of the Compact for forty years, and had written two sections of the protocol that governs it, and I had never once asked what the function was appended *to*." He turned his left hand over on the board, and it did not open the whole way. "I asked what lay under the function."`
+
+6. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"He told me what every mender is told in his first year, and it took him as long as it takes to wipe his hands. There is a thing beneath the plate which is not the office's work. A mender services the housing, and the frame, and the function that sits upon the reading, and he does not go below. I believed him the more readily for a thing that I had watched him do in that shop some while before. A river station had sent in a frame that read a fraction slow on wet mornings. There was nothing the matter with it; it was reporting the damp, and its fraction never varied, but the station did not care to be told about the damp. So he put a slip of brass into the housing, and it left off telling them, and he wrote in his note that they now had a worse frame. He knew that nobody would read the note." Vastin's eyes had gone to the window. "A man who will write that, knowing it will lie unread, does not trouble to deceive me about what is under a plate. I asked whether it was so in our house's frames only, or—"`
+   new: `"He told me that the office cannot reach it," said Vastin. "I asked whether it was so in our house's frames only, or—"`
+
+7. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"I had it from that same leaf, by inference, and from the three oldest papers in the case. I said that the reading was older than the function, and that I could not tell by how much, and that nothing in the box reached down so far. I entered it as a limit." She looked at him steadily. "I did not expect to have it confirmed by a man in an apron."`
+   new: `Karis held his eyes and said no more.`
+
+8. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"He would be surprised to hear that he had confirmed anything. To him it is a rule of the shop, like not leaving oil by a flame."`
+   new: `Vastin inclined his head.`
+
+9. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"I supposed what you suppose," Vastin went on. "I supposed that somebody above my desk kept the thing shut, and I went to look for the door that it was kept behind, meaning to learn who held the other half of the lock. There is an office that has the charge of the stations, and it keeps a schedule for the housing, and one for the plate, and one for the frame; it keeps a schedule for the function, which it maintains very well indeed. Below the function it has a single page. The page says that the layer is not to be opened, and it gives as its reason that it cannot be."`
+   new: `"I supposed what you suppose," Vastin went on. "I went looking for the door, meaning to learn who held the other half of the lock. The office's own page says that the layer is not to be opened, and gives as its reason that it cannot be."`
+
+10. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"I thought so, and I read further, to be certain. It has been attempted, more than once, by the people whose business it is, with the best that they have. Nothing that they own will open it, and nothing that they own will read it. It is not locked against them. A lock is a thing that somebody has the fellow to." His hand closed on the board, slowly. "They sit upon it, and they have built the whole of their record on the top of it; and they could no more go into it than I could go into that hearthstone."`
+   new: `"I read further, to be certain," Vastin said. "Nothing in the office can open it or read it."`
+
+11. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"That agrees with what I had," said Karis slowly. She wrote four words, and looked at them. "It goes no further than what I had, in one way; I still cannot say what the thing is. But I had only an inference. You have it from the people who hold the tools."`
+   new: `Karis wrote the correction exactly as he had given it and said nothing more.`
+
+12. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
+   old: `"I know that you would, and you are right to. But I have carried it three hundred miles without saying it aloud, and I would have you meet it as it stands, in its own ink, before you meet it in my mouth. I am not a witness to what it means. I should only be putting my voice between you and the page."`
+   new: `"I know that you would, and you are right to. But I have carried it three hundred miles without saying it aloud, and I would have you meet it as it stands on the page before you meet it in my mouth. I am not a witness to what it means. I should only be putting my voice between you and the page."`
